/* =====================================================================
   PRACTICE QUESTIONS — ARE Study System
   One question per line:
     {id:'q57', d:'PPD', o:'PPD 2.2', t:'mc', s:'Question stem',
      opts:['Option A','Option B','Option C','Option D'], c:[1], e:'Explanation'},
   id    unique and never reused; answers and the mistake log are saved
         against it. New questions: take the next number. Never change one.
   d     division: 'PA', 'PPD' or 'PDD'
   o     objective, e.g. 'PA 4.1' (drives "Reread" links and weak spots)
   t     'mc' = multiple choice (one answer)
         'cata' = check all that apply (scores only if every pick is right)
   opts  the options, in display order
   c     correct option(s), counted from 0: [2] = the third option.
         'mc' has exactly one; 'cata' can have several, e.g. [0,2,3]
   e     explanation shown after answering — say why the traps are wrong
   Two more types:
     t:'num'   fill in a number: ans:400, tol:0.5 (accepted ± difference), unit:'ft²'
               (no opts or c). "$" and commas typed by the student are ignored.
     t:'match' drag and drop / matching: items:['statement 1','statement 2',…],
               opts:['choice A','choice B',…] and c:[index of the right choice for
               each statement], e.g. c:[0,2,1]. Statements are shown in a shuffled order.
   Write an apostrophe inside '...' as \'  Every line ends with a comma.
   To import a whole quiz text file: node tools/import-quizzes.js <file.txt>
   (it replaces the PPD/PDD questions and keeps PA; see the top of that file).
   ===================================================================== */
window.ARE = window.ARE || {};
ARE.questions = [
{id:"q0",d:"PA",o:"PA 4.1",t:"mc",s:"A programme totals 34,000 SF of net assignable area for a university library. Using the published overall building efficiency for a library, what gross area should the programmer carry?",opts:["22,100 GSF","34,000 GSF","52,308 GSF","68,000 GSF"],c:[2],e:"Library overall efficiency is .65. Gross = net ÷ efficiency = 34,000 ÷ 0.65 = 52,308 GSF. Option A multiplies instead of divides (34,000 × 0.65), which is the trap — net to gross always gets larger. Option D applies a factor of 2.0, which is the office/corporate number, not the library one."},
{id:"q1",d:"PA",o:"PA 4.3",t:"mc",s:"During programming, a client states: \"Decentralise the 2,700-student enrolment into schools of 900 students, with four houses within each school.\" This statement is best characterised as:",opts:["A design concept","A programmatic concept","A project goal","A problem statement"],c:[1],e:"It is a programmatic concept — an abstract idea solving a performance problem, with no physical response specified. Three different design concepts could satisfy it: three dispersed buildings, three floors of one building, or one compact building with three identifiable schools on a floor. A goal would state the desired end (\"maintain each student's sense of identity\"); a problem statement uses the \"Since... must...\" form."},
{id:"q2",d:"PA",o:"PA 2.2",t:"mc",s:"A 40,000 SF lot permits a maximum FAR of 3.0, maximum lot coverage of 60%, and a maximum height of 90 feet. The client requires 16-foot floor-to-floor heights. What is the largest floor area the project can actually achieve?",opts:["96,000 SF","120,000 SF","129,600 SF","144,000 SF"],c:[1],e:"FAR allows 3.0 × 40,000 = 120,000 SF. Footprint allows 0.60 × 40,000 = 24,000 SF. At 24,000 SF per floor you would need 5 floors to reach 120,000 SF. Height: 90 ÷ 16 = 5.6, so 5 storeys fit. 5 × 24,000 = 120,000 SF — the FAR is achievable. The trap is answering 144,000 (6 storeys × 24,000), which the height limit forbids. Always test all three envelope controls and take the binding one."},
{id:"q3",d:"PA",o:"PA 2.2",t:"mc",s:"An owner wants to operate a small café in a district zoned exclusively residential. The ordinance does not list cafés as permitted or conditional in that district. The owner's only argument is that a café would be more profitable than a house. The appropriate application is:",opts:["A conditional use permit","A use variance, which is unlikely to be granted","A special exception","A text amendment, which is the only instrument available"],c:[1],e:"Because the use is not contemplated at all in the district, only a use variance could authorise it — and the test for a variance is a unique hardship arising from the property itself. Greater profitability is not hardship, so it is unlikely to succeed. A conditional use or special exception requires that the ordinance already contemplate the use in that district, which it does not. A text amendment is theoretically possible but is a legislative act applying jurisdiction-wide, not a remedy for one owner."},
{id:"q4",d:"PA",o:"PA 1.2",t:"mc",s:"A geotechnical report gives a plasticity index of 22 for the near-surface soil. The most significant design consequence is:",opts:["The soil has high bearing capacity and shallow footings are economical","The soil is expansive and foundations must accommodate volume change","The soil is organic and cannot be built upon","The soil is frictional and will require dewatering"],c:[1],e:"A plasticity index greater than 15 indicates expansive soil. Expansive clays swell and shrink with moisture change, which heaves slabs and footings — the response is grade beams on piers, deepened footings below the active zone, or moisture control. A high PI does not indicate high bearing capacity (clay is the weakest of the five IBC classes at 1,500 psf), and organic soils are identified by their organic content, not by PI."},
{id:"q5",d:"PA",o:"PA 3.1",t:"mc",s:"Zoning requires 1 parking space per 250 SF of gross floor area for a 55,000 GSF office building. Approximately how much land is needed for surface parking?",opts:["About 1.4 acres","About 1.9 acres","About 2.6 acres","About 4.4 acres"],c:[1],e:"55,000 ÷ 250 = 220 spaces. At 350–400 SF per car including aisles and manoeuvring, take 375: 220 × 375 = 82,500 SF = 1.89 acres. The cars-per-acre check confirms it: 220 ÷ 125 ≈ 1.76 acres. Answering 1.4 acres uses the stall dimension alone (9 × 20 = 180 SF), which omits the aisle — that is the error the item is built on."},
{id:"q6",d:"PA",o:"PA 4.5",t:"cata",s:"Under the April 2026 objective revisions, which of the following now fall within what PA 4.5 asks a candidate to do? Select all that apply.",opts:["Judge whether a stated project scope can be delivered within a given budget","Prepare a detailed line-item construction cost estimate","Assess whether a proposed schedule is realistic for the scope","Identify which of the four negotiable elements could absorb a shortfall","Perform a full quantity takeoff from construction documents","Recognise that fast-track scheduling requires a looser space programme"],c:[0,2,3,5],e:"PA 4.5 was narrowed in April 2026 from creating budgets and schedules to reviewing and assessing their feasibility. Judging fit, assessing a schedule, identifying which of Peña's four negotiable elements can move, and understanding the schedule-precision trade-off are all within the objective. Preparing a detailed estimate and performing a takeoff are not — and a takeoff belongs to PDD in any case."},
{id:"q7",d:"PA",o:"PA 3.2",t:"mc",s:"A survey, a geotechnical report and a Phase I ESA are provided. The survey shows a utility easement crossing the proposed building footprint; the geotechnical report recommends the footprint; the Phase I identifies a recognised environmental condition nearby. What governs the footprint location?",opts:["The geotechnical recommendation, because it addresses structural feasibility","The easement, because it is a recorded legal encumbrance","The Phase I, because environmental liability outranks other considerations","None of them — the building official decides"],c:[1],e:"A recorded legal instrument governs what you may build where. You cannot build over a utility easement without the utility's consent regardless of what the soil will bear. The geotechnical report tells you how to found the building once its location is legal; a Phase I identifies a likely condition but does not by itself relocate a building — it triggers a Phase II. Objective 3.2 is explicitly about reconciling documents that disagree."},
{id:"q8",d:"PA",o:"PA 1.1",t:"cata",s:"Which strategies are appropriate for a building in a hot–humid climate? Select all that apply.",opts:["Long east–west building axis","Compact form with minimal surface area","Large openings oriented to prevailing breezes","Heavy thermal mass with small windows","Tall ceilings and ceiling fans","Vapour control layer toward the exterior"],c:[0,2,4,5],e:"Hot–humid strategy is about moving air and shedding heat: long east–west axis, large openings to breezes, tall ceilings and fans, and — because the vapour drive runs inward — the vapour control layer toward the exterior. Compact form is a cold-climate strategy. Heavy thermal mass with small windows is hot–arid, where the day-night swing makes mass useful; in hot–humid conditions there is no night flush to charge it."},
{id:"q47",d:"PA",o:"PA 4.4",t:"mc",s:"A programmer has produced an interaction matrix and bubble diagrams. The next step, assigning departments to specific areas on a given floor, is called:",opts:["Stacking","Blocking","Zoning","Massing"],c:[1],e:"Blocking assigns departments to areas within a floor; stacking assigns departments to floors. Blocking is horizontal, stacking is vertical — stacking has storeys in it. Peña's own vocabulary is bubble diagram at micro and macro scale; blocking and stacking are the later industry terms the exam expects."},
{id:"q48",d:"PA",o:"PA 4.6",t:"mc",s:"A programme requires a 90-foot column-free span for an exhibition hall on an aggressive schedule and a constrained budget. The most appropriate structural system to carry forward is:",opts:["Post-tensioned concrete flat plate","Long-span steel joists of the LH or DLH series","Cast-in-place concrete beam and girder","Light-frame wood trusses"],c:[1],e:"LH-series joists span to 96 ft and DLH-series to 240 ft, they fabricate off site while foundations proceed, and they are economical for long clear spans. Flat plates and beam-and-girder concrete are short-to-medium span systems and slower to build. Light-frame wood trusses do not reach 90 ft. The trade-off to state is depth: long-span joists are deep and drive floor-to-floor and façade area upward."},
{id:"q52",d:"PA",o:"PA 2.1",t:"mc",s:"At feasibility stage a client proposes converting a 14,000 SF single-storey warehouse into a restaurant and event space. Which code consequence should be raised first?",opts:["The change from Group S to Group A will trigger sprinkler requirements and recalculated egress","Interior finishes will need to be Class A","A new roof assembly will be required","The building will need a new means of egress illumination system"],c:[0],e:"The change of occupancy from storage to assembly is the governing consequence: occupant load factors drop from 500 SF gross to as little as 7 or 15 SF net, which multiplies the occupant load and recalculates exits, widths and separation; and Group A-2 requires sprinklers above 5,000 SF or 100 occupants. Finishes, roofing and egress lighting all follow from that reclassification rather than preceding it. PA identifies these consequences; PPD computes them."},
{id:"q56",d:"PA",o:"PA 3.3",t:"mc",s:"On a topographic plan, a set of contours forms a V pointing toward higher elevations. This indicates:",opts:["A ridge","A valley or swale","A depression","A level bench"],c:[1],e:"Contours point uphill at a valley or swale and downhill at a ridge or crown. The V points toward higher elevation because the low line of the valley cuts back into the slope. Closed concentric loops increasing toward the centre indicate a hill; decreasing toward the centre, a depression. This reading is the basis of most site hotspot items."},
{id:"q57",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 1.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Shading a West Façade</p><p>A two-story pediatric clinic in Sacramento has its main waiting room on the west side of the building. Because of site limits, the long glazed wall must face west. In late afternoon the space gets hot and has bad glare. The owner wants a fixed exterior shading solution.</p><p>Which shading strategy is most effective for this glazing?</p>",opts:["A deep horizontal overhang sized to shade the glass at noon on the summer solstice","Fixed exterior vertical fins along the west glazing","An interior light shelf at 7 ft above the floor to bounce light onto the ceiling","Clear glazing with interior roller shades that occupants can lower"],c:[1],e:"<p>East- and west-facing glass gets low-altitude sun. A horizontal overhang is \"somewhat effective\" only when the sun is high, and \"not effective at low-altitude\" sun angles. If spaces can't be oriented north or south, vertical fins are the effective strategy for east and west orientations. (Eggcrate devices, which combine overhangs and fins, also work well in hot climates.)</p><ul><li>A → This is the right device for south glass, where the summer sun is high. Late-afternoon sun from the west comes in under it.</li><li>C → A light shelf distributes daylight and shades the lower part of a window. It doesn't block low western sun.</li><li>D → Interior shades cut glare, but the solar heat has already passed through the glass. The owner asked for exterior control.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 6, Section 6.5(a) \"Shading for Orientation,\" PDF p.186</p>"},
{id:"q58",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 1.2",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Passive Cooling in a Hot-Dry Climate</p><p>A community library is planned for a high-desert town with hot, dry summer days and cool nights. The client wants to use as little mechanical cooling as possible.</p><p>Check the THREE passive cooling strategies that best suit this climate.</p>",opts:["High-mass construction (exposed concrete/masonry) that is closed to hot outdoor air by day and flushed with cool night air","Thermally lightweight construction kept open to breezes 24 hours a day","Evaporative cooling (for example, a cooltower with wetted pads)","A masonry courtyard with movable shading by day, opened to the night sky","Large areas of unshaded west-facing glass to increase daylight","Sealing the building and relying on dehumidification as the main strategy"],c:[0,2,3],e:"<ul><li>A: High-mass cooling with night ventilation is a \"hot-dry summer design strategy.\" The building is thermally closed by day and opened at night so cool outside air can flush the heat stored in the mass.</li><li>C: Evaporative cooling follows the constant wet-bulb line. It works in conditions that are \"more uncomfortably dry than uncomfortably hot.\"</li><li>D: The masonry courtyard buildings of the Mediterranean are the indigenous example of high-mass cooling. Their floors and roofs are shaded by day (toldos) and opened to night-sky radiation.</li><li>B: Natural ventilation with lightweight construction \"may be the only passive strategy available in humid, hot climates.\" In a desert, lightweight construction gives up the benefit of cool nights.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 4, \"Design Strategies for Cooling\" (a)–(d), PDF pp.127–129</p>"},
{id:"q59",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 1.2",t:"num",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Sizing Skylights by Daylight Factor</p><p>A one-story university drafting and model-making studio has 3,500 ft² of floor area under a flat roof. In schematic design, the architect sets a target average daylight factor (DFav) of 4.0%, the low end of the range for difficult, prolonged tasks like drafting. Assume evenly spaced horizontal skylights and overcast-sky conditions.</p><p>Using the toplighting guideline for horizontal skylights, what is the minimum total skylight glazing area in square feet?</p><p>Formula (horizontal skylights): DFav = 0.35 × (skylight glazing area / floor area)</p>",ans:400,tol:0.5,unit:"ft²",e:"<p>Calculation:<br>0.04 = 0.35 × (A / 3,500)<br>A = (0.04 × 3,500) / 0.35<br>A = 140 / 0.35 = 400 ft²</p><p>Check: 0.35 × (400 / 3,500) = 0.040 = 4.0% ✓</p><p>Table 14.2 recommends a DF of 4–8% for \"difficult, prolonged tasks, such as drafting.\" Table 14.4 Part B gives DFav = 0.35 × (glazing/floor) for horizontal skylights. Vertical monitors are less efficient (0.2 coefficient) and would need 700 ft² for the same target. North-facing sawtooth glazing (0.33) falls in between.</p><p>Note: On the exam, enter the numeric value only (e.g., 400).</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 14, Table 14.2, PDF p.623; Table 14.4 Part B, PDF p.625</p>"},
{id:"q60",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 1.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Reading an Evaporative Cooling Process</p><p>A mechanical engineer plots the supply-air path of a direct evaporative cooler for a warehouse in Phoenix on a psychrometric chart. Outdoor air passes through wetted pads before it enters the building.</p><p>Which description of the process is correct?</p>",opts:["The air moves horizontally to the left along a line of constant humidity ratio. Dry-bulb temperature drops and moisture content stays the same.","The air moves up and to the left along a line of roughly constant wet-bulb temperature (constant enthalpy). Dry-bulb temperature drops while relative humidity and moisture content rise.","The air moves down and to the right. Moisture is removed and dry-bulb temperature rises, with no change in enthalpy.","The air is cooled to its dew point and then continues along the saturation curve, losing both sensible heat and moisture."],c:[1],e:"<p>Evaporative cooling travels along lines of constant enthalpy, which are almost parallel to wet-bulb lines. Sensible heat drops as the air cools, and latent heat rises by the same amount as moisture is added, so total heat doesn't change.</p><ul><li>A → Simple (sensible) cooling, such as a cooling coil before saturation. It runs along a constant humidity-ratio line.</li><li>C → Chemical (desiccant) dehumidifying, the opposite of evaporative cooling.</li><li>D → Mechanical cooling and dehumidifying at a coil (steps 1 and 2 of the cooling process).</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 8, Section 8.12 \"Psychrometry,\" PDF pp.308–309; Ch. 4, \"Design Strategies for Cooling\" (d) Evaporative Cooling, PDF p.129</p>"},
{id:"q61",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 1.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Equipment Noise at the Property Line</p><p>A rooftop condensing unit on a small medical office building produces a sound level of 78 dB measured 25 ft from the unit. The nearest residential property line is 100 ft from the unit, with no barriers or reflecting surfaces in between (free-field conditions).</p><p>What is the approximate sound level at the property line?</p>",opts:["72 dB","66 dB","60 dB","19.5 dB"],c:[1],e:"<p>Calculation:<br>Distance ratio = 100 ft / 25 ft = 4 (two doublings: 25→50→100)<br>Each doubling of distance from a point source = −6 dB<br>78 dB − 6 dB − 6 dB = 66 dB<br>(Check: 20 log(100/25) = 20 × 0.602 = 12.0 dB reduction → 78 − 12 = 66 dB)</p><ul><li>A → Counts only one doubling (−6 dB).</li><li>C → Applies 6 dB per doubling three times (as if the distance were 8× instead of 4×).</li><li>D → Divides the dB value by 4. Decibels are logarithmic and can't be scaled linearly.</li></ul><p>Note that doubling or halving the source power changes the level by only 3 dB. The 6 dB rule is for distance.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 17, Section 17.5, Example 17.4, PDF p.776; Fig. 17.13, PDF p.777</p>"},
{id:"q62",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 1.2",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Passive Solar Heating Systems</p><p>A client is comparing passive solar heating options for a new mountain residence. Match each statement to the passive solar system it describes:</p>",items:["Sunlight enters the living space and strikes exposed thermal mass. The mass surfaces should be at least three times the area of the south-facing glass, typically at least 4 in. thick.","A dense masonry or concrete wall, usually 8 to 12 in. thick, sits behind south glazing. It gives less glare, less overheating, no fading of furnishings, and large radiant heat output in the evening.","A habitable glazed room with big temperature swings, hot in the afternoon and cold before dawn, which passes some of its heat to the space behind it.","Popular for its simplicity, daylight, and view to the south, but prone to glare, overheating on sunny days, large night-time heat loss through glass, and fading of furnishings."],opts:["Direct Gain (DG)","Indirect Gain (TW – Trombe wall)","Isolated Gain (SS – sunspace)"],c:[0,1,2,0],e:"<p>The three strategies differ in where the sun strikes first. In direct gain it strikes the occupied space. In indirect gain it strikes a mass wall that passes only heat to the room. In isolated gain it strikes a separate sunspace. Direct gain pairs well with the summer strategy of night ventilation of mass, because both depend on large areas of exposed internal mass.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 4, Section 4.4(a)–(c), PDF pp.130 and 132</p>"},
{id:"q63",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 2.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Construction Type Selection</p><p>A developer wants a three-story mixed-use building. Code-required exterior walls will be noncombustible: masonry and light-gauge steel with noncombustible sheathing. To control cost, the floors, roof, and interior partitions will be ordinary wood framing (not heavy timber).</p><p>Which IBC construction type describes this building?</p>",opts:["Type IIB","Type III","Type IV","Type VB"],c:[1],e:"<p>In Type III (A and B), exterior walls are noncombustible, but interior elements \"may not be necessarily of noncombustible construction.\"</p><ul><li>A → Types I and II require noncombustible construction throughout, including interior elements.</li><li>C → Type IV also has noncombustible exterior walls, but its interior elements are solid wood heavy timber. Ordinary light wood framing doesn't qualify.</li><li>D → Type V allows any material permitted by code, including combustible exterior walls. Calling this building Type V would understate its construction and forfeit the larger allowances it earns as Type III.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Olin's Construction Principles, Materials, and Methods, Section 1.3 (construction types), PDF p.45</p>"},
{id:"q64",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 2.2",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Construction Type vs. Allowable Area</p><p>A school district needs a new Group E educational building with 40,000 ft² of total floor area. The building will not be sprinklered, and the design team is taking no frontage increase. The team is testing the IBC height-and-area table to see which construction types can hold the program.</p><p>Check the THREE construction types that can accommodate the 40,000 ft² program.</p>",opts:["Type IB","Type IIA","Type IIB","Type IIIA","Type IIIB","Type VA"],c:[0,1,3],e:"<p>Explanation (from the worked example in Olin's):</p><ul><li>Type IA is unlimited (UL). The building could be one 40,000 ft² floor or five floors of 8,000 ft².</li><li>Types IB and IIA also work.</li><li>Type IIB does not: it allows only two floors of 14,500 ft² each, a total of 29,000 ft², which is less than 40,000 ft².</li><li>Type IIIA works; IIIB does not.</li><li>Type IV works; Type V does not.</li></ul><p>Takeaway: Without sprinklers or frontage increases, the \"B\" (unprotected) versions of a type get much smaller allowable areas than the \"A\" (protected) versions. Changing the construction type is one of the planning levers for fitting a program.</p><p>Note: Olin's example uses the 2009 IBC table. For this Group E case, the tabular areas and conclusions are the same in the 2021 IBC (IIB = 14,500 ft² per story, 2 stories, unsprinklered). Always confirm against the adopted code.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Olin's Construction Principles, Materials, and Methods, Section 1.3, Figure 1.3-1 discussion, PDF p.45</p>"},
{id:"q65",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 2.2",t:"num",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Occupant Load and Stair Width</p><p>A new community center has a 4,200 ft² (net) multipurpose hall on the second floor. For lectures, it is set with loose chairs only: no tables and no fixed seating. All occupants of the hall will exit down two exit stairs.</p><p>Using an occupant load factor of 7 ft² net per person (assembly, concentrated use without fixed seating) and 0.3 in. of stair width per occupant, what is the minimum total stair width required, in inches?</p>",ans:180,tol:0.5,unit:"in.",e:"<p>Calculation:<br>Occupant load = 4,200 ft² ÷ 7 ft²/person = 600 occupants<br>Total stair width = 600 × 0.3 in./person = 180 in.<br>(For example, two stairs at 90 in. clear each.)</p><p>Related check (doors/level components): 600 × 0.2 in./person = 120 in. of total door width.</p><p>Common errors:</p><ul><li>Using the less-concentrated (tables and chairs) factor of 15 ft² net gives 280 occupants and 84 in. That would be wrong for a chairs-only layout.</li><li>Using 0.2 in./person for stairs gives 120 in. That factor is for doors, corridors, and ramps, not stairs.</li></ul><p>Divide the floor area by the occupant load factor to find the occupants, then multiply by the capacity factor for each egress component. These are the NFPA 101 values summarized in MEEB; the 2021 IBC uses the same 7 net / 15 net factors and the same 0.3 in. stair / 0.2 in. other-component factors. The IBC's reduced factors for sprinklered buildings with emergency voice/alarm communication are not applied here.</p><p>Note: On the exam, enter the numeric value only (e.g., 180).</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 24, Table 24.4 and Example 24.1, PDF pp.1113 and 1116</p>"},
{id:"q66",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 2.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Sprinkler System for Unheated Space</p><p>A food distributor's new facility includes a 35,000 ft² unheated loading dock and cold-storage area where temperatures regularly drop below freezing. The rest of the building is heated office space.</p><p>Which sprinkler system type is most appropriate for the loading dock and cold-storage area?</p>",opts:["Wet-pipe system with an alarm check valve","Dry-pipe system","Antifreeze system","Deluge system with open heads"],c:[1],e:"<p>Dry-pipe systems are filled with compressed air (or nitrogen) instead of water, and \"are used in unheated areas, including loading docks and cold-storage areas.\" When a head opens, the air escapes, the dry-pipe valve opens, and water fills the piping. The dry-pipe valve itself must be in a heated enclosure, and a maximum system capacity of 750 gal is recommended because of the delay in delivering water.</p><ul><li>A → The pipes are always full of water and would freeze.</li><li>C → Antifreeze systems protect areas subject to freezing that are too small for a dry-pipe system. A 35,000 ft² area is not small.</li><li>D → Deluge systems are for high-hazard areas where extremely rapid fire spread is expected, such as aircraft hangars and flammable liquids. All heads discharge at once.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 24, \"Water for Fire Suppression\" (l) \"Dry-Pipe Systems,\" PDF pp.1140–1141; Architectural Graphic Standards, \"Sprinkler System Types,\" PDF p.283</p>"},
{id:"q67",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 2.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Accessible Route at a Column</p><p>In a renovation of a museum gallery wing, an existing structural column sits along a 36-in.-wide accessible route. Between the column and the wall there is only a short pinch point.</p><p>Under ICC/ANSI A117.1 (as illustrated in AGS), which condition is acceptable at the pinch point?</p>",opts:["Clear width reduced to 30 in. for a length of up to 48 in.","Clear width reduced to 32 in. for a length of up to 24 in.","Clear width reduced to 32 in. for any length, as long as a 60-in. turning space is provided at each end","Clear width reduced to 34 in. for a length of up to 60 in."],c:[1],e:"<p>The clear width of an accessible route must be 36 in. It may be reduced to 32 in. for a distance of 24 in. (Fig. 13.27).</p><ul><li>A → 30 in. is the width of a wheelchair clear floor space (30 × 48 in.), not a permitted route width.</li><li>C → A turning space doesn't permit a longer reduced-width section.</li><li>D → No such provision exists.</li></ul><p>Related rules on the same page: at a 180° turn around an element less than 48 in. wide, the route must be 42 in. minimum (unless the turn is 60 in. wide). Changes in level greater than ½ in. must be ramped.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, \"Accessible Routes and Walking Surfaces,\" Figs. 13.27–13.30, PDF p.487</p>"},
{id:"q68",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 2.2",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Smoke Detector Selection</p><p>A senior living facility includes a commercial kitchen, a library with upholstered furniture where smoldering fires are the main concern, and a quiet chapel with stagnant air. The fire protection engineer is choosing detector types.</p><p>Check the TWO statements that are correct.</p>",opts:["Ionization detectors respond best to fast-burning flaming fires and to the very small particles of a fire's incipient stage.","Ionization detectors are the best choice for the commercial kitchen because they react to minute combustion particles.","Photoelectric detectors are recommended where the fire is expected to be smoky, with the larger, visible particles typical of the smoldering stage.","Ionization detectors should be mounted on warm ceilings where hot air concentrates, so that combustion particles reach them faster.","Once a fire becomes smoky, an ionization detector gains sensitivity."],c:[0,2],e:"<p>Ionization detectors sense particles of 0.01–1.0 micron from the incipient stage. They work best indoors in stagnant or low-velocity air, and they should be kept out of kitchens, bakeries, welding shops, and areas with engine exhaust. Smoldering-stage particles (up to 10 microns) are visible smoke and are best detected photoelectrically. In this facility: photoelectric for the library, ionization suits the chapel, and neither smoke type belongs in the kitchen.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 24, \"Automatic Fire Detection\" (incipient stage; 24.17 smoldering stage), PDF pp.1166–1167</p>"},
{id:"q69",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 1.3",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Daylight Depth from a Side Window</p><p>An elementary school classroom has clear-glazed windows along one exterior wall. The window head is 9'-6\" above the finished floor, and desks (the work plane) are 2'-6\" above the floor. The architect is using a schematic-design rule of thumb to estimate how deep into the room the sidelighting will be adequate under overcast skies.</p><p>Using the 2.5H guideline, approximately how far from the window wall will daylight be sufficient at the work plane?</p>",opts:["15.0 ft","17.5 ft","22.5 ft","23.75 ft"],c:[1],e:"<p>Calculation:<br>H = window head height above the work plane = 9.5 ft − 2.5 ft = 7.0 ft<br>Daylit depth = 2.5 × H = 2.5 × 7.0 = 17.5 ft</p><p>The 2.5H guideline assumes adequate work-plane daylight up to 2.5 times the window head height above the work plane. It assumes clear glazing, overcast skies, no major obstructions, and window width about half of the wall.</p><ul><li>D → Measures H from the floor (2.5 × 9.5 = 23.75 ft).</li><li>A → Confuses this rule with the 15/30 guideline (15 ft fully daylit, 15–30 ft partially daylit).</li><li>C → A miscalculation.</li></ul><p>Placing windows high in the wall pushes daylight deeper, which is why higher heads and light shelves help.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 14, Section 14.9(a) \"The 2.5H Guideline,\" PDF p.623; Fig. 14.21, PDF p.624</p>"},
{id:"q70",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 1.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Sizing Cross-Ventilation Openings</p><p>A charter school in coastal North Carolina has a single-loaded classroom wing with operable windows on both long walls. The client wants to maximize the total volume of air moving through each classroom on breezy days.</p><p>Which window strategy best meets this goal?</p>",opts:["Make the inlet (windward) openings smaller than the outlet (leeward) openings","Make the inlet and outlet openings the same size, and as large as practical","Make the outlet openings smaller than the inlet openings","Close the windward windows and rely on a stack (solar chimney) exhaust at the corridor roof"],c:[1],e:"<p>Airflow within a room increases as window size increases. To maximize airflow, the inlet and outlet openings should be the same size.</p><ul><li>A → A smaller inlet increases inlet velocity, which gives a stronger jet near the window, not more total airflow.</li><li>C → A smaller outlet produces low but more uniform air speed.</li><li>D → The stack effect is weak. It works best when air temperatures are highest, which is when ventilation may not be wanted. Wind-driven cross ventilation is the primary strategy here.</li></ul><p>AGS also notes that a 40/60 to 60/40 inlet/outlet split keeps airflow adequate, and that about 30 air changes per hour is a rule of thumb for structure cooling.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, \"Basic Principles of Natural Ventilation,\" PDF p.453</p>"},
{id:"q71",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 1.1",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Windbreaks and Site Microclimate</p><p>A winery on an exposed hillside wants a sheltered outdoor tasting terrace protected from strong prevailing winter winds. It also wants some breeze on summer afternoons. The landscape architect is studying a windbreak of fencing and trees.</p><p>Check the THREE statements that are correct.</p>",opts:["The denser the windbreak, the greater the reduction in wind speed, but the smaller the area protected behind it.","A solid (impermeable) fence protects the largest area downwind because it blocks all the wind.","Gaps in a windbreak can produce wind speeds through the gap higher than the undiverted wind, which can help summer breezes.","Distances of protection behind a windbreak are expressed in multiples of the windbreak's height.","Winds are generally strongest in the early morning, so the terrace should be oriented for morning shelter only."],c:[0,2,3],e:"<p>As wind passes an obstacle it speeds up, and negative pressure forms at the sides of and behind it. Solid barriers produce the lowest wind speeds just behind them, but the wind recovers full velocity sooner, so a solid barrier is effective for the shortest distance. Gaps threaten winter protection but are an opportunity to speed up summer breezes. Because winds generally build through the day with solar heating, the design can shift from barrier to connector between cool mornings and hot afternoons.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 3, \"(c) Wind Control,\" PDF p.92; Figs. 3.25–3.26, PDF p.93</p>"},
{id:"q72",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 1.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Mean Radiant Temperature Near Glass</p><p>In a Minneapolis office, an employee's workstation sits next to a large single-pane window in an older building. On a winter morning the thermostat reads 72°F, yet the employee complains of feeling cold. From the employee's position, the inner surface of the glass (50°F) occupies 90° of the surrounding view. The remaining interior surfaces (74°F) occupy 270°.</p><p>Using the angle-weighted method, what is the approximate mean radiant temperature (MRT) at the workstation?</p>",opts:["62.0°F","66.0°F","68.0°F","72.0°F"],c:[2],e:"<p>Calculation:<br>MRT = Σ(t × α) / 360<br>MRT = (50°F × 90° + 74°F × 270°) / 360<br>MRT = (4,500 + 19,980) / 360 = 24,480 / 360 = 68.0°F</p><p>MRT is the uniform temperature of an imaginary enclosure that would exchange the same radiant heat with the body as the actual non-uniform surroundings. It is calculated, not directly measured. Dry-bulb air temperature alone is \"usually not an adequate comfort indicator.\" Here the cold glass lowers the MRT about 4°F below the air temperature, which explains the complaint. Fixes include better glazing, interior storm panels, or perimeter heating, not raising the thermostat.</p><ul><li>A → A simple average of 50°F and 74°F, ignoring the angles.</li><li>D → Assumes MRT equals air temperature.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 4, \"Mean Radiant Temperature (MRT),\" Equation 4.2, PDF p.118</p>"},
{id:"q73",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 1.3",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Reducing Reverberation in a Lecture Room</p><p>A 30 ft × 40 ft lecture room with a 12-ft ceiling has hard finishes. It currently has a total of 480 sabins of absorption at mid-frequencies, and faculty complain that speech is hard to understand. The acoustical consultant sets a target reverberation time of 0.8 s.</p><p>Using the Sabine relationship TR = 0.05 V / ΣA (I-P units), how much absorption must be added?</p>",opts:["240 sabins","420 sabins","900 sabins","1,380 sabins"],c:[1],e:"<p>Calculation:<br>V = 30 × 40 × 12 = 14,400 ft³<br>Existing TR = 0.05 × 14,400 / 480 = 1.5 s<br>Required ΣA = 0.05 × 14,400 / 0.8 = 900 sabins<br>Added absorption = 900 − 480 = 420 sabins</p><ul><li>C → 900 sabins is the total absorption required, not the amount to add.</li><li>D → Adds 900 on top of the existing 480.</li><li>A → A miscalculation.</li></ul><p>For speech, intelligibility governs, so speech rooms should be less reverberant than music rooms. The added absorption is often a high-NRC ceiling or wall panels. NRC is the average of the absorption coefficients at 250, 500, 1000, and 2000 Hz. It rates absorption only and says nothing about blocking sound between rooms.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 18, Equation 18.15 and Section 18.10, PDF p.802; Section 18.11, PDF p.804</p>"},
{id:"q74",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 1.3",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Mass Law and Wall Upgrades</p><p>A homeowner next to a busy band-practice studio asks the architect to \"just double the weight\" of the existing solid, sealed masonry party wall. She expects this to cut the transmitted noise dramatically.</p><p>According to the mass law, about how much does doubling the surface mass of a limp, homogeneous, nonporous wall increase its transmission loss?</p>",opts:["3 dB","6 dB","10 dB","Transmission loss doubles (about twice the dB value)"],c:[1],e:"<p>The mass law says transmission loss is proportional to the logarithm of surface mass times frequency. Doubling the mass, or the frequency, raises TL by about 6 dB in theory. Field results are often closer to 4–5 dB because of stiffness, porosity, and non-homogeneity. To get much more isolation, the mass increase must be very large, so designers usually use other strategies: resilient mounting, double-wythe or staggered construction with an air space, and sealing flanking paths.</p><ul><li>A → 3 dB is the change from doubling sound power, not wall mass.</li><li>C → 10 dB corresponds to a tenfold change in intensity.</li><li>D → Decibels are logarithmic and don't double.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 19, Section 19.8 \"Barrier Mass,\" PDF p.830; Olin's Construction Principles, Materials, and Methods, \"Concrete and Unit Masonry Walls,\" PDF p.1423</p>"},
{id:"q75",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 1.1",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Energy-Conscious Site Planning in a Hot Climate</p><p>A two-story regional office building is planned for Houston. The architect is setting up the massing and plan zoning in schematic design.</p><p>Check the THREE strategies AGS recommends for this hot climate.</p>",opts:["Orient the longer walls to face north and south (long axis running east–west)","Place storage and other low-use rooms on the east and west sides as climatic buffers","Place storage and other low-use rooms on the north side as climatic buffers","Use a ventilated double roof with a light-colored, lightweight outer layer shading a well-insulated inner roof","Center the plan on a fully enclosed, glass-roofed atrium as the main microclimate space","Maximize east- and west-facing glazing to capture views and daylight"],c:[0,1,3],e:"<p>Equator-facing walls are ideal for windows because small horizontal overhangs shade them effectively, while east and west walls overheat and are hard to shade. Low-use spaces work as buffers on the east and west in hot climates, or on the north in cool climates. In hot climates the roof is the primary source of unwanted heat gain, so a ventilated double roof, or a radiant barrier, is one of the best roof strategies.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, \"Energy Conservation Strategies for Nonresidential Buildings,\" items 1–3, PDF p.462; item 16, PDF p.463</p>"},
{id:"q76",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 2.2",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Occupancy Classification</p><p>A developer's mixed-use campus includes the tenants and uses below. Match each use to its IBC occupancy group:</p>",items:["A hospital with inpatient medical care","A private art gallery open to the public for exhibitions","A professional engineering firm's offices","A retail store selling outdoor gear","A detached private garage accessory to a caretaker's dwelling","A K–8 school"],opts:["Group A (Assembly)","Group B (Business)","Group E (Educational)","Group I (Institutional)","Group M (Mercantile)","Group U (Utility & Miscellaneous)"],c:[3,0,1,4,5,2],e:"<p>Occupancy classification is the first step of a code analysis. It drives allowable height and area, construction type, fire-resistance ratings, occupant load factors, and sprinkler triggers. Note that the art gallery, often mistaken for M or B, is listed under Assembly.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Olin's Construction Principles, Materials, and Methods, Section 1.3 (use groups), PDF pp.44–45</p>"},
{id:"q77",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 2.2",t:"num",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Accessible Entrance Ramp Length</p><p>An existing library's main entrance floor is 45 in. above the adjacent sidewalk. The architect plans a straight accessible ramp in one direction, with no switchbacks, at the maximum permitted running slope. Landings are required at the bottom, at the top, and wherever needed between runs. Each landing will be the minimum 60 in. long in the direction of travel.</p><p>What is the minimum total horizontal length of the ramp system, including all landings, in feet?</p>",ans:60,tol:0.5,unit:"ft",e:"<p>Calculation:<br>Maximum running slope = 1:12<br>Total horizontal run = 45 in. × 12 = 540 in. = 45 ft<br>Maximum rise per run = 30 in. → 45 in. / 30 in. = 1.5 → 2 runs required (e.g., 22.5 in. rise / 22.5 ft each)<br>Landings: bottom + 1 intermediate + top = 3 landings × 5 ft (60 in.) = 15 ft<br>Total length = 45 ft + 15 ft = 60 ft</p><p>Accessible ramps must have a running slope of 1:12 or less, and anything steeper than 1:20 is a ramp. The maximum rise of a single run is 30 in. Level landings at least as wide as the ramp and at least 60 in. long are needed at the top, the bottom, and intermediate levels. Because the rise exceeds 6 in., handrails are required on both sides, with edge protection at ramps and landings. A switchback design would need 60 × 60 in. turning landings instead. AGS also recommends using the lowest practical slope (1:16 or less benefits older people and children).</p><p>Note: On the exam, enter the numeric value only (e.g., 60).</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Olin's Construction Principles, Materials, and Methods, Section 1.4.3.4 \"Ramps,\" PDF p.59; Architectural Graphic Standards, \"Accessible Ramps,\" PDF p.491 and \"Ramps,\" PDF p.357</p>"},
{id:"q78",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 2.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Sprinkler Head Count for a Restaurant Kitchen</p><p>A new restaurant's back-of-house kitchen and service area measures 40 ft × 52 ft with a smooth, noncombustible, unobstructed ceiling. For sprinkler design, restaurant service areas are classified as Ordinary Hazard (Group 1). The seating area is Light Hazard.</p><p>Using a maximum protection area of 130 ft² per standard upright/pendant sprinkler and a maximum spacing of 15 ft, what is the minimum number of sprinkler heads for the kitchen?</p>",opts:["10","12","16","21"],c:[2],e:"<p>Calculation:<br>Area = 40 × 52 = 2,080 ft²<br>By protection area: 2,080 / 130 = 16.0 → 16 heads<br>By spacing alone: ⌈40/15⌉ × ⌈52/15⌉ = 3 × 4 = 12 heads → 2,080/12 = 173 ft² per head &gt; 130 ft², so spacing alone is not enough<br>Protection area governs → 16 heads (e.g., a 4 × 4 grid at 10 ft × 13 ft = 130 ft² each)</p><ul><li>A → Uses the Light Hazard limit of 225 ft² (2,080/225 = 9.2 → 10). That limit applies to the dining room, not the kitchen.</li><li>B → Checks the 15-ft spacing only.</li><li>D → Uses the Extra Hazard limit of 100 ft² (20.8 → 21).</li></ul><p>Hazard classification sets both density and spacing, which is why one restaurant can have different sprinkler layouts in its dining and kitchen areas.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 24, Table 24.8 (hazard classification), PDF p.1134; Table 24.9 Part A (protection area/spacing), PDF p.1136</p>"},
{id:"q79",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 2.3",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Preaction Systems for Water-Sensitive Spaces</p><p>A university is building a rare-books archive and a server room. The owner is worried about accidental water discharge damaging the collections and equipment. The fire protection engineer recommends a preaction sprinkler system.</p><p>Check the THREE characteristics of a preaction system that support this recommendation.</p>",opts:["The sprinkler piping holds air, not water, until a separate detection system opens the preaction valve.","The heat or smoke detectors are more sensitive than the sprinkler heads, so an alarm sounds before any head opens.","The early alarm often lets staff find and put out the fire manually before any sprinkler opens.","Once the valve opens, water discharges from every head on the system at once.","Piping is filled with water under pressure at all times for the fastest possible discharge."],c:[0,1,2],e:"<p>In a preaction system, water is held back by the preaction valve until detectors open it. Water then fills the pipes, and it discharges only where a head has opened. That is why the system is popular where contents are especially subject to water damage: computer rooms, retail stores, museums. In the double-interlock variation, the pipes hold compressed air and water enters only after both a detector and a head have operated. Caution: delaying water discharge lets a fire grow larger.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 24, \"(m) Preaction Systems\" and \"(n) Deluge Systems,\" PDF p.1142; Architectural Graphic Standards, \"Sprinkler System Types,\" PDF p.283</p>"},
{id:"q80",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 2.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Dead-End Corridor in an Office Building</p><p>A tenant fit-out on one floor of a new office building (business occupancy) creates a dead-end corridor 40 ft long. The building is fully sprinklered.</p><p>Using the egress limits for new business occupancies, how should the architect evaluate this dead end?</p>",opts:["It doesn't comply. Dead ends are limited to 20 ft regardless of sprinklers.","It complies, because sprinklered business occupancies allow dead ends up to 50 ft. Without sprinklers, the limit would be 20 ft.","It complies, because dead ends in business occupancies are limited only by the 300-ft travel distance.","It doesn't comply. Dead ends must not exceed the 75-ft common path of travel minus the corridor width."],c:[1],e:"<p>For new business occupancies, MEEB Table 24.3 lists dead-end limits of 20 ft unsprinklered and 50 ft sprinklered. Common path limits are 75 ft / 100 ft and travel distance limits are 200 ft / 300 ft (unsprinklered / sprinklered). Sprinklers increase the allowable distances, but dead ends are still restricted because people may have to travel toward a fire to get out of one. At least 30% of building fire deaths result from fire cutting off the paths to exits.</p><ul><li>A → Ignores the sprinkler increase.</li><li>C → Confuses dead-end limits with travel distance.</li><li>D → Combines unrelated limits.</li></ul><p>The 2021 IBC uses the same 20 ft / 50 ft dead-end values for sprinklered Group B. Confirm against the adopted code.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 24, Table 24.3 \"Common Path, Dead End, and Travel Distance Limits,\" PDF pp.1114–1115; discussion PDF p.1112</p>"},
{id:"q81",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 2.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Rating of Opening Protectives</p><p>In a six-story hotel, the architect is scheduling doors. Each exit stair is enclosed with 2-hour fire-resistance-rated construction.</p><p>What fire-protection rating is typically required for the doors into the stair enclosure?</p>",opts:["3-hour","1-1/2-hour","3/4-hour","20-minute"],c:[1],e:"<p>Opening ratings depend on what the barrier does:</p><ul><li>4-hour and 3-hour openings: in fire walls, or walls that divide a single building into fire areas (A is incorrect)</li><li>1-1/2-hour and 1-hour openings: in multistory vertical communication enclosures and 2-hour-rated partitions providing horizontal fire separations (B is correct for a 2-hour stair enclosure)</li><li>3/4-hour and 20-minute openings: in walls or partitions between rooms and corridors rated 1 hour or less (C and D are incorrect)</li></ul><p>Fire doors are tested to NFPA 252 (UL 10B) and installed per NFPA 80. They must be self-closing or automatic-closing, and their labels must stay unpainted and unaltered.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, \"Fire-Resistance-Rated Openings — Types of Openings,\" PDF p.218</p>"},
{id:"q82",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 2.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Standpipe System Class</p><p>Case: A seven-story office building is being designed in a suburban jurisdiction. The building will be fully sprinklered. During a pre-design meeting, the fire marshal says:</p><ul><li>Hose stations must be usable by trained firefighters for full-scale firefighting.</li><li>Occupants should evacuate, not fight fires with hoses.</li><li>Hose connections must be 2-1/2 in., at designated locations.</li></ul><p>Which standpipe class best matches the fire marshal's requirements?</p>",opts:["Class I","Class II","Class III","A Class II system combined with the sprinkler riser"],c:[0],e:"<p>Class I systems are for full-scale firefighting by trained firefighters using 2-1/2-in. hose connections at designated locations. They are typically required in both sprinklered and unsprinklered buildings more than three stories high, because laying out hoses from outdoor connections takes so long.</p><ul><li>B → Class II systems are for first aid by occupants, with 1-1/2-in. hose and racks. Their use is declining because untrained people struggle to handle 100 ft of hose carrying 100 gpm or more, and many fire departments prefer that occupants evacuate.</li><li>C → Class III systems combine Classes I and II. They carry the same disadvantages as Class II.</li><li>D → A \"combined\" system means a Class I or III standpipe that also supplies sprinklers. A Class II system wouldn't meet the full-scale firefighting requirement.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., Ch. 24, \"(a) Standpipes and Hoses,\" PDF pp.1125 and 1127; Architectural Graphic Standards, \"Standpipes,\" PDF p.283</p>"},
{id:"q83",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 3.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Concrete Floor System for a Residential Tower</p><p>A developer is planning a 16-story condominium tower with column bays of roughly 24 ft x 26 ft. The pro forma depends on fitting the maximum number of floors under a zoning height limit, the owner wants the underside of the slab painted and exposed in the units (no suspended ceilings), and the unit layouts vary floor to floor, so column locations need some flexibility. Live loads are typical residential loads.</p><p>Which cast-in-place concrete floor system best meets these goals?</p>",opts:["Flat slab with drop panels","Flat plate","Waffle (two-way ribbed) slab","One-way slab on beams"],c:[1],e:"<p>AGS's concrete floor system comparison lists the flat plate's advantages as inexpensive formwork, ceilings that may be exposed, minimum thickness, fast erection, and flexible column location. Its appropriate building types are hotels, motels, dormitories, condominiums, and hospitals. Its typical span range of 18–35 ft (more with post-tensioning) covers a 24–26 ft bay. AGS calls it \"the most economical floor system\" for moderate spans, with \"the lowest structural thickness.\"</p><ul><li>A (Flat slab): economical when design loads are greater than 150 psf (warehouses, industrial, parking). The drop panels add depth and the formwork is costly.</li><li>C (Waffle slab): gives longer two-way spans and an attractive exposed ceiling, but the formwork costs more and it uses more concrete and steel. It is overkill for 24–26 ft residential bays and makes the floor-to-floor taller.</li><li>D (One-way slab on beams): the beams interfere with mechanical services and add depth. It is favored for parking garages.</li></ul><p>Coordination note: AGS warns to avoid penetrations for piping and ductwork through a flat plate near the columns.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.52 (Tables 2.13 and 2.14, Concrete Floor System Comparison); PDF p.49 (floor construction notes)</p>"},
{id:"q84",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 3.4",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Concrete Durability for an Exposed Parking Deck</p><p>A municipal parking deck in a northern city will be exposed to wet-dry cycling, repeated freezing and thawing, and de-icing salts. The architect is reviewing the concrete performance requirements in the structural specifications.</p><p>Check the THREE requirements that will most directly improve the deck's durability.</p>",opts:["Specify the lowest water-cement ratio that meets the strength and durability requirements.","Specify air-entrained concrete.","Require adequate curing that keeps the concrete continuously moist after placement.","Allow the contractor to add water at the site on hot days to restore the specified slump.","Raise the water-cement ratio to about 0.80. Aggregates, not the paste, control durability, so the extra water only improves workability and economy.","Require curing temperatures above 90°F so the concrete reaches design strength faster."],c:[0,1,2],e:"<p>Olin's states that concrete exposed to wet-dry and freeze-thaw cycling and de-icing chemicals needs (1) a low water-cement ratio, (2) air-entrainment, (3) suitable materials, (4) adequate curing, and (5) good construction practices. The w/c ratio selected \"should be the lowest value required to meet design requirements.\" Strength keeps increasing as long as moisture is present to promote hydration, so concrete should be kept constantly moist after placing.</p><ul><li>D: \"Adding water to maintain a desirable slump decreases the concrete's strength and adversely affects its other properties.\"</li><li>E: \"If there is a high water-cement ratio... a portland cement paste becomes thin or diluted and will be weak and porous when it hardens.\" Aggregates are inert; the paste is the binder.</li><li>F: \"The final strength of concrete cured at temperatures above 90°F (32°C) is lower...\"</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Olin's Construction Principles, Materials, and Methods, PDF p.159 (Section 3.7.1 Water-Cement Ratio); PDF p.161 (strength and curing temperature)</p>"},
{id:"q85",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 3.3",t:"num",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">U-Factor of a Wood-Frame Wall Assembly</p><p>During schematic design of a small clinic, the architect checks the thermal performance of the proposed exterior wall. Take the section through the insulated cavity only (ignore framing). Layers, from inside to outside:</p><pre class=\"qtab\">Component                                   R (ft²·°F·h/Btu)\nInside air film (still air)                 0.68\n1/2 in. gypsum board                        0.45\nPlastic film vapor retarder                 nil\n6 in. glass fiber batt insulation           19.00\n1/2 in. plywood sheathing                   0.62\n1 in. wood siding                           0.79\nOutside air film (15 mph wind)              0.17</pre><p>What is the U-factor of this assembly? Enter your answer in Btu/h·ft²·°F, rounded to three decimal places.</p>",ans:0.046,tol:0.0005,unit:"Btu/h·ft²·°F",e:"<p>Calculation:<br>Step 1 — R-values in series add: R_total = 0.68 + 0.45 + 0 + 19.00 + 0.62 + 0.79 + 0.17 = 21.71<br>Step 2 — U = 1 / R_total = 1 / 21.71 = 0.0461 ≈ 0.046 Btu/h·ft²·°F</p><p>Common errors:</p><ul><li>Adding the U-values of the layers instead of the R-values. Only resistances in series add; U is the reciprocal of the total R.</li><li>Leaving out the air films (gives 1/20.86 = 0.048). The films are part of the air-to-air U-factor.</li><li>Using the batt alone (1/19 = 0.053).</li></ul><p>Follow-up concept: If the 12% of wall area occupied by studs, plates, and sills is included (a lower-R path through the framing), the area-weighted U-factor rises slightly. MEEB's Example 7.2 goes from U = 0.045 ignoring framing to 0.048 with framing. With metal studs the thermal bridging penalty is much larger.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.211 (Example 7.1, U = 1/ΣR) and PDF p.212 (Example 7.2, thermal bridging); component R-values from those examples (Tables E.1, E.3)</p>"},
{id:"q86",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 3.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Glazing Selection for a Cooling-Dominated Office</p><p>A three-story office building in a hot climate has an open-plan floor with a long south and west curtain wall. Cooling is the dominant thermal load. The client also wants to daylight the open office and dim the electric lights.</p><p>Which glazing strategy best balances these goals?</p>",opts:["High-transmission low-e coating on the inner glazing, which gives a low U-factor with a high SHGC","Selective-transmission (spectrally selective) low-e coating on the outer glazing, which gives a low SHGC and a relatively high visible transmittance (high light-to-solar-gain ratio)","Low-transmission low-e glazing with a tinted exterior lite, which minimizes both SHGC and visible transmittance","Clear uncoated insulating glass, because clear glass has the highest visible transmittance"],c:[1],e:"<p>MEEB explains that a lower SHGC is better where cooling is the dominant thermal issue, and that spectrally selective glazings \"greatly reduce SHGC with little reduction in VT.\" The light-to-solar-gain ratio (LSG = VT ÷ SHGC) measures this. \"The greater the LSG, the more suitable a glazing is for daylighting in hot climates.\" A selective-transmission low-e coating on the outer glazing blocks incoming infrared, which is then convected away outdoors, while keeping VT relatively high for daylighting.</p><ul><li>A: High-transmission low-e is for passive solar heating. The coating sits on the inner glazing to trap outgoing infrared, and the SHGC is high, which is wrong for a cooling-dominated building.</li><li>C: Low-transmission low-e with tint suits cases \"where the sun is the enemy,\" but its low VT undermines the daylighting goal.</li><li>D: Clear glass has the highest VT but a high SHGC and a poor U-factor. It would drive up cooling loads.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.222 (SHGC, VT, LSG) and PDF p.223 (types of low-ε coatings)</p>"},
{id:"q87",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 4.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">HVAC System for a Deep-Plan Office</p><p>A 12-story speculative office building has deep floor plates, so a large share of each floor is interior zone with internal-load-dominated, nearly year-round cooling. The developer wants the shallowest practical ceiling plenum (to hold floor-to-floor height down), low energy use, and good comfort with well-filtered, humidity-controlled air. A central all-air system has already been chosen.</p><p>Which all-air system type best fits these goals?</p>",opts:["Dual-duct, constant-volume system","Single-duct system with terminal reheat","Single-duct variable-air-volume (VAV) system","Multizone system with a separate duct to each zone from the central station"],c:[2],e:"<p>MEEB calls single-duct VAV \"the most popular large-building system of recent years.\" Its single duct \"requires less building volume for distribution than do multiduct systems,\" and varying the airflow rather than the air temperature saves energy compared to single-duct with reheat. MEEB says it is \"clearly... well suited to serve the interior, always-hot zones of internal-load-dominated buildings.\" All-air systems overall are the best for comfort, because air is filtered, humidity-controlled, and freshened centrally.</p><ul><li>A (Dual-duct CV): better part-load comfort, but \"much more expensive to install, consumes much building volume for the two distribution trees, and usually consumes more energy than the single-duct VAV system that has largely replaced it.\"</li><li>B (Single-duct with reheat): small distribution tree, but where the central airstream must be cooled most of the time, energy is spent reheating it at most zones. These systems are \"notorious for energy wastage.\"</li><li>D (Multizone): \"the total distribution tree volume grows to astonishing size with only a few zones,\" which conflicts with a shallow plenum.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.415 (all-air systems, single-duct VAV); PDF p.416 (multizone, single-duct with reheat); PDF p.418 (dual-duct)</p>"},
{id:"q88",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 4.2",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Locating and Sizing the Central Mechanical Room</p><p>During schematic design of a four-story, 60,000 ft² community college classroom building, the architect is blocking out a central mechanical room that will house both the heating/cooling equipment and the air-handling equipment.</p><p>Check the THREE planning decisions that follow recommended practice.</p>",opts:["Place the mechanical room centrally relative to the area it serves.","Give the mechanical room direct access to the outdoors.","Plan about 12 ft of clear height as a typical minimum for a room with both heating/cooling and air-handling equipment.","Put the room at the far end of the building so the long duct runs help attenuate equipment noise.","Match the room's clear height to the 9-ft classroom ceilings so the floor-to-floor stays uniform.","Wait until construction documents to decide whether to integrate or separate the heating/cooling and air-handling equipment, so the engineer has complete load data."],c:[0,1,2],e:"<p>MEEB states that each mechanical room \"should have both a central location relative to the area it serves and direct access to the outside.\" A central location minimizes the size of the distribution tree. Outdoor access lets outdoor air serve as a heat source (winter) or sink (summer) and \"allows equipment to be installed or removed in later remodeling.\" Rooms serving both heating/cooling and air-handling equipment \"need relatively high ceilings; 12-ft (3.7-m) clear is a typical minimum, 20-ft (6-m) clear a typical maximum.\"</p><ul><li>D: A remote location enlarges the distribution tree (more duct and shaft space, more fan energy). That runs against the central-location guideline.</li><li>E: About 9 ft is well under the typical 12-ft minimum.</li><li>F: MEEB calls integrating vs. separating the equipment \"an important early design decision.\" It determines whether you need one central room or a plant room plus a fan room on each floor.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.420 (Section 10.2(e) Equipment Space Allocations)</p>"},
{id:"q89",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 4.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Domestic Water Distribution for a Mid-Rise Building</p><p>An eight-story apartment building has floor-to-floor heights of about 13 ft. The top-floor fixtures are roughly 104 ft above the street main. The water utility reports a main pressure of 55 psi. The top floor includes flush-valve water closets, which need about 20 psi to operate.</p><p>Which approach should the design team plan for in the core and mechanical spaces?</p>",opts:["A simple upfeed system using street pressure, since 55 psi is well above the 20 psi a flush valve needs","Larger risers and a larger meter, so that lower friction loss lets street pressure reach the top floor","Pressure boosting, such as pumped upfeed, a hydropneumatic tank, or a downfeed system pumped to a roof tank","Pressure-reducing valves on the lower floors, so more of the street pressure is left for the upper floors"],c:[2],e:"<p>Static head alone = 104 ft x 0.434 psi/ft ≈ 45 psi<br>Required at the main ignoring friction = 45 psi + 20 psi (flush valve) ≈ 65 psi, which is more than the 55 psi available.</p><p>Olin's notes that the practical upfeed limit, after pipe, fitting, and meter friction, is only about 40 ft for 50 psi service and 60 ft for 60 psi service. \"If the building is higher than the limits determined, a downfeed or combination system is required.\" MEEB lists the options for taller buildings: pumped upfeed, hydropneumatic tanks, and downfeed from roof storage tanks.</p><ul><li>A: ignores static head. Each foot of rise costs 0.434 psi.</li><li>B: friction is not the governing problem. Even with zero friction, static head plus fixture pressure is more than street pressure.</li><li>D: pressure-reducing valves lower pressure where they are installed. They cannot add pressure at the upper floors.</li></ul><p>Integration note: pumped and downfeed systems need space for pumps and, for downfeed, a roof tank with its structural load. These should be planned early.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Olin's Construction Principles, Materials, and Methods, PDF p.1488–1490 (Section 15.2.1.3 System Configuration); MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.996 (Section 21.9 Water Distribution)</p>"},
{id:"q90",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 4.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Electrical Service Voltage for a Large Office Building</p><p>A 20-story office building will be lit mainly with fluorescent luminaires and has large mechanical motor loads. The 120-V receptacle and equipment load is estimated at about one-quarter of the total building load. The owner asks the architect why the electrical engineer's proposed feeders, conduits, and switchgear are smaller than expected.</p><p>Which service system is the engineer most likely proposing?</p>",opts:["120/240-V, single-phase, 3-wire","120/208-V, 3-phase, 4-wire","277/480-V, 3-phase, 4-wire, with small dry-type transformers stepping down to 120 V","2400/4160-V, 3-phase, 4-wire"],c:[2],e:"<p>MEEB says the 277/480-V, 3-phase, 4-wire system suits large buildings \"where lighting is principally fluorescent and/or HID and the 120-V load does not exceed one-third of the total load.\" It provides 277 V for lighting and 480 V for machinery. Small (3- to 25-kVA) dry-type transformers step down to 120 V. It is \"ideally suited to multistory office buildings.\" Cost savings come from smaller feeder and conduit sizes and smaller switchgear, which more than offset the transformer cost. For example, a 15-kW 3-phase heater draws about 42 A at 208 V but only about 18 A at 480 V.</p><ul><li>A: the 120/240-V single-phase service comes from a center-tapped single-phase transformer and suits small buildings, not a 20-story office.</li><li>B: 120/208-V, 3-phase, 4-wire is widely used for \"all facilities except very large ones,\" where feeder lengths and loads require higher voltage.</li><li>D: 2400/4160 V is \"used only in very large commercial or industrial buildings with machinery requiring these voltages,\" and is costly to run within a building.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.1310 (120/208-V system); PDF p.1311 (277/480-V system); PDF p.1312 (2400/4160-V); PDF p.1309 (120/240-V single-phase)</p>"},
{id:"q91",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 5.2",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Matching Cost Estimate Methods to Design Phases</p><p>For a new 120-bed skilled nursing facility, the architect's agreement requires a cost estimate at the end of each phase. Match each estimating method to its description, typical accuracy, and the phase where it is typically used.</p>",items:["Total cost = 120 beds x historical cost per bed. The program is still being written.\"  (accuracy ~20–25%; Programming or early Schematic Design)","Construction type defined, gross and net areas known; cost per square foot or cubic foot applied.\"  (accuracy ~15–20%; Schematic Design)","Costs per unit of assembled construction (roof assembly, floor structure, exterior wall) organized by UniFormat.\"  (accuracy ~10%; Design Development)","Complete quantity takeoff of all materials, labor, and equipment, organized by MasterFormat.\"  (accuracy ~3–5%; Construction Documents)"],opts:["Order of Magnitude","Unit of Measure","Assemblies (Systems)","Unit Price"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p>PDPG Table 4.1 ranks the methods from quickest and least accurate to most effort and most accurate. Order of magnitude uses per-unit-of-occupancy costs (cost per bed, per seat). Unit of measure uses cost per unit of area or volume. Assemblies/systems requires DD-level information and can use UniFormat. Unit price uses the completed CDs and a full quantity survey, is commonly organized by MasterFormat, and is generally used to set the final design estimate. A common error is swapping the two middle methods. SF/CF pricing is the SD method; assemblies pricing needs DD-level information.</p><p>Remember: an estimate is an opinion of probable cost, not a price. A price comes from bids.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide 3e, PDF p.168–169 (Section 4.2.3.1 Cost Estimating; Table 4.1 Method of Cost Estimation)</p>"},
{id:"q92",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 5.3",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">What Belongs in a Life-Cycle Cost Comparison</p><p>A school district asks the architect to compare two classroom lighting systems on a life-cycle cost basis before choosing one. Both systems deliver the same design illuminance.</p><p>Check the FOUR items that should be included in the comparison.</p>",opts:["Investment (first) cost of each system, less any salvage value at the end of the analysis period","Maintenance and repair costs, such as relamping and cleaning","Operating costs, including energy and labor","Each system's effect on the HVAC system (cooling capacity and energy) and on the wiring system","First cost only, since both systems produce the same illuminance","An analysis period always equal to the building's full 60-year design life, regardless of the lighting system's service life"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p>MEEB defines life-cycle cost as \"the sum of the first cost and all future costs, less salvage\": LCC = IC + MC + AC + OC. Here IC is investment cost (first cost minus salvage), MC is maintenance and repair, AC is amortization (replacement) cost, and OC is operating costs, including labor and energy. Costs are usually expressed in present value. MEEB adds: \"in comparing lighting systems, the costs must reflect the impact of lighting on the HVAC system (capacity and energy use) and the wiring system.\" PDPG's list of life-cycle costs is similar: design, construction, operation, maintenance, and resale/demolition/salvage.</p><ul><li>E: First-cost comparisons are \"legitimate only when operating/replacement costs are nominal or hold no concern for an owner.\" That is not true for a school district that will operate the building for decades.</li><li>F: A lighting system may last 15–20 years while the building lasts at least twice that. MEEB notes \"it is often wiser to conduct the analysis over the shorter time period,\" since technology will have changed by the time replacement comes.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.1723–1724 (Appendix I.2 Life-Cycle Cost, Eq. I.1); CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide 3e, PDF p.170 (Section 4.2.3.2)</p>"},
{id:"q93",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 5.3",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Timing of Value Analysis</p><p>A university's new research building is projected to come in over budget. The facilities director suggests waiting until bids are opened and then holding a value analysis (value engineering) session with the low bidder, \"so we're working with real prices.\"</p><p>What is the architect's best advice?</p>",opts:["Agree. Value analysis is most effective once real bid prices are available.","Recommend a multidisciplinary value analysis workshop now, during design. The earlier it is done, the more efficient and effective it is, and the later it occurs, the more disruptive it is and the less value is retained.","Recommend skipping value analysis and simply cutting the building's area, since reducing size never affects the project's functional value.","Recommend deferring value analysis to construction, when field conditions show which systems can be simplified."],c:[1],e:"<p>PDPG: \"Timing is also an important factor in value analysis processes. The earlier it is implemented in the design process, the more efficient and effective it can be. The later it occurs in the project, the more disruptive it can be to design and scheduling, resulting in less value retained in the project.\" The most effective approach is a multidisciplinary team of stakeholders and participants. When value analysis happens during design, it affects fewer related decisions. During construction, \"scheduling and project costs are at greater risk.\"</p><ul><li>A: Simple changes can still be made at the pricing stage, but complex changes may force redesign and extend the schedule.</li><li>C: PDPG notes \"size matters.\" Because size is directly related to the project's functional attributes, owners are usually reluctant to cut it, and value analysis used only to cut construction cost can reduce life-cycle value.</li><li>D: This puts schedule and cost at the greatest risk.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide 3e, PDF p.172 (value analysis timing, multidisciplinary team); PDF p.173 (value analysis during design vs. pricing vs. construction)</p>"},
{id:"q94",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 5.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Contingency at Project Conception</p><p>A private nonprofit is in the project conception stage of a new community center. There is no written program yet, the site has not been fully investigated, and construction is about three years away. The owner's advisor is putting together the first project budget and asks what contingency to carry.</p><p>Per the PDPG, which contingency is most appropriate at this stage?</p>",opts:["0%, because the contractor will carry its own contingency in its price","3–10%, because the budget only needs to cover minor scope changes","About 15%, because a site investigation will be done before design starts","As high as 20%, because unknowns are greatest before programming and site investigation"],c:[3],e:"<p>PDPG: \"The contingency factor can be as high as 20 percent during project conception, 15 percent during programming, and 3 to 10 percent during construction.\" Contingencies cover the unquantifiable effects of unknowns: funding, weather, labor and material shortages, regulatory restrictions, geological conditions, and possible hazardous materials. These are greatest before programming and site investigation. More thorough preparation during conception and programming means less contingency is needed later. The budget should also include an escalation factor for the time to complete programming, design, procurement, construction, and commissioning, which matters here with construction about three years out.</p><ul><li>A: The contractor's contingency is part of the construction cost (a hard cost). The owner still needs a project budget contingency for unknowns outside the construction contract and for design not yet developed.</li><li>B and C are the levels for later phases (3–10% during construction, 15% during programming).</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide 3e, PDF p.88 (Section 2.2.7.7 Contingencies, escalation); PDF p.89 (contingency percentages by phase); PDF p.80 (contractor's contingency listed as a hard cost)</p>"},
{id:"q95",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 3.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Roof Assembly for an Occupied Rooftop Plaza</p><p>A mixed-use building in a climate with severe winters and hot summers will have a landscaped rooftop plaza over the top floor of offices. The plaza will get heavy foot traffic and periodic maintenance equipment. The owner's main concern is a long-lasting, leak-free membrane.</p><p>Which roof assembly is most appropriate?</p>",opts:["A conventional compact assembly with the membrane on top of polyisocyanurate insulation, finished with a walkable coating","A protected membrane roof (inverted roof / IRMA) with the membrane on the deck, extruded polystyrene insulation above it, then a pervious fabric and ballast or pavers","A protected membrane roof using expanded polystyrene or polyisocyanurate boards above the membrane, which save money compared to extruded polystyrene","An insulated framed system with stack vents every 900 ft² to let moisture escape from the insulation"],c:[1],e:"<p>In a typical roof the membrane is applied over the insulation and is \"exposed to temperature extremes, as well as wear and tear from people walking or working on the roof.\" In a protected membrane roof, extruded polystyrene insulation protects the membrane. AGS says this system \"is best used in extreme climates... or where the rooftop will receive heavy use (e.g., plaza or parking deck applications).\" Olin's gives the order: ballast, pervious fabric, insulation, membrane, underlayment, deck. The membrane is on the warm side and acts as the vapor retarder, and ballast holds the insulation in place.</p><ul><li>A: leaves the membrane exposed to thermal cycling and traffic, which is exactly what the owner wants to avoid.</li><li>C: \"Extruded polystyrene is the only material generally approved for this application because it does not absorb moisture.\" Insulation above the membrane sits in water.</li><li>D: Olin's warns that vents \"can produce more problems than they solve.\" They can admit water vapor and drifting snow, leak at the flashings, and act as chimneys for moist air.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.188 (Protected Membrane Roofing); Olin's Construction Principles, Materials, and Methods, PDF p.698 (protected membrane systems, venting) and PDF p.840 (Section 7.8.2 Roof Configurations)</p>"},
{id:"q96",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 3.3",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Movement Joints in Brick Veneer and CMU Backup</p><p>An L-shaped, three-story middle school has clay brick veneer over concrete masonry unit (CMU) backup walls. Each wing is more than 120 ft long. The architect is laying out movement joints and specifying masonry to limit cracking.</p><p>Check the THREE measures that are appropriate.</p>",opts:["Provide expansion joints in the brick veneer at the junction of the L-shaped wings and at offsets where the wall expanding into the offset is 50 ft or more long.","Provide control joints in the CMU backup to accommodate drying shrinkage.","Specify moisture-controlled concrete masonry units and horizontal joint reinforcement in the CMU.","Use control joints instead of expansion joints in the brick veneer, because drying shrinkage is the main movement in clay brick.","Omit movement joints at steel lintels and window frames, because metal and masonry expand at about the same rate.","Add expansion joints at very close spacing (every 10 ft), because they are inexpensive and maintenance-free."],c:[0,1,2],e:"<p>Clay and concrete masonry move in opposite ways, so they need different joints.</p><ul><li>Clay brick expands. An unrestrained 100-ft clay masonry wall grows about 3/8 in. with a 100°F temperature rise. Olin's: expansion joints \"should be located at offsets when the wall expanding into the offset is 50 ft (15.24 m) or more in length and at junctions of walls in L-, T-, and U-shaped buildings.\"</li><li>CMU shrinks. \"Most initial movement in a concrete masonry wall is due to contraction from drying shrinkage.\" Cracking is controlled by moisture-controlled units, horizontal steel reinforcement, and control joints.</li><li>D: \"Because shrinkage is minimal in brick masonry, thermal movement is critical and expansion joints must be provided.\"</li><li>E: Metal lintels, windows, and door frames \"have substantially greater coefficients of expansion than does masonry,\" which causes large differential movement.</li><li>F: \"Expansion joints are expensive, may be difficult to maintain, and should be avoided when it is possible to do so without having the walls crack.\"</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Olin's Construction Principles, Materials, and Methods, PDF p.314–315 (Section 4.6.4 Crack Control); PDF p.1148 (brick vs. cementitious movement)</p>"},
{id:"q97",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 3.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Differential Shrinkage in a Wood-Framed Floor</p><p>On a prior two-story wood-framed office project, the owner had cracked finishes and sticking doors near the center of the building. Floor joists bear on a sill plate at the foundation walls and on a built-up wood girder at mid-span. The architect is reviewing the same framing approach for the owner's new building.</p><p>Which design response best addresses the cause?</p>",opts:["Specify longer studs, because most wood shrinkage happens along the length of the member","Balance the total depth of cross-grain wood at the center support with that at the exterior walls, or use a steel beam as the center support","Specify green (S-GRN) lumber for the girder, so that it shrinks in place before finishes go in","Increase the girder depth with more plies of 2x lumber, since a deeper member shrinks less overall"],c:[1],e:"<p>Wood shrinks very little along the grain and a lot across it. Olin's: shrinkage parallel to the grain \"is practically negligible.\" Across the grain, wood shrinks appreciably and most in the tangential direction. Drying to oven-dry, wood shrinks about 0.1% longitudinally, 2% radially, and 4% tangentially. Sills, plates, joists, and girders are all cross-grain in the vertical direction, so if the center support has much more cross-grain wood than the exterior bearing, the floor settles unevenly. Olin's: \"By using approximately the same total depth of cross-grain wood at the center girder as at the exterior wall, differential shrinkage can be minimized... To avoid uneven shrinkage, a steel beam center support generally is preferred. If a wood girder is used, MC 15 lumber should be specified.\"</p><ul><li>A: longitudinal shrinkage is negligible. The problem is cross-grain shrinkage.</li><li>C: green lumber makes the shrinkage worse. Olin's calls for MC 15 lumber in a wood girder.</li><li>D: more cross-grain depth at the center adds to the imbalance.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Olin's Construction Principles, Materials, and Methods, PDF p.512 (effects of moisture content on wood); PDF p.587 (equalizing shrinkage in floor framing); PDF p.681 (dimensional instability percentages)</p>"},
{id:"q98",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 3.3",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">HID Lighting in a Crowded Assembly Space</p><p>A county is renovating an indoor exhibition hall that holds large crowds. To save energy, the lighting designer proposes high-pressure sodium (HPS) high-bay luminaires for general lighting. The building committee asks what happens if the power flickers off during an event.</p><p>Which statement correctly guides the design?</p>",opts:["Standby HPS lamps with a second arc tube count as the code-required emergency lighting, so no other emergency lighting is needed.","HPS lamps can take a minute or more to restrike after going out, so the hall must also have instant-on emergency lighting.","Switch to low-pressure sodium lamps. They have the highest efficacy and restrike instantly, so emergency lighting is unnecessary.","Instant-restrike ballasts make HPS lamps immune to power loss, so emergency lighting can be omitted."],c:[1],e:"<p>MEEB: \"HPS lamps require a minute or more to restrike after being extinguished. A crowded public area plunged into complete darkness is a recipe for disaster; therefore, such areas must always be furnished with instant-on emergency lighting.\" Light output on restrike drops sharply with the length of the outage. After a 2-minute outage, output is only 10%, and full output takes about another minute.</p><ul><li>A: standby lamps \"may be acceptable to some code authorities as a substitute for instant-on replacement lighting following a momentary power outage. It is not acceptable as emergency lighting as required by NFPA 101: Life Safety Code.\"</li><li>C: low-pressure sodium has very high efficacy (more than 150 lm/W) but a CRI of 0 and monochromatic yellow light, \"making it inapplicable for general lighting.\" It is used for streets, parking lots, and pathways.</li><li>D: instant-restrike ballasts help only once voltage returns. They do nothing during an actual outage.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.580 (HPS operating characteristics, restrike); PDF p.581 (standby lamps, NFPA 101; low-pressure sodium)</p>"},
{id:"q99",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 4.4",t:"num",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Design Heat Loss Through the Envelope</p><p>To help the mechanical engineer size the heating plant early, the architect estimates the design conduction heat loss of a one-story branch library. Design conditions: 70°F inside, 4°F outside.</p><pre class=\"qtab\">Envelope component          Area (ft²)    U-factor (Btu/h·ft²·°F)\nOpaque walls                2,400         0.046\nWindows (NFRC rated)          600         0.35\nRoof                        5,000         0.032</pre><p>Ignore infiltration and the slab-on-grade. What is the total design conduction heat loss? Enter your answer in Btu/h, rounded to the nearest whole number.</p>",ans:31706,tol:0.5,unit:"Btu/h",e:"<p>Calculation:<br>Formula: q = Σ(U x A) x Δt</p><p>Step 1 — U x A for each component:</p><pre class=\"qtab\">Walls:   0.046 x 2,400 = 110.4 Btu/h·°F\nWindows: 0.35  x   600 = 210.0 Btu/h·°F\nRoof:    0.032 x 5,000 = 160.0 Btu/h·°F\nΣUA = 480.4 Btu/h·°F</pre><p>Step 2 — Δt = 70°F − 4°F = 66°F<br>Step 3 — q = 480.4 x 66 = 31,706.4 ≈ 31,706 Btu/h</p><p>Design insight: The windows are only 600 ft² (7.5% of the 8,000 ft² envelope area) but account for about 44% of the conduction loss (210.0 of 480.4). MEEB notes that windows and skylights \"still usually have the lowest R (highest U) of all components of an envelope.\" Window area and U-factor are therefore the strongest early-design levers for the heating plant size.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.249 (whole-building heat loss: list U and A for each component and multiply U x A); PDF p.217 (Example: design heat loss = UA x Δt with 70°F inside and 4°F outside); PDF p.221 (windows have the highest U)</p>"},
{id:"q100",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 4.2",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Allocating HVAC Space in Early Planning</p><p>A university is programming a new 75,000 ft² (gross) laboratory building. Before an engineer is hired, the architect needs a placeholder for HVAC equipment space (central equipment and fans) in the stacking diagram.</p><p>Check the THREE statements that are consistent with AGS rules of thumb.</p>",opts:["About 10% of the gross floor area, roughly 7,500 ft², is a reasonable placeholder for HVAC space in a laboratory building of this size.","As a percentage of gross floor area, HVAC space tends to decrease as building size increases.","HVAC space requirements tend to increase with higher load density and system complexity.","A 10,000 ft² residential building needs a larger percentage of its area for HVAC than a 10,000 ft² laboratory.","These percentages cover only air-handling fans. Chillers, boilers, and pumps are budgeted separately."],c:[0,1,2],e:"<p>AGS Table 4.54 gives HVAC system space as a percentage of gross building floor area:</p><pre class=\"qtab\">Gross floor area   Residential   Institutional   Assembly   Laboratory\n10,000 ft²             6%             8%            9%         11%\n50,000–100,000 ft²     4%             6%            7%         10%\n500,000 ft²            3%             4%            5%          8%</pre><p>For 75,000 ft² of lab: 0.10 x 75,000 = 7,500 ft². The table note says the values include space for central equipment (chillers, boilers, pumps) and air circulation equipment (fans). HVAC space \"tend[s] to increase with increased load density and complexity, whereas the percentage space requirements tend to decrease with increased building size.\"</p><ul><li>D: it is the reverse. Residential needs 6% and laboratory 11% at 10,000 ft².</li><li>E: the percentages explicitly include central equipment.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.262 (Table 4.54, HVAC System Space Requirements as a Percentage of Gross Building Floor Area, and note)</p>"},
{id:"q101",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 4.2",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Matching HVAC Systems to Their Classification</p><p>A design team is weighing HVAC options for different parts of a mixed-use project. Match each system description to its HVAC classification.</p>",items:["Office interior zones: one central supply duct with terminal boxes above the ceiling that vary the airflow to each zone in response to its thermostat.","Perimeter offices: fan-coil units under the windows heat or cool room air, and a separate, centrally conditioned stream of tempered ventilation air is ducted to each space.","Hotel guestrooms: four-pipe fan-coil units with chilled and hot water piped to each room, and ventilation from operable windows. There is no central ventilation air.","A small, perimeter-dominated retail pavilion: a self-contained heating and cooling unit at each space with almost no distribution tree."],opts:["All-Air","Air-and-Water","All-Water","Direct Refrigerant"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p>MEEB groups large-building HVAC into four classes by the medium carrying heat between the central station and the zones. All-air systems have the largest distribution trees. Air-and-water systems use a thin water tree for most of the heating and cooling plus a small central airstream equal to the required fresh air, and their total space is \"almost always less than that required by all-air systems.\" All-water systems have the smallest trees, with \"less thorough provision of outdoor air\" a potential concern. Air is handled so locally that there is little mixing between zones, but maintenance is high (filters and drain pans in each unit). Direct refrigerant systems \"nearly eliminate the distribution trees\" and are common in skin-load-dominated (usually smaller) buildings.<br>Common error: a fan-coil unit can be either air-and-water or all-water. What decides it is whether centrally conditioned ventilation air is delivered (B) or not (C).</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.405 (distribution tree classes); PDF p.415 (four system classifications; direct refrigerant; all-air VAV); PDF p.419 (air-and-water; all-water); PDF p.420 (Fig. 10.14 note on outdoor air)</p>"},
{id:"q102",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 4.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Weighted HVAC Selection Matrix</p><p>Case: A city is converting a historic two-story masonry building into a branch library. Floor-to-floor heights are fixed and plenum space is tight, and quiet reading rooms are a priority. The team uses a project-specific weighted selection matrix to compare three HVAC options. Each system is rated 1–10 on each criterion (10 = best).</p><pre class=\"qtab\">Criterion              Weight   System 1   System 2   System 3\nFirst cost                8         4          9         10\nSpace requirements       10         9          6          5\nEnergy use                7         6          8          5\nNoise                     9         9          6          4</pre><p>Which system scores highest in the weighted evaluation, and with what score?</p>",opts:["System 2, 29 points","System 2, 242 points","System 1, 245 points","System 3, 201 points"],c:[2],e:"<p>Calculation (weighted score = Σ weight x rating):<br>System 1: (8x4) + (10x9) + (7x6) + (9x9) = 32 + 90 + 42 + 81 = 245<br>System 2: (8x9) + (10x6) + (7x8) + (9x6) = 72 + 60 + 56 + 54 = 242<br>System 3: (8x10) + (10x5) + (7x5) + (9x4) = 80 + 50 + 35 + 36 = 201</p><p>Why the distractors are wrong:</p><ul><li>A: 29 is System 2's unweighted rating total (9+6+8+6). Unweighted, System 2 \"wins\" (29 vs. 28), but that ignores the owner's priorities on space and noise.</li><li>B: 242 is System 2's correct weighted score, but it is 3 points short of System 1.</li><li>D: System 3 has the best first cost but the lowest overall score.</li></ul><p>AGS recommends a project-specific weighted selection matrix because \"several system types would function adequately\" in most facilities. The criteria include life-cycle cost, energy consumption, space and volume requirements, noise, flexibility, maintainability, and comfort, and their relative importance \"will vary from project to project.\" AGS also stresses that the earlier a system is selected, \"the greater the opportunity for system coordination,\" and that zoning depends on building layout and should be set during schematic design.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.261 (System Selection; Table 4.51 Sample System Selection Matrix; Zoning)</p>"},
{id:"q103",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 5.3",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Identifying Soft Costs in a Project Budget</p><p>A private hospital system is assembling the project budget for a new outpatient clinic. Its finance office asks the architect to help sort the budget lines into hard costs and soft costs.</p><p>Check the FOUR items that are soft costs.</p>",opts:["Geotechnical report and site survey","Architectural and engineering fees","Demolition of an existing structure on the site","Extending water, sewer, and electric utilities to the site","Movable furniture, fixtures, and equipment (FF&E)","The contractor's bonds and contractor-obtained insurance","Costs of relocating staff and operations to the new facility","The purchase price of the land"],c:[0,1,4,6],e:"<p>PDPG: hard costs are \"the tangible components of the completed project.\" They include extending utilities to the site, demolition, construction costs (materials, labor, tools and equipment, bonds, contractor-obtained insurance, contractor's contingency, and contractor profit and overhead), and permanent fixtures and equipment. Soft costs are \"the non-tangible components and any other cost related to the project.\" They include due diligence (geotechnical reports, site surveys), professional service fees, jurisdictional fees, financing costs, legal fees, site acquisition fees, relocation costs, temporary facilities, lease termination, remediation and hazardous material abatement, testing and inspection, advertising, owner-obtained insurance, FF&amp;E (movable items), utility connection fees, and owner's contingency.</p><ul><li>H: \"The site purchase price is frequently considered a separate cost and is neither a hard cost nor a soft cost. However, the fees and other costs associated with acquiring the site are considered soft costs.\"</li></ul><p>Watch the pairs: permanent fixtures are hard but movable FF&amp;E is soft, and contractor-obtained insurance is hard but owner-obtained insurance is soft.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide 3e, PDF p.80–81 (Section 2.2.7.3 Project Budget, hard and soft costs)</p>"},
{id:"q104",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 5.3",t:"num",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Net Present Value of Energy Savings</p><p>During design development, the engineer proposes an upgraded building automation and controls package for a county office building. Data:</p><ul><li>Added first cost: $24,000</li><li>Estimated annual energy savings: $4,500 (constant, no escalation)</li><li>Analysis period: 10 years</li><li>Owner's discount rate: 8%</li><li>Present value factor for a uniform annual series (n = 10 years, i = 8%) from MEEB Table I.1a: 6.71</li></ul><p>What are the net life-cycle savings of the upgrade, in present-value dollars? Enter a whole number with no commas or dollar sign.</p>",ans:6195,tol:0.5,unit:"dollars",e:"<p>Calculation:<br>Step 1 — Present value of lifetime savings = annual savings x PV factor = $4,500 x 6.71 = $30,195<br>Step 2 — Net savings = life-cycle savings − life-cycle cost = $30,195 − $24,000 = $6,195</p><p>Common errors:</p><ul><li>$21,000: undiscounted savings ($4,500 x 10 = $45,000 − $24,000). This overstates the benefit because it ignores the time value of money.</li><li>5.33 years: the simple payback ($24,000 ÷ $4,500). It is a different metric and does not answer the question.</li></ul><p>MEEB measures cost-effectiveness as net lifetime savings (life-cycle savings minus life-cycle cost), with all figures in present value. Table I.1a gives the present value of n equal future payments at a given discount rate. Since the net present savings are positive, the upgrade is cost-effective at the owner's 8% discount rate.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.1724 (present value, Eq. I.2); PDF p.1725 (Section I.4 Cost-Effectiveness Comparison, net savings); PDF p.1726 (Table I.1a, n = 10, 8% = 6.71)</p>"},
{id:"q105",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 5.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Value Analysis Workshop Phases</p><p>A public school district holds a SAVE-style value analysis workshop at the end of schematic design. The architect presents the background and the decisions that shaped the design, and the team brainstorms dozens of alternative systems and materials. Next, the team scores those ideas against agreed criteria, throws out the weak ones, and keeps the ideas with the greatest potential for cost savings and value improvement.</p><p>Which workshop phase is the team in when it scores and screens the ideas?</p>",opts:["Information phase","Speculation (creative) phase","Evaluation (analysis) phase","Development phase"],c:[2],e:"<p>PDPG describes five workshop phases:<br>1. Information: the architect presents the background and the decisions that shaped the design. Key functional issues are analyzed, and the owner's objectives, criteria, and definition of value are set.<br>2. Speculation (creative): generate as many ideas as possible for lower initial cost or better life-cycle value.<br>3. Evaluation (analysis): once the criteria are defined, evaluate the ideas. \"Unworthy ideas are discarded and worthy ideas that represent the greatest potential for cost savings and value improvement are developed further.\"<br>4. Development: expand the selected ideas into workable solutions, weigh advantages and disadvantages, and determine and compare costs with the original program.<br>5. Presentation: prepare a written report with recommendations and costs.</p><ul><li>A and B happen before screening.</li><li>D comes after screening. It turns the surviving ideas into workable, priced solutions.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide 3e, PDF p.172 (value analysis workshop goals) and PDF p.173 (five workshop phases)</p>"},
{id:"q106",d:"PPD",o:"PPD 5.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Comparing Energy Investments: Payback vs. Net Savings</p><p>An owner is comparing two energy-saving proposals for a new office building, both evaluated at the same discount rate:</p><ul><li>Proposal X costs less to install, has a shorter simple payback period, and produces savings for 8 years.</li><li>Proposal Y costs more, has a slightly longer simple payback period, and produces savings for 15 years.</li></ul><p>The owner's facilities manager says, \"Pick X. The shortest payback is always the best investment.\"</p><p>What is the architect's best response?</p>",opts:["Agree. Simple payback is the standard measure for comparing alternative investments.","Choose the proposal with the higher initial (simple) rate of return, since it always matches the actual internal rate of return.","Compare net lifetime savings (present value of life-cycle savings minus life-cycle cost) for each proposal. A shorter payback does not necessarily mean a more cost-effective investment.","Ignore discounting, since annual savings are roughly constant, and pick the proposal with the larger total undiscounted savings."],c:[2],e:"<p>MEEB stresses \"the very important fact that a shorter payback period does not necessarily indicate a more cost-effective investment.\" In its example, the proposal with the longer payback had the better return \"precisely because it yields good savings for a longer period.\" It concludes that initial rate of return and simple payback \"are useless in comparative studies.\" The right comparison is net savings = life-cycle savings − life-cycle cost, in present value. The discounted payback period is \"usually longer than\" the simple one.</p><ul><li>A: simple payback ignores both the time value of money and how long the savings last.</li><li>B: MEEB notes the actual rate of return \"differs from the simple rate (and is usually lower),\" and initial rate of return \"can readily give misleading results.\"</li><li>D: life-cycle costs are expressed in present value. Leaving out discounting overstates long-term savings.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.1724–1725 (Sections I.3–I.5: initial rate of return, cost-effectiveness comparison, IRR); PDF p.1726 (Section I.6 Payback Period)</p>"},
{id:"q107",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.5",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Weep Holes Above Through-Wall Flashing</p><p>You are reviewing a CD wall section for a brick veneer cavity wall over a steel stud backup. Through-wall flashing is shown at the base of the wall and at each steel lintel. The mason has asked to use cotton/glass-fiber rope wicks in the head joints instead of open head joints because the owner is worried about insects getting into the cavity.</p><p>Which note should you add to the drawings to accept the substitution?</p>",opts:["\"Wicks at 24 in. o.c. max., in the head joints of the course immediately above all flashing.\"","\"Wicks at 16 in. o.c. max., in the head joints of the course immediately above all flashing.\"","\"Wicks at 32 in. o.c. max., in the bed joint directly below the flashing.\"","\"Wicks at 16 in. o.c. max., in the head joints two courses above the flashing so mortar droppings do not block them.\""],c:[1],e:"<p>Weep holes belong in the head joint immediately above all flashing; concealed flashing without weeps acts as a trap that concentrates water. Open weep holes (open head joints, removed rods, tubes) are spaced at 24 in. Closed weep holes (wicks, glass-fiber insulation) do not drain as well, so they are spaced every 16 in.</p><ul><li>A → 24 in. is the spacing for OPEN weeps; wicks drain less well and must be closer.</li><li>C → Weeps go in head joints above the flashing, never below it (water below the flashing is already inside the wall). 32 in. is AGS's general upper limit for open head-joint weeps, not wicks.</li><li>D → Moving the weeps up two courses leaves water sitting on the flashing below the weep. Mortar droppings are kept off with care during construction, not by relocating the weep.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Olin's Construction Principles, Materials, and Methods, PDF p.362–363 (Sec. 4.7.2.11 Weep Holes); Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.398 (Masonry Cavity Drainage, Weepholes, and Vents)</p>"},
{id:"q108",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.5",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Embedded Masonry Flashing Details</p><p>A project architect sends you a draft of the masonry flashing notes for a school with a brick cavity wall. Check the FOUR notes that agree with recommended practice for embedded flashing.</p>",opts:["Discontinuous flashing (lintels, sills) shall have ends turned up at least 1 in. to form end dams.","Lap continuous embedded flashing at least 6 in. and seal laps with an appropriate sealer.","15-lb asphalt-saturated building felt may be used as through-wall flashing at concealed locations.","Extend flashing through the exterior wythe at least 1/4 in. to form a drip.","Stop the flashing 1/2 in. behind the face of brick so it is not visible.","Extend embedded flashing at least 8 in. vertically up the backup within the wall system."],c:[0,1,3,5],e:"<ul><li>A → End dams (ends turned up at least 1 in.) stop collected water from running off the ends of discontinuous flashing back into the wall, framing or mullions. Olin's gives the same 1 in. turn-up for sill flashing ends.</li><li>B → Continuous flashing laps of at least 6 in., sealed.</li><li>D → Extending through the face at least 1/4 in. forms a drip and reduces staining.</li><li>F → Embedded flashing should extend at least 8 in. vertically and at least 1-1/2 in. into the interior wythe.</li><li>C is wrong: AGS states asphalt-impregnated building paper (felt) is NOT an acceptable flashing material.</li><li>E is wrong: if the flashing is recessed and does not reach the surface, water collected on it can be channeled by the mortar under the flashing and back into the wall.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.398 (Masonry Embedded Flashing — Materials, Installation); Olin's, PDF p.362 (Flashing Locations)</p>"},
{id:"q109",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Vapor Retarder Location in a Cold Climate</p><p>An office building in a heating-dominated (cold) climate has a deep insulated exterior wall. The electrical engineer wants to run wiring and boxes along the inside face of the wall, and the contractor worries that the poly vapor retarder behind the gypsum board will be punctured many times.</p><p>Which design approach best addresses both concerns?</p>",opts:["Move the vapor retarder to the exterior face of the sheathing, behind the cladding, where it won't be punctured.","Put the vapor retarder within the insulation about one-third of the way from the interior, so the inner third can be used as a wiring chase while the insulation outboard of the retarder keeps it above the dew point.","Leave out the vapor retarder and use vinyl wallcovering on the interior as the only vapor control.","Use foil-faced batt insulation facing the stud cavity, because foil works as both a radiant barrier and a vapor retarder with no airspace needed."],c:[1],e:"<p>In cold climates the moist air is on the warm (interior) side, so the vapor retarder goes as close to the warm side as possible, before the dew point can be reached. With higher insulation R-values, MEEB notes it is becoming common to put the retarder within the insulation about one-third of the way from the interior to the exterior. The inner third becomes a chase for wiring and plumbing without penetrating the retarder, and there is still enough insulation outboard to keep the retarder's temperature above the dew point.</p><ul><li>A → A low-perm layer on the cold side traps vapor where it will condense. AGS's condensation analysis warns that cold-side materials with high vapor resistance are \"highly undesirable.\"</li><li>C → Vinyl wallpaper and vapor-retarder paints give less protection than a well-installed plastic film with sealed, wrapped edges.</li><li>D → Foil facing is a weaker vapor retarder than film, and it only works as a radiant barrier when it faces an air space.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.220 (Ch. 7 Heat Flow — cold-climate envelope condensation); Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.142 (Building Section Analysis for Potential Condensation); Olin's, PDF p.699 (7.1.5.3 Vapor Retarder Installation)</p>"},
{id:"q110",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.5",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Temperature Gradient Through a Wall</p><p>During DD you are checking a wall for condensation. From inside to outside, the assembly and R-values (ft²·°F·h/Btu) are:</p><pre class=\"qtab\">Inside air film ............................ 0.68\n1/2 in. gypsum board ....................... 0.45\nR-13 batt in 3-5/8 in. studs ............... 13.00\n1/2 in. exterior gypsum sheathing .......... 0.45\n1-1/2 in. continuous rigid insulation ...... 7.50\nOutside air film ........................... 0.17</pre><p>Indoor design temperature = 70°F; outdoor design temperature = 10°F.</p><p>What is the approximate temperature at the inside face of the exterior sheathing (the plane between the batt and the sheathing)?</p>",opts:["12.5°F","31.9°F","34.9°F","48.1°F"],c:[1],e:"<p>Calculation (MEEB method: the temperature drop across any layer is proportional to that layer's share of total resistance):<br>Total R = 0.68 + 0.45 + 13.00 + 0.45 + 7.50 + 0.17 = 22.25<br>Total ΔT = 70 − 10 = 60°F<br>Cumulative R from the interior to the plane in question = 0.68 + 0.45 + 13.00 = 14.13<br>Temperature drop to that plane = (14.13 / 22.25) × 60 = 38.1°F<br>Temperature at the plane = 70 − 38.1 = 31.9°F</p><p>Why the other answers are wrong:</p><ul><li>A (12.5°F) → This is the result if you leave out the continuous insulation (total R = 14.75; 70 − (14.13/14.75)(60) = 12.5°F). It shows why exterior continuous insulation warms the sheathing.</li><li>C (34.9°F) → Uses only the batt's resistance (13/22.25) and ignores the film and gypsum layers that come before it.</li><li>D (48.1°F) → Adds the drop to the OUTDOOR temperature (10 + 38.1) instead of subtracting it from the indoor temperature.</li></ul><p>Design note: Compare the result with the indoor air's dew point. If the sheathing plane is below the dew point, you need better vapor/air control on the warm side, or more of the insulation outboard of the sheathing.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.217 (Ch. 7, (g) Predicting Surface Temperatures and Condensation, Example 7.5)</p>"},
{id:"q111",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.5",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Sealant Joint Failure</p><p>One year after occupancy, the polyurethane sealant joints between precast panels are tearing down the middle. A field investigation finds the sealant filled each joint full depth and bonded to the precast on both sides and to the back of the joint. Bead depth is about equal to joint width.</p><p>Which correction is most appropriate for the replacement joints?</p>",opts:["Replace with an acrylic latex sealant, which is more flexible, and fill the joint full depth.","Install a correctly sized backer rod (or bond-breaker tape where depth is limited) to prevent three-sided adhesion, and tool the sealant to an hourglass profile with a depth about one-half its width.","Use a butyl sealant tooled flush with the face, because butyl needs no priming.","Keep the same joint profile but use a double line of sealant at the face so the outer bead protects the inner bead."],c:[1],e:"<p>AGS says the classic sealant profile is an hourglass with a depth one-half its width. Backer rod and bond-breaker tape prevent three-sided adhesion, which keeps the sealant from stretching freely. Bond-breaker tape is used where there isn't enough depth for a rod. Joint width should be conservatively sized at four times the anticipated movement.</p><ul><li>A → Acrylic/latex sealants have very low movement capability and limited weathering ability. They are generally not used on the exterior of commercial buildings.</li><li>C → Butyl has relatively low movement capability and cannot be exposed to daylight in the finished work. It is meant for lap joints in sheet metal and curtain wall.</li><li>D → A double line adds redundancy, but it does not fix the cause (three-sided adhesion and wrong shape). AGS also requires the space between the two lines to be weeped.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.133 (Joint Sealants — Sealant Types; Joint Design; Double Line Joint Sealants)</p>"},
{id:"q112",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.5",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Movement Joint Types</p><p>Match each joint type to the situation where it belongs:</p>",items:["A CMU wall 120 ft long needs to be divided into panels to relieve shrinkage and temperature cracking. The joint is a continuous, unobstructed opening through the wythe filled with an elastomeric material, sized to look like a mortar joint (3/8–1/2 in.).","An L-shaped building is split into two discrete structural sections so that stresses in one wing don't affect the other. The joint runs through the whole building, including the roof assembly.","A joint between a hollow metal window frame and the adjacent masonry absorbs the different movements of the two materials.","A cast-in-place slab placement is stopped at the end of the day; the joint is located where it will least impair the strength of the structure."],opts:["Control joint","Expansion (isolation) joint","Separation (isolation) joint between dissimilar materials","Construction (cold) joint"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p>AGS stresses that each type of movement joint does a specific job and that they \"should not be used interchangeably.\" Control joints divide masonry into segments to limit cracking from temperature, moisture and creep. Expansion joints divide the building into discrete structural sections and run through the roof. Separation joints sit between dissimilar materials. Construction joints are used mainly in concrete when work is interrupted. Control and expansion joints need not align in cavity walls, but they should align in multiwythe walls.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.399–400 (Masonry Movement Joints; Spacing of Masonry Control and Expansion Joints; Table 9.25)</p>"},
{id:"q113",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Lost Trap Seal</p><p>A client owns a weekend lake house. She calls to say there is a sewer-gas odor whenever she arrives after a few weeks away. The odor goes away soon after she runs water. The worst odor is near a floor drain in the mechanical room that is rarely used.</p><p>What is the most likely cause, and the best design response?</p>",opts:["The stack vent is undersized. Increase the vent stack diameter through the roof.","The trap seals are evaporating during long periods without use. Provide a deep-seal trap and/or a water source (drip or nearby hose bibb) to keep the floor drain trap filled.","Back-pressure from the municipal sewer is pushing gas through the traps. Install a house trap on every fixture branch.","The fixture traps are more than 2 ft from the fixtures. Relocate all traps directly under the fixture outlets."],c:[1],e:"<p>MEEB notes that unoccupied residences, such as weekend or vacation homes, can get sewer gas through traps emptied by evaporation. Floor drains in particular often lose their seal between uses. Remedies include a special hose bibb above the drain for manual refilling, a deep-trap drain, and a drip source of water to maintain the seal.</p><ul><li>A → Vents protect trap seals from siphonage and back-pressure while fixtures drain. An undersized vent would not explain an odor that shows up only after long absences and goes away once water runs.</li><li>C → A house trap is a single fitting on the building drain (optional or even illegal under some codes), not something placed on every branch. It does not refill evaporated traps.</li><li>D → Traps are usually placed within 2 ft of the fixture, but trap location is not why the seal is lost here.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.1028–1029 (Ch. 22 Liquid Waste — Principles of Drainage: traps; (b) Vents)</p>"},
{id:"q114",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.5",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Pressure-Equalized Rainscreen</p><p>The owner of a high-rise in a wind-driven-rain climate wants the brick veneer detailed as a pressure-equalized (PE) rainscreen rather than a simple drained cavity. Check the FOUR provisions that are required or recommended to make the PE strategy work.</p>",opts:["A continuous air barrier at the inner face of the cavity.","Vents in the outer layer so the cavity pressure can equalize with the exterior.","Compartments in the cavity about one story high by 15–20 ft wide, with narrower (about 5 ft) compartments at inside and outside corners.","A double line of sealant at every joint in the outer (rainscreen) layer.","Through-wall flashing and weeps to drain incidental water that enters the cavity.","A vapor-impermeable sheet on the exterior face of the brick to stop water at the outer surface."],c:[0,1,2,4],e:"<p>A PE rainscreen controls the kinetic and pressure forces that drive water across the outer joints by venting the cavity so the pressure difference across the rainscreen is equalized. AGS says an air barrier at the inner face of the cavity, venting, and compartmentalization are required, and the cavity is still flashed and weeped. Behind brick veneer, divide the air space into zones about one story high by 15–20 ft wide, with about 5 ft compartments at corners (where pressure changes are large) and extra venting at the top.</p><ul><li>D is wrong: AGS notes that joints in the outer layer of a drained cavity or PE rainscreen do NOT require a double line of sealant, as long as the inner weather barrier is properly sealed.</li><li>F is wrong: sealing the outer face makes the wall a face-sealed barrier wall, which is the opposite strategy and depends on a perfect seal.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.46 (Definitions — Pressure-equalized rainscreen wall), p.102 (Basic Exterior Wall Assembly Types), p.112 (Brick Veneer on Studs), p.133 (Double Line Joint Sealants)</p>"},
{id:"q115",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.5",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Steel Frame Connection Strategy</p><p>During DD for a four-story steel-framed office building, the structural engineer proposes rigid (moment-resisting) connections at every beam-to-column joint so the architect can avoid braced bays. The cost estimate comes back high, and the owner asks whether the framing approach could change.</p><p>Which response best reflects how steel frame connections affect cost?</p>",opts:["Keep moment connections throughout; bolted-only moment joints are the simplest and cheapest connection type.","Add diagonal bracing or shear panels in selected bays so most joints only need to resist shear and can be simple bolted connections, reducing the number of more expensive moment joints.","Use semirigid (Type 3) connections everywhere, since they need no lateral bracing and cost the same as shear connections.","Weld every connection in the field, which eliminates the shop drawing review for connections."],c:[1],e:"<p>Olin's describes AISC's three approaches: Type 1, rigid connections that resist all forces; Type 2, diagonal bracing or shear panels that resist the lateral/moment forces so most joints need resist only shear; and Type 3, semirigid joints. Shear-only joints are usually just bolted. Joints that must resist both moment and shear are welded or, more often, bolted and welded, and they cost more. So, especially on larger buildings, designers try to reduce the number of moment joints.</p><ul><li>A → Bolted-only moment joints are described as \"both complicated and expensive.\"</li><li>C → Semirigid joints still resist moment forces, so they are not equivalent to cheap shear connections.</li><li>D → Shop drawings showing joint details are always prepared by the fabricator and reviewed by the designer.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Olin's, PDF p.455–456 (Sec. 5.4.1.2 Conventional Steel Framing; Figs. 5.4-3, 5.4-4)</p>"},
{id:"q116",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 5.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Estimating Method at End of DD</p><p>The design development documents for a community library are complete. Before authorizing construction documents, the owner wants a cost estimate that prices the roof, floor structure, exterior walls and other building elements as complete systems, organized by building element rather than by trade.</p><p>Which estimating method is this, and what accuracy should the owner expect?</p>",opts:["Unit of measure (per SF of gross area); about 15–20 percent","Order of magnitude (per unit of occupancy); about 20–25 percent","Assemblies/systems; about 10 percent","Unit price (quantity takeoff); about 3–5 percent"],c:[2],e:"<p>An assemblies/systems estimate uses a cost per unit of assembled construction (for example, $/SF of roofing assembly or exterior wall assembly), with per-unit costs for items such as windows, doors and fixture groups. UniFormat can be used as the cost format. It needs the information produced in design development and is accurate to about 10 percent.</p><ul><li>A → Unit of measure ($/SF or $/CF of gross or net area) fits schematic design: 15–20 percent.</li><li>B → Order of magnitude (cost per bed, per seat) fits programming or early SD: 20–25 percent.</li><li>D → Unit price needs complete construction documents and a full quantity survey (\"takeoff\"), usually organized by MasterFormat: 3–5 percent.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide 3rd Ed., PDF p.168–169 (4.2.3.1 Cost Estimating; Table 4.1 Method of Cost Estimation)</p>"},
{id:"q117",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 5.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Schematic Design Estimate from Net Area</p><p>A developer's program calls for 36,000 SF of net leasable office space. For this building type, your firm assumes an efficiency (net ÷ gross) of 80 percent. Published cost data gives $260 per gross square foot for the construction type. Because the design is still broadly defined at SD, you carry a 10 percent design contingency.</p><p>What is the schematic design estimate of probable construction cost, including the contingency?</p>",opts:["10,296,000","11,700,000","12,355,200","12,870,000"],c:[3],e:"<p>Calculation:<br>Gross area = Net ÷ efficiency = 36,000 ÷ 0.80 = 45,000 GSF<br>Base cost = 45,000 GSF × $260/GSF = $11,700,000<br>With contingency = $11,700,000 × 1.10 = $12,870,000</p><p>Why the other answers are wrong:</p><ul><li>A ($10,296,000) → Applies the gross-area unit cost to the NET area (36,000 × 260 × 1.10).</li><li>B ($11,700,000) → Leaves out the contingency.</li><li>C ($12,355,200) → Adds 20 percent to net area (36,000 × 1.20) instead of dividing by 0.80. Dividing gives 45,000 GSF; multiplying by 1.20 gives only 43,200 GSF.</li></ul><p>A unit of measure (square foot/volume) estimate fits SD. Unit costs are set for gross area (the whole facility) and net area (the area for the primary use, such as leasable space), so you must use the matching area. PDPG notes that an SD estimate is broad and should include a substantial contingency for unknowns.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide 3rd Ed., PDF p.169 (Unit of measure), p.210 (4.5.3 Estimate of the Construction Cost)</p>"},
{id:"q118",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 5.1",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Alternates</p><p>A public school district must spend its full bond amount. The district asks your team to include several alternates in the bid documents, for example a brick exterior in place of fiber-cement siding. Check the THREE statements about alternates that are accurate.</p>",opts:["An alternate is a defined portion of the work priced separately. It can add or delete work, or choose between products.","If bids come in high, deductive alternates can be used to reduce the contract amount; if bids are low, additive alternates can be awarded.","Alternate prices are always true competitive market prices, because each bidder prices them in a competitive setting.","Alternates should be clearly delineated on the drawings, described in Section 01 23 00 – Alternates, and listed on the bid form, along with the criteria for awarding them.","Alternates are the right tool when the type of work is known but the quantity is not, such as the final depth of drilled piers.","A large number of alternates tells bidders that the design is fully resolved and the budget is secure."],c:[0,1,3],e:"<ul><li>C is wrong: if an alternate isn't fully documented, contractors add money to guard against loss, so alternate prices \"may not be truly competitive market prices.\"</li><li>E is wrong: unit prices are used where the type of work is defined but the extent is unknown (PDPG's example is drilled pier depth).</li><li>F is wrong: from the contractor's perspective, many alternates can indicate that the design is not fully determined or that the budget may not support the design. Over-reliance on alternates also makes it harder to determine the low bidder.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide 3rd Ed., PDF p.175–176 (Allowances, Alternates, Unit Prices)</p>"},
{id:"q119",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 5.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Life Cycle Cost vs. Value Analysis</p><p>For a new county courthouse, the design team is choosing among three HVAC systems. For each system they prepare the expected costs to design, build, operate, maintain and replace it over the owner's planned 50-year use. They then weigh those numbers alongside equipment size, performance and noise to recommend the system that best meets the essential functions at the lowest total cost.</p><p>Which term best describes the overall process the team is carrying out?</p>",opts:["Life cycle cost analysis","Value analysis","Order of magnitude estimating","Unit price estimating"],c:[1],e:"<p>PDPG distinguishes the two. Life cycle cost is the total cost of ownership over a long period (design, construction, operation, maintenance, and resale/demolition/salvage). Value analysis looks at the best value the owner will get by reviewing options. It uses life cycle costs as one input, along with other features such as size, performance and noise, to pick the system with the best value. PDPG's own example is a value analysis to choose the best mechanical system, with a life cycle cost prepared for each system.</p><ul><li>A → The LCC figures are only one input. Comparing them alongside size, performance and noise is value analysis.</li><li>C, D → These are construction cost estimating methods, not methods for evaluating alternatives.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide 3rd Ed., PDF p.169–171 (4.2.3.2 Life Cycle Costs and Value Analysis)</p>"},
{id:"q120",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.5",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Curtain Wall Anchorage and Movement</p><p>You are coordinating a stick-built aluminum curtain wall on a 10-story concrete-frame building. The head of the curtain wall at each level sits just below a slab edge, and the structural engineer expects slab-edge deflection and lateral drift.</p><p>Which anchorage and head detail is most appropriate?</p>",opts:["Fix every anchor at every floor for both gravity and wind, and caulk the head tight to the slab edge.","Make every other floor a gravity-and-wind anchor with a wind-only slip anchor between, and detail the head to take movement from above (typically about 1/2 in.).","Hang the entire curtain wall from the roof level so no floor anchors are needed.","Use gravity-only anchors at each floor; the mullions will resist wind as simple cantilevers."],c:[1],e:"<p>Curtain walls are typically anchored to each floor slab or spandrel beam. AGS describes \"every other floor is a gravity and wind anchor, with a wind-only slip anchor in between.\" Because the curtain wall spans slab to slab, it must handle differential movement of the spandrel beams, lateral drift, and aluminum's large thermal movement. Heads located at shelf angles or slab edges must accommodate movement typically in the range of 1/2 in. from above. (For comparison, unitized panels commonly have a wind and gravity anchor at each floor, with slip joints at the stack joints.)</p><ul><li>A → Fixing everything and caulking tight leaves no room for movement, so the frame or glass can be stressed and seals can fail.</li><li>C → Not a standard anchorage approach in AGS. Accumulated thermal movement over 10 stories would be large.</li><li>D → The curtain wall needs anchors that resist wind at the floors; the mullions span between anchors.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.163 (Curtain Wall — Installation Methods; Detailing: Anchoring, Movement)</p>"},
{id:"q121",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Preliminary Duct Sizing</p><p>During SD/DD you need to check whether the main supply duct for an office floor will fit above the ceiling. The duct carries 4,800 cfm, and the maximum recommended velocity for this duct is 1,200 fpm. The ceiling space is shallow, so the duct will be a thin rectangle with a width-to-depth ratio of about 5:1.</p><p>Using MEEB's approximation, what minimum cross-sectional duct area is required, in square inches?</p>",opts:["576 in²","605 in²","634 in²","720 in²"],c:[3],e:"<p>Calculation:<br>A (in²) = [volume of air (cfm) ÷ velocity (fpm)] × 144 in²/ft² × friction allowance<br>= (4,800 ÷ 1,200) × 144 × 1.25<br>= 4 ft² × 144 × 1.25<br>= 576 × 1.25 = 720 in²<br>Check fit: at a 5:1 ratio, depth d satisfies 5d × d = 720, so d² = 144 and d = 12 in. That is roughly a 60 in. × 12 in. duct (before insulation/liner).</p><p>Friction allowances (MEEB): round = 1.0; nearly square &lt;1,000 cfm = 1.10; nearly square &gt;1,000 cfm = 1.05; thin rectangular (1:5) = 1.25.</p><p>Why the other answers are wrong:</p><ul><li>A (576) → Leaves out the friction allowance (round-duct value of 1.0).</li><li>B (605) → Uses the large nearly-square allowance (1.05).</li><li>C (634) → Uses the small nearly-square allowance (1.10).</li></ul><p>Note: MEEB warns that the minimum duct area carries a penalty of increased noise and friction.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.451–452 (Sec. 10.4(a) Air Ducts — approximate duct sizing)</p>"},
{id:"q122",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.4",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Differential Wood Shrinkage</p><p>A two-story wood-frame house is on a foundation wall at the perimeter and a built-up wood girder at the center. A year after move-in, the owners report cracked drywall and sticking interior doors along the middle of the house. The exterior walls have no problems. The perimeter has about 12 in. of cross-grain wood (sill plate + joist depth). At the center, the joists bear on top of a deep built-up wood girder.</p><p>Which detail change would best have prevented this?</p>",opts:["Use deeper joists at the perimeter so the floor is stiffer.","Use about the same total depth of cross-grain wood at the center support as at the exterior wall, for example a steel beam center support or joists framed into the side of the girder, so shrinkage is equal.","Use plain-sawn lumber for the girder, since it shrinks less than vertical-grain lumber.","Space the joists at 24 in. o.c. instead of 16 in. o.c. to reduce the total amount of wood that can shrink."],c:[1],e:"<p>Wood shrinks very little along the grain but a lot across it. Olin's says it is important to equalize shrinkage at the exterior walls and at the center support by using about the same total depth of cross-grain wood in both places. If there are 12 in. of cross-grain wood at the foundation wall, match it at the center girder. A steel beam center support is generally preferred. With a wood girder, specify MC 15 lumber or support the joists so that cross-grain depth is balanced.</p><ul><li>A → Stiffness doesn't fix unequal shrinkage. Deeper perimeter joists would actually add cross-grain wood at the perimeter.</li><li>C → Plain-sawn boards are prone to seasoning distortion. Vertical-grain (quarter-sawn) lumber is used where pieces must stay flat. Neither changes the cross-grain imbalance.</li><li>D → Joist spacing doesn't change how much cross-grain depth there is at each support.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Olin's, PDF p.587 (wood floor framing — equalizing shrinkage); Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.423–424 (Moisture Movement in Wood)</p>"},
{id:"q123",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.3",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Fire Protection of Structural Steel</p><p>Match each project condition to the most appropriate fire protection method:</p>",items:["Exposed architecturally exposed structural steel (AESS) columns in a hotel atrium. The design team wants the steel profile to read crisply and to be painted.","Steel beams and girders concealed above a suspended acoustical ceiling in an office floor.","Exposed steel framing at a parking garage and loading dock, subject to impact, abuse and humidity.","The gap between a floor slab edge and the curtain wall spandrel panel."],opts:["Intumescent coating","Sprayed fire-resistive material (cementitious / mineral fiber, standard density)","High-density cementitious fireproofing","Safing insulation / firestopping"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p>Intumescent coatings are used mainly on steel exposed to view. They expand when heated into an insulating carbonaceous foam, and some can serve as a base coat for paint (Olin's). Sprayed cementitious and mineral-fiber products are used mostly in concealed locations. For exposed areas subject to contact, abuse, impact or humidity (parking garages, loading docks), AGS recommends denser, harder fire-resistive materials. Where curtain walls pass slab edges, the opening must be filled with firestopping/safing (mineral wool or slag-fiber safing insulation).</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Olin's, PDF p.885–887 (7.12.2.2 Spray-Applied Fireproofing; 7.12.2.3 Intumescent Coatings; 7.12.2.6 Through-Penetration Fireproofing); Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.206 (Applied Fireproofing; Exposed Applications; Slag-Fiberboard), p.163 (Firestopping at curtain wall)</p>"},
{id:"q124",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Branch Circuit Voltage Drop</p><p>In a small commercial tenant fit-out, the electrical panel has been placed at one end of a long suite. The farthest 20-A receptacle branch circuit now runs about 130 ft from the panel. The engineer normally wires these circuits with No. 12 AWG copper.</p><p>Which approach is consistent with MEEB's guidance for limiting voltage drop?</p>",opts:["Keep No. 12 AWG; voltage drop only matters on feeders, not branch circuits.","Increase the conductor to No. 10 AWG for this 100–150 ft run, or relocate the panel so no branch circuit is longer than about 100 ft.","Reduce the conductor to No. 14 AWG and add a GFCI to compensate for the voltage drop.","Increase the conductor to No. 8 AWG, which is required for any branch circuit over 100 ft."],c:[1],e:"<p>To limit voltage drop on a branch circuit, MEEB says panelboards should be located so that no circuit is longer than 100 ft. If 15-A or 20-A circuits longer than that can't be avoided, use No. 10 AWG for 100–150 ft runs and No. 8 AWG for longer runs; these circuits are normally No. 12 AWG. MEEB also suggests keeping branch-circuit drop to about 2 percent to the farthest outlet within an overall 5 percent limit, which leaves about 3 percent for feeders.</p><ul><li>A → Branch circuits are a stated part of the voltage-drop budget.</li><li>C → A smaller conductor increases voltage drop. A GFCI protects people from ground-fault shock; it does nothing for voltage drop.</li><li>D → No. 8 AWG is for runs longer than 150 ft. A 130 ft run needs No. 10.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.1319 (Electrical Equipment Spaces — panel location and voltage drop), p.1346 (branch circuit 2% / overall 5% voltage drop); p.1315 (GFCI function)</p>"},
{id:"q125",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.5",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Low-Slope Roof Detailing</p><p>You are redlining a consultant's low-slope membrane roof plan and details for a lab building. The roof has a large rooftop air handler on a curb, several pipe penetrations, and a building expansion joint. Check the FOUR redlines that reflect recommended roof detailing practice.</p>",opts:["Add a cricket on the upslope side of the air-handler curb.","Depress each roof drain in a sump about 3–4 ft square and at least 1-1/2 in. deep, using tapered insulation.","Replace the pitch pockets at the pipe penetrations with square or round penetration details nearly perpendicular to the roof plane.","Detail the roof expansion joint flush with the membrane surface so water drains across it.","Provide secondary (overflow) drainage in addition to the primary drains; overflow scuppers are the most reliable.","Rely on the manufacturer's standard warranty details as the best indication of the highest-quality detail."],c:[0,1,2,4],e:"<ul><li>A → Provide crickets behind any interruption to the downhill flow of water, such as curbs, rails and rooftop equipment.</li><li>B → Sumps about 3–4 ft square and at least 1-1/2 in. deep, made with tapered insulation, keep standing water from collecting at drains.</li><li>C → Avoid pitch pockets; detail penetrations with square or round shapes nearly perpendicular to the roof.</li><li>E → Roofs need secondary drainage in addition to primary drains. Overflow scuppers are the most reliable; piped secondary drains are an option in most jurisdictions.</li><li>D is wrong: expansion joints and area dividers should be detailed ABOVE the plane of the roof.</li><li>F is wrong: AGS warns that manufacturers' published details are often chosen to be less expensive, and that warrantable assemblies are a compromise between dependable service and low first cost. Compare them with NRCA details and with the manufacturer's extended-warranty details.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.192 (Roof Detailing), p.178 (Roof Slope Schemes; Warranties)</p>"},
{id:"q126",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Protected Membrane Roof</p><p>A cold-climate office building has a concrete roof deck that will also serve as a ballasted roof terrace. The client wants the waterproofing protected from foot traffic, UV and freeze-thaw cycling, and doesn't want a separate vapor retarder that could trap moisture under the roofing.</p><p>Which roof assembly best meets these goals?</p>",opts:["A conventional compact roof: vapor retarder on the deck, insulation above it, and the membrane exposed on top.","A protected membrane roof: the membrane goes directly on the deck below the insulation, so it sits on the warm side and acts as its own vapor retarder, with ballast or pavers over the insulation.","An insulated framed roof with the insulation below the deck and a ventilated space above it.","A conventional roof with edge venting only, which is adequate for any roof width."],c:[1],e:"<p>Olin's defines a protected membrane roofing system as a compact system in which the roofing membrane is below at least part of the insulation. The roofing lies on the warm side and acts as a vapor retarder itself. In most of the country no condensation is associated with this assembly when it is properly designed and installed. In humid areas over air-conditioned buildings, calculations are needed.</p><ul><li>A → Olin's warns that a vapor retarder in cold climates with high indoor humidity greatly increases the chance of trapping moisture between the retarder and the roofing. The exposed membrane is also unprotected from traffic.</li><li>C → This doesn't protect a walkable membrane on a concrete terrace deck.</li><li>D → Edge venting is adequate only for roofs up to about 40 ft wide. Wider roofs also need stack venting, and vents can cause their own leaks.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Olin's, PDF p.698 (Roof and Ceiling Assemblies — Protected Membrane Systems; edge and stack venting)</p>"},
{id:"q127",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.5",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Air Barrier Continuity</p><p>An exterior wall for a mid-rise building is 8 in. CMU backup with board insulation, brick veneer, and aluminum windows and curtain wall. The commissioning agent wants the drawings to show a continuous air barrier. Check the THREE details that provide or maintain air barrier continuity.</p>",opts:["A fluid-applied or self-adhered air barrier membrane on the CMU, with transition membranes sealed to the window and curtain wall frames across the perimeter gap.","Rely on the 8 in. CMU backup as the air barrier because it is a heavy, dense material.","Connect the curtain wall's line of air barrier to the inner shoulder of the glazing pocket so it continues uninterrupted across the perimeter gap.","Rely on tightly fitted, compressed board insulation joints as the air barrier.","Continue the air barrier at the lowest-level slab/foundation walls and tie it to the roof's air barrier, so the conditioned space is fully enclosed."],c:[0,2,4],e:"<p>AGS says the building envelope must have a continuous air barrier, including the lowest slab or crawl space, foundation walls, exterior walls and roof, with sealed connections. Windows and curtain walls must be detailed so the air barrier line runs uninterrupted across the perimeter gap. For curtain walls, that means connecting to the inner shoulder of the glazing pocket.</p><ul><li>B is wrong: AGS lists CMU among common materials that are NOT air barriers.</li><li>D is wrong: board and blanket insulation, even when compressed, is also listed as not being an air barrier.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.143 (Air Barriers; Building Enclosure Sealing; Air Barrier Systems), p.147 (Air and Vapor Barriers at windows), p.163 (Curtain Wall — Air barriers)</p>"},
{id:"q128",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 1.5",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Brick vs. CMU Movement</p><p>A wall section shows clay brick veneer over a CMU backup. A junior designer has labeled the vertical joints in BOTH wythes as \"control joints, 3/8 in., filled with mortar-colored sealant over backer rod,\" spaced the same in both wythes.</p><p>What is the key reason the brick and CMU wythes need different kinds of movement joints?</p>",opts:["Brick has reversible moisture movement and CMU has irreversible moisture expansion, so CMU needs expansion joints.","Clay brick undergoes irreversible moisture EXPANSION, so the brick veneer needs expansion joints that can close. Concrete masonry SHRINKS, so the CMU needs control joints that let it open.","Neither material moves with moisture; only thermal movement matters, so identical joints are correct.","Brick is subject to creep but CMU is not, so only the brick needs joints."],c:[1],e:"<p>AGS Table 9.21 shows that brick masonry has irreversible moisture movement (expansion), while concrete masonry has reversible moisture movement and drying shrinkage. Both also have thermal movement, elastic deformation and creep. So clay brick is jointed with expansion joints, kept free of mortar or hard material so they can compress. CMU uses control joints, which make a plane where shrinkage cracking can happen in a controlled way. Control joints are often sized like a mortar joint (3/8–1/2 in.). AGS adds that control and expansion joints do not have to align in cavity walls but should align in multiwythe walls.</p><ul><li>A → This reverses the two materials.</li><li>C → Table 9.21 shows moisture-related movement in both materials.</li><li>D → The table shows creep for both brick and concrete masonry.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.399 (Movement of Building Materials, Table 9.21; Movement Joints), p.400 (Spacing of Control and Expansion Joints)</p>"},
{id:"q129",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 5.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Life Cycle Cost Comparison</p><p>For a high-traffic public lobby you are comparing two flooring systems over the owner's study period. All future costs are already converted to present value.</p><pre class=\"qtab\">                                    Option A (carpet)   Option B (stone tile)\nFirst cost                              $120,000            $210,000\nSalvage value (PV)                      $0                  $15,000\nMaintenance and repair (PV)             $60,000             $25,000\nReplacement cost (PV)                   $80,000             $0\nOperating cost — cleaning labor (PV)    $45,000             $30,000</pre><p>Using MEEB's equation LCC = IC + MC + AC + OC, where IC = first cost minus salvage, what is the life cycle cost of Option B?</p>",opts:["$210,000","$250,000","$265,000","$280,000"],c:[1],e:"<p>Calculation:<br>Option B: IC = $210,000 − $15,000 = $195,000<br>LCC(B) = $195,000 + $25,000 (MC) + $0 (AC) + $30,000 (OC) = $250,000<br>For comparison, Option A: IC = $120,000 − $0 = $120,000<br>LCC(A) = $120,000 + $60,000 + $80,000 + $45,000 = $305,000<br>So Option B costs $55,000 less over the life cycle, even though its first cost is $90,000 higher.</p><p>Why the other answers are wrong:</p><ul><li>A ($210,000) → First cost only. This is the \"lowest first cost\" thinking LCC is meant to correct.</li><li>C ($265,000) → Leaves out the salvage credit (210 + 25 + 30).</li><li>D ($280,000) → Adds salvage as a cost instead of subtracting it.</li></ul><p>PDPG uses the same example: stone or ceramic tile costs much more than carpet at first but lasts longer and needs less maintenance, making it more economical for a long-term owner. Inexpensive products can become the most expensive once maintenance and replacement are counted.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: MEEB 11th Ed., PDF p.1724 (Appendix I, Eq. I.1 Life-cycle cost); CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide 3rd Ed., PDF p.170–171 (4.2.3.2 Life Cycle Costs)</p>"},
{id:"q130",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 5.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Estimating Method for a Feasibility Budget</p><p>A parking management company needs a construction budget for a new public paid-parking structure. No design exists yet. The feasibility study shows the garage must hold at least 500 cars to make a profit.</p><p>Which estimating method is most appropriate at this point?</p>",opts:["Order of magnitude, using a historical cost per parking space","Unit of measure, using cost per square foot of gross floor area","Assemblies/systems, using UniFormat assembly costs","Unit price, using a full quantity takeoff"],c:[0],e:"<p>An order of magnitude estimate is the quickest and least accurate (about 20–25 percent) method. It uses per-unit-of-occupancy costs (PDPG's examples are cost per bed for hospitals and hotels, or per seat for theaters and restaurants), drawn from historical cost data. It is used while the project concept or program is still at its earliest stage. Cost per parking space is the same kind of unit.</p><ul><li>B → Unit of measure needs the construction type and approximate total facility size, which come in schematic design.</li><li>C → Assemblies/systems needs design development information.</li><li>D → Unit price needs completed construction documents.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: CSI Project Delivery Study Workbook, PDF p.123 (3D – Learning Activity #1, Scenario 3); CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide 3rd Ed., PDF p.168–169 (Order of magnitude; Table 4.1)</p>"},
{id:"q131",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 5.1",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">What an Estimate Is Based On</p><p>At the end of SD, the owner asks what your \"estimate of probable construction cost\" is based on. Check the FOUR sources that PDPG lists as a basis for an estimate.</p>",opts:["Cost of similar constructed projects","Published cost data","Firm bids from contractors, subcontractors and suppliers on this project","Economic conditions expected at the proposed time of construction","A contingency for factors that can't be estimated, such as unknown details, hidden conditions, soil conditions, market changes and weather delays","The contractor's guaranteed maximum price"],c:[0,1,3,4],e:"<p>PDPG says that \"an estimate is not a price.\" A price is based on bids from contractors, subcontractors and suppliers. An estimate is an opinion of probable construction cost based on: the cost of similar projects, published cost data, input from construction participants, the economic conditions expected at the time of construction, and a contingency for factors that can't be estimated.</p><ul><li>C and F are prices (bids, a GMP), not the basis of the architect's estimate.</li></ul><p>PDPG also notes that when estimates exceed the budget, design changes may be needed, and the earlier those changes are made, the less they affect the design schedule.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide 3rd Ed., PDF p.168 (4.2.2.6 Budget; 4.2.3 Managing Construction Costs; 4.2.3.1 Cost Estimating)</p>"},
{id:"q132",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 5.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Over Budget at the End of DD</p><p>Case: A 60,000 SF medical office building is at the end of design development.</p><ul><li>Owner's construction budget: $24.0 M</li><li>Architect's DD assemblies estimate: $25.9 M (about 8 percent over)</li><li>The biggest cost drivers are a custom unitized curtain wall, moment connections at every steel joint, and a fully flat structural roof deck with tapered insulation built up over the entire roof.</li><li>The owner wants to stay on schedule and does not want to raise the budget.</li></ul><p>Which course of action is most appropriate?</p>",opts:["Proceed into construction documents unchanged and include enough deductive alternates to cut the overage at bidding.","Before starting CDs, review scope and component costs with the owner, revise the design (for example, braced bays to reduce moment connections, and a sloped structural deck with tapered insulation used only for crickets), and re-estimate before proceeding.","Replace the curtain wall specification with a cash allowance equal to the curtain wall's current estimated cost so the bids come in on budget.","Do nothing yet. An assemblies estimate is only accurate to about 10 percent, so the project may still bid within budget."],c:[1],e:"<p>PDPG says a change in project scope must be matched by a change in budget, or a change in component cost must be offset by reducing scope or raising the budget. When estimates exceed the budget, design changes may be needed, and changes made earlier have less impact (Fig. 2.1: it is less costly to change the design early). The suggested changes come from the references: reducing moment joints in favor of shear joints in braced frames lowers steel cost (Olin's), and it is typically less expensive to slope the roof deck and use tapered insulation only for crickets in small areas (AGS).</p><ul><li>A → Over-reliance on alternates complicates bidding, and alternate prices may not be competitive. PDPG notes that if the team has time to design and estimate accurately, alternates usually aren't needed.</li><li>C → Cash allowances suspend competitive pricing on the product, deprive bidders of information, and \"almost always increase project costs.\" Using one doesn't reduce the cost of the work.</li><li>D → An 8 percent overage is close to the whole accuracy band of the estimate. Waiting until bids come in pushes the fix to the costliest time to make changes.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide 3rd Ed., PDF p.168 (4.2.3 Managing Construction Costs), p.169 (Table 4.1), p.175–176 (Allowances; Alternates); Olin's, PDF p.456 (moment vs. shear joints cost); Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.178 (Roof Slope Schemes)</p>"},
{id:"q133",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 2.3",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Generic vs. Proprietary Notes on Drawings</p><p>During a QC review of a plaza paving plan, the spec writer finds that every paving callout reads \"ACME HOLLAND-STONE CONCRETE PAVER, CHARCOAL, 60 MM.\" The specifications (Section 32 14 13) list Acme as basis of design and allow substitutions under the Division 01 procedures. Late in CDs, the owner is considering a switch to clay brick pavers.</p><p>Which revision to the drawing notes best follows CSI coordination practice?</p>",opts:["Keep the full proprietary callout so bidders know exactly what was designed, and add \"OR EQUAL\" to each note.","Change each callout to \"UNIT PAVERS,\" and keep the product, color, size, and installation requirements in the specification section only.","Change each callout to \"UNIT PAVERS — SEE SPECIFICATIONS\" so the contractor knows where to look.","Delete the paving specification section and move the full product description into a general note on the cover sheet so it appears only once."],c:[1],e:"<p>Drawings should use only generic notes that identify, but do not describe, a material. CSI uses this exact example: if drawings say \"concrete pavers,\" switching to brick means finding and changing many notes plus the specs; if the drawings say \"unit pavers,\" only the specifications change. Proprietary names should stay off the drawings unless a closed proprietary spec limits the choice to one product. Otherwise, accepting a substitution could mean revising every proprietary reference by addendum or contract modification.</p><ul><li>A → Still proprietary on the drawings. A substitution or design change would require revising every note.</li><li>C → CSI says to avoid \"see specifications\" and \"refer to specifications\" notes, because drawings and specs are complementary parts of one set.</li><li>D → Detailed product information belongs in the specifications, not in drawing general notes. General notes should be used sparingly.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide 3rd ed. (PDPG3e_digital), PDF p.266 (5.8.2.1 Drawings; unit pavers example), PDF p.267 (proprietary names on drawings; no \"see specifications\" cross-references), PDF p.231 (5.2.4.6 Notations)</p>"},
{id:"q134",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 2.3",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">What the Drawings Show</p><p>A junior designer is cleaning up notes on a set of wall sections for a lab building. Wall type W-3 includes a vapor retarder. She asks which kinds of information about W-3 belong on the DRAWINGS and which belong in the project manual.</p><p>Check the FOUR kinds of information that CSI says the drawings should convey.</p>",opts:["The location and extent of the vapor retarder within the wall assembly","The minimum perm rating and physical properties of the vapor retarder","The size and form of the wall components, dimensioned","The quantity and relationship of components (for example, how many layers and where they sit relative to the studs)","The generic type of each material, such as \"vapor retarder\" or \"Type A roof membrane\"","The installation methods and quality control provisions for lapping and sealing the vapor retarder","Reference standards the material must comply with"],c:[0,2,3,4],e:"<p>Drawings and specifications complement each other. The drawings show size, form, quantity, relationship, generic type, and graphic representation of materials. The specifications define qualitative requirements for products, materials, and workmanship. CSI's own example: the drawings \"might simply refer to a vapor retarder,\" while the specifications define \"minimum perm rating, physical properties, required installation methods, and provisions for quality control.\" Specifications usually should NOT include information that belongs on the drawings, such as quantity, form, and location.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.265 (5.8.2 Coordinating Drawings and Specifications), PDF p.251 (5.5 Developing Specifications; vapor retarder example), PDF p.233 (\"drawings show products... quantitatively, specifications detail their qualitative requirements\")</p>"},
{id:"q135",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 3.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Closed vs. Open Proprietary Specification</p><p>A spec writer drafts PART 2 of Section 08 71 00 (Door Hardware) for a corporate client with a campus-wide hardware standard:</p><pre class=\"qtab\">\"2.01 MANUFACTURERS\n A. Locksets: Manufacturer A, Series 1; Manufacturer B, Series 2; or Manufacturer C, Series 3.\n B. Substitutions: Not permitted.\"</pre><p>How is this specifying method best described?</p>",opts:["Open proprietary specification, because more than one product is named","Closed proprietary specification","Descriptive specification, because the products are named by series","Open proprietary specification using the \"controlled substitutions\" technique"],c:[1],e:"<p>A closed proprietary specification can \"list one product or name several products as options. In either case, substitutions are not allowed.\" Naming three manufacturers does not make a spec open. What makes it open or closed is whether substitutions are allowed. Closed methods give bidders little or no choice. They let the design be completed to a high level of detail and promote accurate pricing, but they do not protect against higher prices from a sole source.</p><ul><li>A → Listing several products does not make the spec open when substitutions are prohibited.</li><li>C → A descriptive spec describes required properties and workmanship and does NOT use proprietary names.</li><li>D → Controlled substitutions name specific products but allow substitutions under specified procedures. This spec forbids them.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.237 (Closed and Open Proprietary Specifications), PDF p.234 (5.3.1.1 Descriptive Specifications)</p>"},
{id:"q136",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 3.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Alternate vs. Substitute</p><p>On a library project, the bid form asks for an additive price to use a brick exterior finish instead of the base-bid fiber-cement siding. During the bidding period, a roofing sub-bidder asks the architect to accept a TPO membrane in place of the EPDM membrane named in Section 07 53 23.</p><p>Which statement correctly identifies the two items?</p>",opts:["Both are alternates, because each gives the owner a pricing choice.","Both are substitutions, because each proposes a product other than the base-bid product.","The brick finish is an alternate. The TPO membrane is a substitution request.","The brick finish is an allowance. The TPO membrane is an alternate."],c:[2],e:"<p>CSI notes that the two terms are often misused. \"An alternate is something that is named for which alternative pricing is requested; a substitute is something that is not named in the specification and is requested to replace an item as specified.\" An alternate is a defined portion of the work priced separately. It can be additive or deductive and can choose between products, for example brick instead of wood siding. If the substitution is accepted during bidding, the architect issues an addendum so all bidders can price it.</p><ul><li>A, B → These blur the named/not-named distinction that separates the two terms.</li><li>D → An allowance is a sum of money or a quantity carried in the bid for work not yet fully defined. It does not set up a choice between two named options.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.237 (alternate vs. substitute definition), PDF p.175–176 (alternates; brick vs. wood siding example), PDF p.301 (6.6.1.3 Substitution Requests)</p>"},
{id:"q137",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 3.1",t:"num",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Cash Allowance Reconciliation</p><p>The face brick for a clinic has not been selected at bid time. Section 01 21 00 (Allowances) includes a cash allowance of $42,000 for purchase of face brick. Following the traditional approach, the allowance covers product cost only, and installation labor, overhead, and profit are carried in the contractor's base bid. During construction the owner selects a brick, and the contractor's submitted product cost is $47,350.</p><p>By how much should the contract sum be adjusted by change order? (Enter a whole number in dollars, positive for an increase.)</p>",ans:5350,tol:0.5,unit:"",e:"<p>Calculation: Actual product cost − allowance = $47,350 − $42,000 = +$5,350 (increase)</p><p>When the actual selection is made, the contractor submits a price for the specified brick. The contract sum is then adjusted by change order to cover the difference between the actual price and the allowance. Traditionally, cash allowances pay for the product, and labor, overhead, and profit are included in the cost of the work outside the allowance. That is why only the product difference is adjusted here. CSI warns that the documents must say exactly which costs the allowance includes (product, taxes, delivery/handling, installation labor, overhead, profit, bond). Cash allowances should not be used just to put off decisions.</p><p>Note: On the exam, enter the numeric value only (5350), with no \"$\" or commas.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.174 (cash allowance, brick example, adjustment by change order, list of costs to define), PDF p.175 (Section 01 21 00 Allowances)</p>"},
{id:"q138",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 2.5",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Prebid Meeting Answers</p><p>At a mandatory prebid meeting for a public fire station, a bidder asks whether the apparatus-bay trench drains are in the base bid or in Alternate No. 2. The project architect answers out loud that they are in the base bid. The architect's assistant records this in the meeting minutes, and the minutes are emailed to every attendee.</p><p>What should the architect do next?</p>",opts:["Nothing more. The emailed minutes are binding because all bidders received them.","Issue the answer as a written addendum to all document holders, to be acknowledged on the bid form.","Answer by RFI response after award, since the question is about scope.","Issue a change order after award to add the trench drains to the base contract."],c:[1],e:"<p>Meeting proceedings are not procurement or contract documents. Oral interpretations and new information from the prebid meeting must be documented in an addendum. Addenda modify the procurement documents. They are issued after bidding starts and before bids are submitted, and they are acknowledged on the bid form. In competitive bidding, the same information must go to all bidders. Not every bidder may have attended, and minutes are not part of the documents.</p><ul><li>A → Minutes are not procurement or contract documents.</li><li>C → RFIs are a construction-phase tool. The question has to be resolved before bids are priced.</li><li>D → Change orders modify the contract after execution. Using one here would invite unequal bids and an avoidable cost change.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.301 (6.6.1.1 prebid proceedings must be documented in an addendum; 6.6.1.4 Addenda), PDF p.302 (Figure 6.6, sample addendum)</p>"},
{id:"q139",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 3.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">SectionFormat Placement</p><p>A spec writer is editing Section 09 65 19 (Resilient Tile Flooring). She needs to place a requirement that the installer verify substrate moisture and flatness before installing tile, and document any unsatisfactory conditions.</p><p>Under SectionFormat, where does this requirement belong?</p>",opts:["PART 1 – GENERAL, under Quality Assurance","PART 1 – GENERAL, under Field [or] Site Conditions","PART 2 – PRODUCTS, under Source Quality Control","PART 3 – EXECUTION, under Examination (Verification of Conditions)"],c:[3],e:"<p>SectionFormat has three parts. PART 1 General covers administrative, procedural, and temporary requirements unique to the section, as an extension of Division 01. PART 2 Products covers products, materials, and fabrication with their quality requirements. PART 3 Execution covers installation or application, including preparatory actions and post-installation cleaning and protection. Examination, with its Verification of Conditions, Preinstallation Testing, and Evaluation and Assessment articles, is the first article group in PART 3.</p><ul><li>A → Quality Assurance in PART 1 covers qualifications, certifications, mock-ups, and similar items, not on-site checks of the substrate before installation.</li><li>B → Field/Site Conditions in PART 1 covers ambient and existing conditions as administrative requirements. The installer's actual inspection before work is an execution activity.</li><li>C → Source Quality Control is testing and inspection at the manufacturer or fabricator, not at the site.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.242–243 (Figure 5.15, organizational structure of SectionFormat), PDF p.51 (1.11.1.4 SectionFormat Parts 1–3)</p>"},
{id:"q140",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 3.1",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Division 01 and Division 00 Homes for Special Pricing and Substitutions</p><p>Match each requirement to the MasterFormat document or section where CSI says it should be described:</p>",items:["Add Alternate No. 1: Replace granite base with brick in all areas indicated.","Rock excavation: price per cubic yard to adjust the contract sum for actual quantities removed, with the method of measurement and who measures.","Include the sum of $25,000 for purchase of signage, to be selected by Owner.","Requests to use unnamed products during construction must be submitted with a comparison of product data and a statement of cost and time effects.","Proposed substitutions must be submitted in writing at least 15 days before the date for opening bids."],opts:["00 26 00 Procurement Substitution Procedures","01 21 00 Allowances","01 22 00 Unit Prices","01 23 00 Alternates","01 25 00 Substitution Procedures"],c:[3,2,1,4,0],e:"<p>Allowances, alternates, and unit prices each have a Division 01 section, and each must be coordinated with the bid form and the affected sections in Divisions 02–49. Substitution rules can be in Division 00 (procurement), Division 01 (construction), or both, which is why SectionFormat's \"Substitution Limitations\" article points to both 00 26 00 and 01 25 00. Statement E paraphrases the sample addendum in your PDPG, which revised the Instructions to Bidders.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.175 (01 21 00), PDF p.176 (01 23 00), PDF p.177 (01 22 00), PDF p.283–285 (bid form allowances/alternates/unit prices), PDF p.896 (SectionFormat Substitution Limitations: coordination with 00 26 00 and 01 25 00), PDF p.302 (sample addendum, 15-day substitution deadline)</p>"},
{id:"q141",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 2.4",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Sheet Numbering</p><p>A drawing index for a mid-rise office building lists sheet A-201 under the architectural discipline. Following the sheet numbering convention described in your CSI materials, what does \"A-201\" tell the user?</p>",opts:["Architectural discipline, sheet type \"2\" = elevations, and \"01\" = the first sheet of elevations","Architectural discipline, second floor, detail 01","Architectural discipline, phase 2 of construction, revision 01","Addendum No. 2, sheet 01"],c:[0],e:"<p>The PDPG uses this example: \"If a title block includes the sheet A-201, this represents an Architecture (discipline) drawing, the 200 series number which indicates the drawing elevations, and the final two numbers indicates it is the first sheet of elevations.\" Sets are traditionally grouped by discipline and then by drawing type (plans, elevations, sections, and so on). The CSI Uniform Drawing System, part of the U.S. National CAD Standard, sets the content and order of the set, sheet identification, and file naming.</p><ul><li>B → The number after the hyphen identifies sheet type and sequence, not a floor level.</li><li>C → Phasing and revisions are tracked separately, for example with revision clouds and identifiers. They are not part of the sheet number.</li><li>D → Addenda are numbered and dated separately, as shown in the sample addendum.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.52 (1.11.2 Graphic Formats; A-201 example; NCS), PDF p.53 (1.11.2.3 CSI Uniform Drawing System), PDF p.230 (Figure 5.13, traditional drawing set organization)</p>"},
{id:"q142",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 4.1",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Accessible Door Details</p><p>An architect is reviewing the interior door details and door hardware requirements for the accessible route in a new medical office building. The doors are interior and not fire-rated. Under the accessible-door guidance in Architectural Graphic Standards (based on ICC/ANSI A117.1 and consistent with the 2010 ADA Standards), which FOUR requirements should the documents include?</p>",opts:["Thresholds limited to 1/2 in. maximum height for new work","Door closers adjusted so the door takes at least 5 seconds to move from 90° open to 12° from the latch","Interior door opening force of 5 lb maximum","Clear width of a hinged door measured from the face of the door to the door stop, with the door open 90°","Clear width measured from jamb to jamb of the rough opening","Floor within the maneuvering clearance sloped no steeper than 1:20 so it can drain","Hardware that requires a firm grasp and twist, so that doors stay latched"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p>AGS notes for accessible doors:</p><ul><li>Hardware must be operable with one hand, without tight grasping, pinching, or twisting of the wrist.</li><li>Thresholds are typically limited to 1/2 in. maximum. Some standards allow 3/4 in., beveled at 1:2 maximum, for existing or altered thresholds.</li><li>Interior doors other than fire doors must open with 5 lb of force. The AHJ may regulate exterior doors and fire doors.</li><li>Closers need at least a 5-second interval from 90° to 12° open.</li><li>Hinged-door clear width is measured between the face of the door and the stop with the door open 90°.</li><li>The floor within the maneuvering clearance must not slope more than 1:48 and must be stable, firm, and slip-resistant.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.496 (13.56 Accessible Door Features notes a–d), PDF p.497 (clear width measurement; maneuvering clearance slope 1:48)</p>"},
{id:"q143",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 4.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Firestopping Ratings in a Penetration Detail</p><p>A plumbing riser passes through a 2-hour rated floor. The architect's detail references a tested through-penetration firestop system. During submittal review, the firestop installer's product data lists both an \"F-rating\" and a \"T-rating\" for the system.</p><p>Which statement correctly describes what the T-rating adds beyond the F-rating?</p>",opts:["The T-rating confirms that flames did not pass through the opening and that the hose stream did not penetrate.","The T-rating confirms that, during the rating period, heat moving through the system did not raise the temperature on the unexposed surface or on the penetrating item by more than 325°F above its starting temperature.","The T-rating is the time the assembly can carry its full structural load during the fire test.","The T-rating is the smoke-leakage rating of the system, required at every penetration."],c:[1],e:"<p>Penetration firestopping performance is measured in F- and T-ratings, tested to ASTM E 814 (Fire Tests of Through-Penetration Fire Stops).</p><ul><li>The F-rating means that, for the rating period, there was no flame through openings, no flaming on the unexposed side, and no openings that let the hose stream project through.</li><li>The T-rating adds a heat-transmission limit: no thermocouple on the unexposed surface or on the penetrating item rose more than 325°F above its initial temperature.</li></ul><p>AGS adds that the designer must understand both the code requirements and how the AHJ interprets and enforces them when selecting firestopping.</p><ul><li>A → That is the F-rating criterion.</li><li>C → Load-carrying fire endurance belongs to the rated floor or wall assembly, not to the firestop system.</li><li>D → Smoke leakage is not what the T-rating measures.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.206 (Penetration Firestopping: F- and T-ratings, ASTM E 814); Olin's Construction Principles, Materials, and Methods, PDF p.887 (7.12.2.6 Through-Penetration Fireproofing/firestopping materials)</p>"},
{id:"q144",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 2.4",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">When the Documents Disagree</p><p>Case: A university classroom building is under construction. Two issues come up:</p><p>Issue 1: The cover-sheet general notes say \"All interior wood doors: paint finish.\" The door schedule on A-601 lists Door 214 as \"WD-2, transparent stain finish.\"</p><p>Issue 2: A specification paragraph written by the architect reads: \"Provide sealant at joints as required.\" The contractor sealed only exterior joints. The architect says interior control joints were intended too. The owner later goes to court over the omission.</p><p>Which pair of court interpretation rules from your CSI materials applies to Issue 1 and Issue 2?</p>",opts:["Issue 1: Trade customs. Issue 2: Practical interpretation.","Issue 1: Specific takes precedence over general. Issue 2: Construction against the drafter.","Issue 1: Entire agreement. Issue 2: Autonomy to contract.","Issue 1: Construction against the drafter. Issue 2: Specific takes precedence over general."],c:[1],e:"<ul><li>Issue 1: \"When specific information is written, the courts interpret it as intentional for the work and will be given more importance than general information or printed provisions.\" The door schedule entry for Door 214 is specific. The cover-sheet note is general.</li><li>Issue 2: \"Ambiguous provisions will be interpreted against the party drafting the provision on the basis that the drafter had superior knowledge of the subject matter and should have been more specific or accurate.\" The phrase \"as required\" is ambiguous, and CSI lists \"as required\" among the inappropriate terms to avoid in specifications.</li><li>Trade customs apply only when the documents do not establish intent. The court may then rely on industry or trade custom, after looking at any referenced trade association standards.</li><li>Entire agreement means the court reads all documents together to find the parties' intent.</li><li>Autonomy to contract is the basic freedom of the parties to set terms. It does not settle a conflict.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.61 (1.14.8 Interpretation of Contract Documents by the Courts), PDF p.245 (5.3.3.9 Inappropriate Terms: \"as required\"); Project Delivery Study Workbook, PDF p.32 (1E Case Study on court interpretations)</p>"},
{id:"q145",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 3.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Choosing a Performance Specification</p><p>A state agency is procuring a new building automation and monitoring system for a laboratory. No single product is recognized as the exclusive solution. Several systems on the market are reasonably competitive in cost. The agency wants competing vendors to propose their best solution as long as it meets measured energy and control outcomes.</p><p>Which statement best describes the specification the architect should write?</p>",opts:["A descriptive specification that lists the detailed properties of each component and its installation, with no manufacturer names","A closed proprietary specification naming the agency's preferred system, to reduce bidding variables","A performance specification: a statement of required results, with criteria for verifying compliance, without unnecessary limits on the means of achieving the results","A reference standard specification that cites a national standard in place of any project requirements"],c:[2],e:"<p>A performance specification is \"a statement of required results with criteria for verifying compliance but without unnecessary limitations on the methods for achieving the required results.\" Conditions that favor it are all present here:</p><ul><li>no single, distinct solution is recognized as the exclusive choice</li><li>the costs of the options are reasonably competitive</li><li>the item is a system where competition among options will produce optimum solutions</li></ul><p>Building monitoring and data/communication systems are listed among the non-standard technologies suited to performance specifying, and CSI notes that performance specifications are common on public projects. An incomplete statement of results leads to \"a major loss of quality control.\"</p><ul><li>A → Descriptive specs fix the products and processes, not the results. The A/E takes on the performance criteria, and the method is lengthy and used less often as projects grow more complex.</li><li>B → Closed proprietary removes the competition the agency wants.</li><li>D → Reference standards set minimum requirements and do not, on their own, state the project's required outcomes.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.233 (5.3.1 Methods of Specifying), PDF p.234 (5.3.1.1 Descriptive; 5.3.1.2 Performance, favoring conditions), PDF p.235 (Utilizing Non-Standard Technologies; building monitoring systems)</p>"},
{id:"q146",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 3.2",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Liabilities of Reference Standard Specifying</p><p>To shorten Section 04 20 00 (Unit Masonry), a spec writer plans to rely heavily on ASTM and TMS standards incorporated by reference. Her project manager asks her to list the risks first.</p><p>Check the FOUR liabilities that CSI identifies with reference standard specifying.</p>",opts:["The standard may contain embedded options that the specifier must select or close.","Reference standards generally state minimum requirements.","The standard can create duplication and contradiction within the contract documents.","Different AHJs may enforce different editions of the same standard, which may have conflicting requirements.","A referenced standard applies only to the paragraphs the specifier quotes in full in the section.","Reference standards always make the section longer than a descriptive specification."],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p>A reference standard is a consensus document. Once incorporated by number, title, or designation, \"its provisions take effect just as though it had been included in its entirety.\" This saves elaborate writing but creates these liabilities:</p><ul><li>inadequate standards coexist with stringent ones</li><li>duplication and contradiction within the contract documents</li><li>embedded options</li><li>minimum requirements only</li><li>possible undesired requirements</li><li>different AHJs enforcing different editions</li></ul><p>Where applicable, CSI recommends using standards listed in the model or local codes so the AHJ will accept them.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.239 (5.3.1.4 Reference Standard)</p>"},
{id:"q147",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 3.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Division 00 vs. Division 01</p><p>An architect is assembling the project manual for a design-bid-build middle school. She has the following draft documents: the Invitation to Bid, the Instructions to Bidders, the Bid Form, the owner's Supplementary Conditions, and a section describing the contractor's temporary field offices, temporary utilities, and the procedures for submittals and quality control.</p><p>Which statement correctly places these documents?</p>",opts:["All five are specifications and belong in Division 01 – General Requirements.","The Invitation, Instructions, Bid Form, and Supplementary Conditions belong in Division 00 (Procurement and Contracting Requirements). The temporary facilities and procedures section is a Division 01 specification.","The Supplementary Conditions belong in Division 01 because they apply to all sections. The rest belong in Division 00.","The temporary facilities requirements belong in Division 00 because they affect the bid price."],c:[1],e:"<p>MasterFormat's Procurement and Contracting Requirements Group has a single division, Division 00. It holds procurement requirements (advertisements and invitations, instructions, available information, forms and supplements) and contracting requirements (forms, project forms, conditions of the contract, bonds, insurance). These are in the project manual but \"are not specifications.\" Division 01 – General Requirements IS a specification division. It covers administrative and procedural requirements (contract administration, communication, quality), temporary facilities and controls, performance requirements for facilities and systems, and life-cycle activities such as commissioning. It applies broadly to Divisions 02–49.</p><ul><li>A → Procurement and contracting documents are not specifications.</li><li>C → Conditions of the contract, including supplementary conditions, are contracting requirements in Division 00. CSI's Figure 5.18 contrasts them with Division 01 (\"Are not specifications\" vs. \"Are specifications\").</li><li>D → Temporary facilities are specified in Division 01. Cost impact does not move them to Division 00.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.240 (MasterFormat groups; Division 00 is the single division of the Procurement and Contracting Requirements Group), PDF p.256 (5.6 Project Manual; 5.6.1 Division 00), PDF p.257 (Figure 5.18; Division 00 lists), PDF p.258 (5.6.3 Division 01)</p>"},
{id:"q148",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 4.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Fire-Rated Door Documentation</p><p>The life safety plan for an office building requires a 90-minute fire-rated door from the corridor into an exit stair. The architect's door schedule and hardware set for this opening are being finalized, and the door must have panic-type hardware.</p><p>Which documentation approach is correct?</p>",opts:["Specify a labeled 90-minute door leaf. A standard commercial hollow metal frame and standard panic hardware are acceptable because the rating comes from the leaf.","Specify a fire-door assembly (door, frame, hardware, and accessories) that together provides the required protection, each component labeled or listed for the use, with fire exit hardware.","Specify a solid-core wood door. Most solid-core doors carry a 20-minute rating, which can be increased by adding a closer.","Specify any door and note \"fire rating per code\" so the contractor chooses the components."],c:[1],e:"<p>AGS defines a fire-door assembly as \"any combination of a fire door, frame, hardware, and other accessories that together provide a specific degree of fire protection.\" Fire exit hardware is \"panic hardware that is listed for use on fire-door assemblies.\" \"Labeled\" means marked by an approved testing organization showing compliance with standards. \"Listed\" means included in a testing agency's published list for use in fire-rated assemblies. Olin adds that doors requiring specific fire ratings must carry a fire door label from an approved testing agency such as UL.</p><ul><li>A → The rating applies to the whole assembly. Ordinary panic hardware is not fire exit hardware.</li><li>C → Many regular solid-core doors may reach 20 minutes, but a closer does not raise the rating. A 90-minute opening needs a door built and labeled for that rating.</li><li>D → The architect must document the requirement. A vague \"per code\" note shifts the design decision to the contractor and invites a discrepancy.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.206 (Basic Door Terminology: fire-door assembly, fire exit hardware, labeled, listed, panic hardware); Olin's Construction Principles, Materials, and Methods, PDF p.923 (fire door label; wood door ratings 20 min to 1-1/2 hr)</p>"},
{id:"q149",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 3.1",t:"num",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Unit Price Adjustment for Drilled Piers</p><p>Because subsurface conditions are uncertain, the procurement documents set the depth of 16 drilled concrete piers at 28 ft each for pricing. The bid form asks for a unit price for added length. The successful bidder quoted $120 per linear foot for added depth. During construction, the geotechnical engineer measures the completed piers. Ten piers are at the stipulated 28 ft, and six piers had to be drilled to 33 ft.</p><p>What is the change order amount for the added pier length? (Enter a whole number in dollars.)</p>",ans:3600,tol:0.5,unit:"",e:"<p>Calculation:<br>Added length = 6 piers × (33 ft − 28 ft) = 6 × 5 = 30 LF<br>Adjustment = 30 LF × $120/LF = $3,600 (increase)</p><p>Unit prices are used where the type of work is defined but the extent is unknown or likely to change. CSI uses drilled piers as the example. A stipulated depth is set for pricing, the bidder quotes unit prices for extra or reduced length, measurements are taken during construction, and the contract sum is adjusted by change order based on the unit prices for the actual pier depths. Section 01 22 00 Unit Prices should cover how actual quantities are determined and who measures them, and it should be coordinated with the general conditions.</p><p>Note: On the exam, enter 3600 only.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.176 (unit prices; drilled pier example), PDF p.177 (adjustment by change order; Section 01 22 00 requirements), PDF p.284 (Figure 6.3, sample bid form unit price clause)</p>"},
{id:"q150",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 2.5",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Which Instrument Fits the Situation?</p><p>Match each situation on a design-bid-build project under the AIA A201-2017 family to the correct instrument:</p>",items:["Bids are due in nine days. The architect discovers the mechanical plan shows a 12 x 6 exhaust duct that must be 12 x 18.","During construction, the architect relocates a wall-mounted fire extinguisher cabinet 2 ft along the same wall. There is no change in cost or time.","The owner needs added blocking for future casework installed immediately. The owner and contractor have not agreed on the price, and the work cannot wait.","The contractor's proposal for an added roof hatch is reviewed and accepted. The architect, contractor, and owner sign a document adjusting the contract sum by $4,200 and the contract time by 2 days."],opts:["Addendum","Architect's Supplemental Instructions (AIA G710)","Construction Change Directive (AIA G714)","Change Order"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p>Changes that affect contract sum or time require a change order. A change directive is used to proceed before agreement, and minor changes that don't affect sum or time can be handled with supplemental instructions. CSI also cautions that the architect should not rely on modifications to correct poorly prepared documents.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.301 (6.6.1.4 Addenda), PDF p.302 (Figure 6.6, sample addendum: M-1 duct revision), PDF p.359 (8.9 Interpretations and Modifications), PDF p.360 (8.9.4 Minor Changes, G710; 8.9.5 Change Directives, G714), PDF p.361 (8.9.6 Change Orders)</p>"},
{id:"q151",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 2.4",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Coordination Practices for Drawings and Specs</p><p>A firm is updating its CD drafting standard to reduce conflicts between drawings and specifications. Check the FOUR practices that are consistent with your CSI Project Delivery Practice Guide.</p>",opts:["Use the same terminology on the drawings as in the specifications, for example not \"WDW\" on drawings when curtain wall is specified.","It is acceptable to reference a specific specification section in a note, such as \"Provide firestopping at wall penetration — see Section 07 84 00.\"","List abbreviations used on the drawings on the drawings themselves, edited to the project, rather than in the specifications.","Use the drawings to show the extent of alternates, phasing areas, and limits of work, while defining their written scope in Division 01 and PART 1 of the affected sections.","Assign each drawing sheet to a specific subcontractor so trade scopes are clear.","Repeat key product requirements both in drawing notes and in the specs so nothing is missed."],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p>CSI recommends:</p><ul><li>consistent terminology and abbreviations between drawings and specs</li><li>an edited abbreviation list placed on the drawings</li><li>no generic \"see specifications\" notes, though references to specific sections are acceptable</li><li>drawings that do not define the work of specific subcontractors or trades</li><li>alternates, phasing, limits of work, and owner/separate-contract work delineated graphically, with the written scope in Division 01 and PART 1</li></ul><p>Duplication is an opportunity for discrepancies. An addendum may fix an item in one location but miss it in the other.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.231 (5.2.4.6 Notations; 5.2.4.7 Abbreviations), PDF p.265 (Duplications; \"say it once and in the right place\"), PDF p.267 (cross-referencing; trades; alternates/phasing on drawings; 5.8.2.2 Specifications)</p>"},
{id:"q152",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 2.5",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Delivery Method and Level of Specification Detail</p><p>An architect is setting up the documents for four different clients:</p><pre class=\"qtab\">1. A small tenant fit-out with standard finishes\n2. A design-build medical office where the design-builder is responsible for most decisions\n3. A negotiated design-bid-build project with a contractor who has built three similar buildings for this owner\n4. A construction manager at risk project with multiple-prime contracts</pre><p>For which project does the PDPG say full-length specifications are usually more suitable than shortform specifications, unless the shortform is written to address the delivery method's complications?</p>",opts:["Project 1, the tenant fit-out","Project 2, the design-build medical office","Project 3, the negotiated design-bid-build project","Project 4, the CM at risk project with multiple-prime contracts"],c:[3],e:"<p>CSI says shortform specifications suit small projects, interior design and tenant improvement work, negotiated design-bid-build where the contractor is familiar with the owner's expectations, design-build (where the single contract and point of accountability improve coordination), and owner-build. For construction manager at risk, \"multiple-prime contracts may present coordination complications. Unless the shortform specification is written to cover such situations, full-length specifications are usually more suitable.\" Even shortform specs must give enough detail to describe the project requirements.</p><ul><li>A, B, C → Each is listed as a project type that lends itself to shortform specifications.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.248 (5.4.3 Shortform Specifications: project types including CMAR), PDF p.247 (Table 5.1; shortform for small-to-medium projects), PDF p.251 (5.4.4 Full-Length Specifications)</p>"},
{id:"q153",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 3.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">A Disguised Proprietary Specification</p><p>A public owner requires nonrestrictive specifications. To comply, a spec writer takes the manufacturer's data sheet for a particular wall panel, deletes the brand name, and copies every listed property verbatim into PART 2 as fixed values with no ranges.</p><p>How does the PDPG characterize this specification?</p>",opts:["A nonrestrictive performance specification, because no proprietary name appears","A reference standard specification, because the properties came from published data","A fully proprietary, closed specification that is not a suitable nonrestrictive solution","An open proprietary specification using the \"proposed substitutions\" technique"],c:[2],e:"<p>\"A proprietary specification masquerading as a performance specification is not nonrestrictive.\" If the specifier deletes the product name and lists the salient qualities verbatim from the manufacturer's literature without any ranges, \"this method results in a fully proprietary, closed specification.\" Nonrestrictive specifications can be built from any of the four methods, but the requirements must be achievable by several manufacturers or suppliers. Otherwise the spec is restrictive even without a brand name, or impossible to meet.</p><ul><li>A → Removing the name does not remove the restriction.</li><li>B → A reference standard is a consensus document, not one manufacturer's data sheet.</li><li>D → Open methods allow other products to be proposed. Here only one product can comply.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.239 (5.3.1.5 Nonrestrictive Specifications)</p>"},
{id:"q154",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 4.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Recessed Door Maneuvering Clearance</p><p>On an accessible route in a new clinic, a door to an exam room sits in a 12-in.-deep wall recess. The wall on the latch side, within 18 in. of the doorway, projects 12 in. beyond the face of the door. The door is approached from the push side.</p><p>According to the AGS accessible door guidance (ICC/ANSI A117.1, consistent with the 2010 ADA Standards), what must the architect show for this door?</p>",opts:["No additional clearance is needed, because push-side approaches don't require latch-side clearance.","Maneuvering clearance for a forward approach, because an obstruction within 18 in. of the latch side projects more than 8 in. beyond the face of the door","A full-powered automatic operator, because recessed doors cannot comply with manual-door clearances","A 60-in.-diameter turning space on the pull side only"],c:[1],e:"<p>AGS: \"Where any obstruction within 18 in. of the latch side of a doorway projects more than 8 in. beyond the face of the door (e.g., a recessed door) maneuvering clearances for a forward approach must be provided.\" Maneuvering clearances include the full width of the door. They are also required at power-assisted doors but not at full-powered automatic or low-energy power-operated doors. The floor within the clearance may not slope more than 1:48.</p><ul><li>A → The recess triggers the forward-approach requirement.</li><li>C → An automatic operator is one way to remove maneuvering clearances, but it is not required. Providing forward-approach clearance complies.</li><li>D → A turning space is a separate requirement, for example inside accessible toilet rooms. It is not the door-maneuvering rule.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.497 (maneuvering clearances; recessed door, 18 in./8 in. rule; 1:48), PDF p.484 (13.17 clear floor space slope 1:48), PDF p.500 (turning space required within accessible toilet rooms)</p>"},
{id:"q155",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 4.1",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Documenting Rated Partitions and Penetrations</p><p>An architect is preparing the partition plans and details for a 1-hour fire-resistance-rated corridor wall on a metal-stud project. Check the THREE approaches supported by your study materials.</p>",opts:["Use shading or hatching on the plans to indicate the fire-resistive wall, with a legend.","Identify the partition type on the drawings and detail a tested assembly, such as one layer of 5/8-in. Type X gypsum board each side of 3-5/8-in. metal studs at 24 in. o.c. (one-hour listing in AGS Table 3.2).","Note penetrations generically and reference the firestopping section, for example \"Provide firestopping at wall penetration — see Section 07 84 00,\" with systems tested to ASTM E 814.","Put the complete firestop product data and installation instructions in a drawing note at each penetration, so the specs don't need a firestopping section.","Use regular (non-Type X) 1/2-in. gypsum board in a single layer each side, because any gypsum board partition carries a 1-hour rating."],c:[0,1,2],e:"<ul><li>Shading or hatching on a drawing is a standard way to show a fire-resistive wall.</li><li>The tested assembly should be identified and detailed. The AGS one-hour metal-stud table includes \"one layer 5/8-in. Type X gypsum board ... each side of 3-5/8-in. metal studs, 24 in. o.c.\"</li><li>For penetrations, CSI explicitly allows a drawing note referencing a specific section (\"see specification Section 07 84 00\"), while product and installation requirements stay in the specifications.</li><li>AGS notes that firestopping is rated by F- and T-ratings under ASTM E 814. Reducing the number of penetrations reduces firestopping, fire safing, and fire damper costs.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.231 (5.2.4.3 Graphics: shading/hatching to indicate a fire-resistive wall), PDF p.267 (Section 07 84 00 firestopping reference example); Architectural Graphic Standards, PDF p.205 (Table 3.2, Metal-Frame Gypsum Board Partitions, one-hour), PDF p.206 (Penetration Firestopping; ASTM E 814)</p>"},
{id:"q156",d:"PDD",o:"PDD 2.5",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Shop Drawings, Coordination Drawings, and Record Drawings</p><p>During construction of a hospital addition, three documents are in circulation:</p><pre class=\"qtab\">(1) A steel fabricator's piece drawings showing every member and connection\n(2) A contractor-prepared overlay of HVAC, plumbing, sprinkler, electrical, and telecom routing in a tight corridor ceiling\n(3) A marked-up set showing field changes and actual locations of diagrammatic items, to be turned over to the owner at closeout</pre><p>Which statement is correct?</p>",opts:["(1) is a contract document once the architect stamps it \"reviewed,\" and it then takes precedence over the structural drawings.","(1) is a shop drawing, which is not a contract document and is reviewed for conformance with the design concept only. (2) is a coordination drawing. (3) is the record drawings.","(2) is a supplemental drawing issued by the architect to change the work. (3) is a resource drawing that becomes part of the contract.","(1) and (2) are both record drawings, because they reflect actual construction."],c:[1],e:"<p>Shop drawings are prepared by the contractor, subcontractor, or supplier to show how part of the work will be fabricated and installed. They \"are not contract documents.\" The architect reviews them \"for conformance with the design concept only,\" and the review does not extend to fabrication processes, field techniques, or coordination of trades. The contractor develops coordination drawings for congested areas such as a small ceiling cavity with multiple systems. Record drawings are contract drawings the contractor modifies to show changes made during construction and actual locations of diagrammatic items. They are given to the owner for operation, maintenance, and future modifications.</p><ul><li>A → Architect review does not turn a shop drawing into a contract document.</li><li>C → Supplemental drawings are architect-issued and often clouded. Resource drawings, such as record drawings of existing structures, are not included in the contract.</li><li>D → Shop and coordination drawings are not record drawings.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PDPG3e_digital, PDF p.232 (5.2.6.1 construction vs. resource drawings), PDF p.233 (5.2.6.2 Construction-Related Drawings; 5.2.6.3 Project Record Drawings), PDF p.350 (8.6.3 shop drawings as submittals)</p>"},
{id:"q157",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 3.2",t:"mc",s:"<p>Architectural practice is dictated by a wide variety of legal requirements and codes. Which of the following is LESS LIKELY to be a direct legal requirement imposed on the architect's practice?</p>",opts:["Building Codes & Standards","The AIA contract between the Owner and the Architect","The OSHA rules defining the construction means and methods","Local ordinances"],c:[2],e:"<p>Under a typical contract, architects are not responsible for the means and methods of construction. OSHA rules for construction site safety govern the contractor, not the architect.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AHPP (chapter/section not specified on page)</p>"},
{id:"q158",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.3",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">AIA Code of Ethics Structure</p><p>The structure of the AIA Code of Ethics includes three tiers:<br>Match each statement to the correct tier:</p>",items:["Architects shall not engage in harassment or discrimination in their professional activities on the basis of race, religion, national origin, age, disability, caregiver status, gender, gender identity, or sexual orientation.","Architects are expected to do the right thing professionally, even challenged by the opposition, and present fairness while dealing with clients, contractors, or consultants.","Architects should consider the impacts of their design and building activities on the environment and promote sustainable design principles. They should consider public interest while doing their professional work and hold their integrity. The client relations should be professional and free of prejudice."],opts:["Canons (C)","Ethical Standards (ES)","Rules of Conduct (RC)"],c:[2,1,0],e:"<p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Chapter 1, Section 1.1</p>"},
{id:"q159",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.1",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Strategic Planning vs. Design Phases</p><p>Match each strategic planning stage to the corresponding architectural design phase (Programming, Schematic Design, Construction Documentation, Construction Administration):</p>",items:["Strategic plans are put to the test during the implementation stage. All the goals, ideals, and details come into play and realize themselves.","Defining the firm's objectives and deciding the long-term goals is similar to the ……… phase of the design process.","At this stage of the strategic planning, all the opportunities and the problems that the firm can potentially have along the way are on the table.","Once the targets are set, the strategic plan should assign roles to the parties and set deadlines to achieve those goals and details."],opts:["Construction Administration","Schematic Design","Programming","Construction Documentation"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Chapter 5, Section 5.3, Table 5.2</p>"},
{id:"q160",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 4.2",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">AIA B101-2017 Basic Services</p><p>Project: New restaurant. Owner hires own kitchen consultant and interior designer. Architect provides structural, MEP engineering per B101-2017.</p><p>Which THREE of the following are within the architect's basic services?</p>",opts:["The architect should provide the schedule of performances for the owner's approval, right after signing the contract.","The architect should provide the schedule of values for the owner's approval, right after signing the contract.","The architect is responsible for the accuracy of the information furnished by all consultants.","The architect should assist the owner in filing for the required permits required by the local authorities.","The architect should manage the coordination between the interior designer and the kitchen consultant.","The architect should consider sustainable design alternatives while selecting the materials."],c:[0,3,5],e:"<p>References:</p><ul><li>A: B101-2017, Article 3.1.3</li><li>D: B101-2017, Article 3.1.6</li><li>F: B101-2017, Article 3.2.5.1</li></ul>"},
{id:"q161",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 3.3",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">MBE Firm Collaboration Benefits</p><p>A small MBE-certified firm (8 employees) signs a teaming agreement with a large firm (56 employees) on a public project. Which TWO are potential benefits for the MBE firm?</p>",opts:["The MBE firm will share the incentives with the larger firm, however, their overhead expenses will be significantly lower than usual.","Due to being part of a large public project, the small firm will expand its portfolio, improve the signed contract sum, and increase its knowledge base.","If the large firm hires all team members of the MBE firm as independent contractors, the MBE firm will reduce labor & overhead expenses significantly and provide a much more secure job environment.","By collaborating with the large firm, the MBE firm can provide a more stabilized workload to its team.","If the MBE firm wishes to increase their chances to be part of larger projects, they can ask to be the main decision-maker and warranty their best effort."],c:[1,3],e:"<p>Three main benefits of this type of collaboration: Project Acquisition, Flexible Staffing, Learning Opportunities</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Chapter 5, Section 5.7</p>"},
{id:"q162",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 3.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Origin of Fast-Tracking Approach</p><p>Which historical event most likely catalyzed the creation of the fast-tracking approach?</p>",opts:["In the 1980s, the liability crisis made owners increasingly interested in single entities delivering both design and construction.","In the last 20 years, increased legal and financial risks led to the fast-tracking approach.","As buildings got more complex from the early 1980s onward, owners became more interested in collaboration and information sharing.","In the 1970s, the cost of borrowing money got higher, causing owners to seek faster return on investment rather than just the lowest first cost."],c:[3],e:"<p>High borrowing costs in the 1970s pushed owners to deliver buildings faster to service debt quicker, leading to fast-tracking.</p><ul><li>Option A → triggered demand for design-build</li><li>Option C → triggered integrated project delivery</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Chapter 9, Section 9.1</p>"},
{id:"q163",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 3.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Project Delivery Method Selection</p><p>Owner's priorities: risk-sharing, top construction quality, multiple prime contracts with best-in-class contractors, professional guidance.</p><p>Diagram options (A=Architect, O=Owner, C=Contractor, CM=Construction Manager):<br>1 = CM-Constructor<br>2 = Integrated Project Delivery<br>3 = CM-Advisor<br>4 = Standard Design-Build</p><p>Which project delivery method is most advisable?</p>",opts:["Option 1 (CM-Constructor)  ✗","Option 2 (Integrated Project Delivery)  ✗","Option 3 (CM-Advisor)","Option 4 (Standard Design-Build)  ✗"],c:[2],e:"<p>Key factors: Risk (primary) + Quality (secondary) + multiple prime contracts + professional advisor = CM-Advisor</p><ul><li>CM-Constructor: primary driver is time; uses single construction contract</li><li>IPD: reduces risk but doesn't address multiple prime contracts with an advisor</li><li>Design-Build: risk + cost (not quality); single construction contract</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Chapter 9, Section 9.1, Figure 9.2</p>"},
{id:"q164",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Solvency Ratio</p><p>A firm has a solvency ratio of 2.4 and $1,888,800 in total current assets. What is the total current liabilities?</p><p>Formula: Solvency Ratio = Total Current Assets / Total Current Liabilities</p>",opts:["$4,533,120","$1,925,800","$1,050,000","$787,000"],c:[3],e:"<p>Calculation: $1,888,800 ÷ 2.4 = $787,000</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Chapter 7, Section 7.2 (Backgrounder)</p>"},
{id:"q165",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Financial Tool Identification</p><p>Which financial tool is described by all three of the following?</p><ul><li>One of the most important financial resources available to an architectural firm</li><li>Is the basis of the accounting data-entry process; affects project profit or loss</li><li>Tracks the effort spent on each project</li></ul>",opts:["The Balance Sheet","The Chart of Accounts","The Timesheets","The Profit-Loss Statement"],c:[2],e:"<p>Timesheets define hours billed to the client → creates revenue → determines project profit/loss. Also allows PMs to track time/budget remaining, and enables calculation of break-even/multiplier rates.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Chapter 7, Section 7.3</p>"},
{id:"q166",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.3",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Annual Budget: Revenue Section</p><p>An architecture firm's annual budget has four sections: Revenue / Fixed Expenses / Variable Expenses / Miscellaneous Revenue &amp; Expenses.</p><p>Which FOUR line items belong to the REVENUE section?</p>",opts:["Direct Labor Salary  ✗ (Fixed Expenses)","Payroll Taxes  ✗ (Fixed Expenses)","Fees Billed","Office Lease & Maintenance Services  ✗ (Fixed Expenses)","Billed Reimbursable Expenses","Consultant Expenses","Project Related Expenses","Marketing & Business Development Expenses  ✗ (Variable Expenses)"],c:[2,4,5,6],e:"<p>Anything billed to the client = Revenue. Architects mark up consultant and project expenses, bill to client, and pay consultants from those funds. Revenue section shows billed items as (+) and expenses paid out as (–).</p><p>Incorrect placements:</p><ul><li>Direct Labor Salary → Fixed Expenses</li><li>Payroll Taxes → Fixed Expenses</li><li>Office Lease &amp; Maintenance → Fixed Expenses</li><li>Marketing &amp; Business Development → Variable Expenses</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Chapter 7, Section 7.4</p>"},
{id:"q167",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Profit-Loss from Operations</p><p>Given budget line items (from table):</p><ul><li>A: Fees Billed = $745,000</li><li>B: Reimbursable Expenses = $92,000</li><li>C: Outside Consultants = $417,000</li><li>D: Project Related Expenses = $68,000</li><li>E: Direct Labor Salary = $194,000</li><li>F: Total Indirect Expenses = $45,000</li></ul><p>What is the profit-loss from operations?</p>",opts:["$99,000","$113,000","$122,000","$181,000"],c:[1],e:"<p>Calculation:<br>Total Net Operating Revenue = A + B – C – D = $745,000 + $92,000 – $417,000 – $68,000 = $352,000<br>Total Expenses = E + F = $194,000 + $45,000 = $239,000<br>Profit-Loss from Operations = $352,000 – $239,000 = $113,000</p><p>Note: On exam, enter numeric value only (e.g., 113000), no commas or dollar signs.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Chapter 7, Section 7.4, Tables 7.5–7.9</p>"},
{id:"q168",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Utilization Rate</p><p>Revenue projection table for 2022. All employees work 40 hrs/week for 52 weeks (no holidays/PTO).</p><p>For employee S. Ride:</p><ul><li>Hourly rate: $47/hour</li><li>Indirect labor: $14,664</li></ul><p>What is S. Ride's utilization rate?</p>",opts:["82%","85%","89%"],c:[1],e:"<p>Calculation:<br>Total annual hours = 52 weeks × 40 hrs/week = 2,080 hours<br>Total Labor (S. Ride) = 2,080 × $47 = $97,760<br>Direct Labor = $97,760 – $14,664 = $83,096<br>Utilization Rate = $83,096 / $97,760 = 0.85 = 85%</p><p>Formula: Utilization Rate = Direct Labor / Total Labor</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Chapter 7, Section 7.4, Table 7.10</p>"},
{id:"q169",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.2",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Professional Liability Insurance Selection</p><p>A recently licensed architect is starting her own practice and shopping for professional liability insurance. Which THREE criteria are commonly advised?</p>",opts:["Since she's just starting out and doesn't have many clients yet, choosing a cheaper option would be wise.","The architect should hire an experienced broker and collect multiple quotes from different firms.","Selecting widely used and highly reviewed insurance plans would be advisable.","According to AIA, all architects must carry professional liability insurance to start their practice, so she should select the best coverage ASAP.","Selecting the insurance company with the longest history in the market is the most important factor.","Depending on their potential projects, the architect can list their priorities and select coverage accordingly."],c:[1,2,5],e:"<p>Key points:</p><ul><li>Professional liability insurance is NOT required by AIA; it is good practice but not mandated</li><li>Cheapest option is not advised</li><li>Longevity in market alone does not ensure good service</li><li>Important factors: customer service ratings, user reviews, reputation, specific knowledge in architectural field</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Chapter 16, Section 16.2</p>"},
{id:"q170",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 4.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Evidence-Based Design</p><p>A large firm sets up a research department for evidence-based design. Which indicates responsible EBD?</p>",opts:["The research department should entirely rely on the firm's knowledge of past design experiences on similar projects.  ✗","The research department should encourage designers to follow their intuition shaped through experience.  ✗","The research department should pre-determine the research results so the design has clear direction from the start.  ✗","Following occupancy, the research department should rigorously evaluate completed buildings to analyze their condition and use this data to inform future projects."],c:[3],e:"<p>EBD uses evidence (not intuition or experience alone) to answer design problems. Pre-determining results is unacceptable — results must be open-ended. Post-occupancy evaluation of completed buildings is a valid EBD process.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Chapter 14, Section 14.4</p>"},
{id:"q171",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Risk Management Strategy</p><p>A small firm owner prefers clients with good reputations and focuses on residential projects where he has the most experience.</p><p>The four risk management strategies are: Avoid Risk / Transfer Risk / Assume Risk / Control Risk</p><p>Which strategy is this firm owner using?</p>",opts:["Avoid Risk","Transfer Risk","Assume Risk","Control Risk"],c:[0],e:"<p>By selecting reputable clients and projects within his expertise, the owner avoids risks before they arise.</p><p>Other strategies:</p><ul><li>Transfer Risk: using contracts to transfer responsibility to sub-consultants</li><li>Assume Risk: maintaining cash reserves for potential lawsuits/insurance deductibles</li><li>Control Risk: regularly educating staff to keep risk under control</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Chapter 16, Section 16.1</p>"},
{id:"q172",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.1",t:"mc",s:"<p>An owner who decides the design-build method for her project selects the best-value project procurement method and issues documents to the potential candidates. Which of the following documents would the owner put out first to gather information about the candidates' technical eligibility?</p>",opts:["RFP","RFQ","QBS","GMP"],c:[1],e:"<p>In the best value procurement method, the owner first issues RFQs and reviews the response of the firms to decide whether they qualify for the job or not. Once the owner evaluates the technical qualifications of the firms, they announce a short-list and request a response for proposals (RFP).</p><p>Notes:</p><ul><li>QBS: Qualifications-based selection, a method used in traditional design-bid-build projects by owners to select the most qualified architect.</li><li>GMP: Guaranteed Maximum Price</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 9, Section 9.4</p>"},
{id:"q173",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.1",t:"mc",s:"<p>A large architectural firm wants to acquire a small architecture firm and review its financial documents and reports. Based on the table [financials provided], what is the profit-to-earnings ratio of this firm?</p>",opts:["58%","67%","72%","85%"],c:[0],e:"<p>Profit to Earnings Ratio = Net Profit / Net Operating Revenue</p><p>Total Net Operating Revenue = Fees Billed – Consultant Expenses = $356,000 – $178,000 = $178,000<br>Profit/Loss From Operations = $178,000 – $66,000 – $9,000 = $103,000<br>Profit to Earnings Ratio = $103,000 / $178,000 = 0.58 = 58%</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 7, Section 7.2</p>"},
{id:"q174",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.1",t:"mc",s:"<p>During the regular site visit of an office project, the architect gets suspicious of the quality and correctness of some of the structural work completed by the contractor. Before deciding whether or not to reject the work, the architect contacts the structural engineer and requests their assistance on-site to verify the compliance of the work with structural drawings. Following the site visit, the structural engineer submits an additional payment request to the architect related to the site visit.</p><p>According to AIA Document C401-2017, which of the following statements is correct in this scenario?</p>",opts:["The structural engineer is entitled to additional payment, and the architect should cover it.","The structural engineer is entitled to additional payment, and the contractor should cover it.","The structural engineer can only be entitled to additional payment if the uncovered work doesn't comply with the project documents. In that case, the cost should be covered by the owner.","The structural engineer is not entitled to an additional payment."],c:[3],e:"<p>According to AIA Document C401-2017, Article 3.6, it is part of the structural engineer's basic scope of services to assist the architect in deciding whether or not to reject the work that the architect thinks may not comply with the project. Therefore, they are not entitled to an additional payment.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA Document C401-2017</p>"},
{id:"q175",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.1",t:"cata",s:"<p>A firm that has been producing successful BIM projects has a well-developed BIM execution plan. Which of the following statements regarding their BIM execution plan is more likely to be correct? Check the THREE that apply.</p>",opts:["The level of development of the virtual model may be indistinct while the execution plan is written.","The execution plan should clearly define the goals for the design team, the owner, and the contractor.","A project directory should be included in the execution plan.","The execution plan should assign specific roles like scheduling, coordination, energy analysis to responsible parties.","Traditional project delivery methods cannot be used while using BIM technology; therefore, the execution plan should exclude conventional delivery methods by default.","Since BIM allows the design team to implement changes in a project at a higher speed, the execution plan should allocate less time for it."],c:[1,2,3],e:"<ul><li>The level of development must be determined EARLY ON — when it's not defined at the beginning, problems arise later.</li><li>BIM can be delivered via ANY project delivery method (methods may need modification, but aren't excluded).</li><li>BIM doesn't equate to a faster design process — this is a common misconception.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 10, Section 10.4</p>"},
{id:"q176",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.1",t:"mc",s:"<p>Tables 1 &amp; 2 [project budget tables for a residential project] show the workload distribution. Based on the information given, how much money is available for the design development phase of this project?</p>",opts:["$195,000","$180,000","$70,000","$55,000"],c:[3],e:"<p>Project Labor Budget = Gross Fee – Consultants Fee – Non-Reimbursable Expenses – Contingency</p><p>Consultant Fees = 50% × $500,000 = $250,000<br>Contingency = 3% × $500,000 = $15,000<br>Project Labor Budget = $500,000 – $250,000 – $15,000 – $15,000 = $220,000</p><p>DD Phase = 25% of $220,000 = $55,000</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 10, Section 10.3, Tables 10.6 &amp; 10.7</p>"},
{id:"q177",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.4",t:"mc",s:"<p>A successful fast-food restaurant owner wishes to scale his business by adding three more locations. Instead of the architect who designed the first restaurant, the owner approaches a different architect to design these three new restaurants with the same design elements and concept. What is the FIRST thing the architect should do to start the project process?</p>",opts:["Visit the existing restaurant to understand the design concept and start discussing program requirements.","Visit the new restaurant locations to verify local zoning code and ADA requirements.","Ask the client to provide in writing that the architect of the first restaurant has transferred the copyright of the design to the owner.","Reach out to the designer of the original restaurant to get information regarding finishes, furniture, and other design elements."],c:[2],e:"<p>Before anything else, the architect must ensure – in writing – that the owner has the permission/blessing of the original designer. Copyright must be addressed first.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 5, Section 5.1</p>"},
{id:"q178",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.2",t:"match",s:"<p>In a project where sustainable design is the goal, match the following roles and responsibilities to the associated team member.</p>",items:["Purchasing required construction materials and coordinating the recycling process during construction","Overseeing the project and specifying sustainable materials","Deciding the implementation costs and providing information regarding building usage","Design of sustainable building systems"],opts:["THE CONTRACTOR","THE ARCHITECT","THE OWNER","THE ENGINEER"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 15, Section 15.1</p>"},
{id:"q179",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.1",t:"cata",s:"<p>An owner discusses the pros and cons of the Construction Manager-at-Risk (CM-at-Risk) delivery method with an architect. Which of the following could be highlighted as ADVANTAGES of the CM-at-Risk project delivery method? Check the THREE that apply.</p>",opts:["The owner can get the lowest cost with the most straightforward delivery method in the market.","During development of AE drawings, the CM can provide accurate constructibility information that would decrease the number of RFIs during construction.","The CM-at-Risk delivery method might be a good solution if the owner wants fast project delivery with multiple bid packages.","In the CM-at-Risk with a fast-track schedule, the architect's services are very similar to those expected in the traditional delivery method.","The experience of a construction manager always guarantees the owner the lowest cost possible.","If multiple bid packages are required, the CM can provide valuable insight to the AE team to increase their efficiency.","Since the CM will be involved from start to finish, the owner can expect to pay the architect less during the construction administration phase."],c:[1,2,5],e:"<ul><li>\"Lowest cost / simplest method\" = traditional design-bid-build, NOT CM-at-Risk.</li><li>Architect's services in CM-at-Risk fast-track typically EXCEED basic services (multiple bid packages).</li><li>Never \"always\" guarantee lowest cost — avoid absolute terms.</li><li>Architect's CA services don't decrease just because a CM is on board; in complex projects they often increase.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 9, Section 9.2</p>"},
{id:"q180",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.1",t:"mc",s:"<p>An architect and owner sign AIA Document B101-2017 for a new residential project. After paying off consultants, the architectural firm assumes a $85,500 stipulated sum fee and expects to finish in eight weeks. They complete the project two weeks early. If the initial profit rate was 20%, what is the profit rate after the early delivery?</p>",opts:["15%","25%","30%","40%"],c:[3],e:"<p>Explanation (Short Method):</p><ul><li>Project total = 100X; expense = 80X; profit = 20X</li><li>Spent over 8 weeks → weekly cost = 10X</li><li>Finished in 6 weeks → total expense = 60X</li><li>New profit = 40X → 40%</li></ul><p>Explanation (Long Method):</p><ul><li>Original profit: 20% × $85,500 = $17,100</li><li>Total expenses expected: $85,500 – $17,100 = $68,400</li><li>Weekly spending: $68,400 / 8 = $8,550</li><li>Actual spending (6 weeks): $8,550 × 6 = $51,300</li><li>New profit: $85,500 – $51,300 = $34,200</li><li>New profit rate: $34,200 / $85,500 = 40%</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Professional Practice: A Guide to Turning Designs into Buildings, Paul Segal, FAIA, Chapter 6</p>"},
{id:"q181",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.4",t:"mc",s:"<p>An architect and owner sign AIA Document B101-2017 for a new residential project. The architect provides the owner with a preliminary cost estimate. Based on AIA contract and the list of items given, what is the cost of the work of this project?</p>",opts:["$10,100,000","$11,000,000","$13,600,000","$14,997,000"],c:[1],e:"<p>Per AIA Document B101-2017, Article 6.1, the cost of the work includes total cost of all material, labor, and elements required to build the project. It does NOT include: architect's fees, cost of land, right-of-way, financing, or contingencies.</p><p>Cost of Work = $9,800,000 + $1,200,000 (owner-provided labor &amp; material) = $11,000,000</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA Document B101-2017, Article 6.1</p>"},
{id:"q182",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.2",t:"match",s:"<p>There are four main approaches to ethics. Match the relevant statements to one of the ethical approaches.</p>",items:["Architects have responsibilities toward their clients, the future users of the building, and the environment","Architects practicing in the US are obliged to create buildings that comply with the Americans with Disabilities Act laws","Architects should consider the consequences of their designs and make sure the outcome creates a happy environment for the majority of users","When met with opposition or under stress from clients or contractors, architects have to display self-control"],opts:["DUTY-BASED ETHICS","CONTRACT-BASED ETHICS","RESULTS-BASED ETHICS","CHARACTER-BASED ETHICS"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 1, Section 1.2</p>"},
{id:"q183",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.4",t:"mc",s:"<p>An architectural office is working on RFPs of four potential residential projects with similar total building areas. Which project should be compensated the most?</p><p>Project A: Client has finished many similar projects but no prior work history with this firm. Traditional design-bid-build delivery.<br>Project B: Design-build firm the architect worked with before, but not experienced in residential.<br>Project C: Client wants to alter contract documents to increase the standard of care significantly. Owner also wants fast-track scheduling.<br>Project D: Client has prior work history with firm and is experienced in residential, but sometimes delays payments.</p>",opts:["Project A","Project B","Project C","Project D"],c:[2],e:"<p>Risk factors that increase compensation:</p><ul><li>Unknown client</li><li>New project type</li><li>Fast-track schedule ← HIGH RISK</li><li>Owner heightened standard of care ← HIGH RISK</li><li>Owner delays payments</li></ul><p>Project C has TWO of the highest-risk factors (fast-track + increased standard of care), making it the riskiest and warranting the highest fee.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 15, Section 15.1</p>"},
{id:"q184",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 3.2",t:"mc",s:"<p>The architect of a very complex hospital project tries to convince the owner to<br>consider the IPD method for project delivery. The owner is reluctant to<br>investigate new delivery methods and is very conservative in that sense. Which<br>of the following options would represent the most helpful characteristics of the<br>IPD method to convince the client?</p>",opts:["In the IPD method, the early involvement of the stakeholders in the process increases collaboration and aligns the stakeholders' goals. This provides predictability to the client, which equates significant reduction of the risk for better value.","The IPD method provides the flexibility to the owner to change their minds later in the process.","The IDP method provides the lowest possible bid and promises a very straightforward project experience to owners.","In the IPD method, the number of agreements and the amount of work required to organize the team is the lowest."],c:[0],e:"<p>According to The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 9,<br>Section 9.3, the IDP method has potential benefits to the owners:</p><ul><li>The early involvement of the stakeholders increases the collaboration. It also</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">aligns the goals/benefits of all of them. Everyone works as an equal part of\nthe team, which improves collaboration. This significantly reduces the risk for\nthe clients (which also equates to increased value for the owners).</pre><ul><li>A better project experience for the owner.</li><li>The owner can change the design later down the road (a positive characteristic</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">of IPD too, but option A retains more power to convince the client).</pre><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, AIA, John Wiley &amp; Sons, 15th edition (2014)</p>"},
{id:"q185",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 3.2",t:"mc",s:"<p>At the end of the Construction Documentation phase of a project, the architect<br>provides a cost of work estimate to the owner in writing, but the owner never<br>proceeds to the project procurement phase. Four months later, the owner reaches<br>out to the architect and informs her that they are ready to go for bidding.</p><p>According to AIA Document B101-2017, what is the next thing the architect should<br>do, following this?</p>",opts:["The architect should help the owner to create a list of contractors.","The architect should review the bids with the owner and help the owner obtain the best value.","The architect should revise the construction cost estimate.","The architect should distribute the project documents to prospective bidders."],c:[2],e:"<p>According to AIA Document B101-2017, Article 6.4, if the project procurement<br>phase does not occur within 90 days after the architect submits their final<br>construction work cost estimate, the architect should revise the estimate to<br>reflect the changes in prices in the current construction market.</p>"},
{id:"q186",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 3.2",t:"cata",s:"<p>An architect hires an MEP engineer for his new project and signs the consultant's<br>AIA Contract C 401-2017. According to the prime contract between the owner and<br>the architect, the project delivery process is expedited, and the standard of<br>care expectancy is heightened.</p><p>Based on this information, select the correct statements regarding the<br>relationship between the architect and the mechanical engineer?<br>(Check the two that apply.)</p>",opts:["The engineer is responsible for coordinating their drawings with other consultants of the architect and can directly communicate with the other consultants to do so.","","The engineer is expected to perform its services consistent with the professional skill and care ordinarily provided by professionals practicing in the same or similar locality under the same or similar circumstances.","","When the cost of the work exceeds the owner's budget, as an additional service to the architect, the engineer should make revisions to the project's size or quality to reduce the cost."],c:[1,3],e:"<p>Correct Answers:</p><ul><li>B: AIA Document C401-2017, Article 1.3.</li><li>D: AIA Document C401-2017, Article 2.8.</li></ul><p>Incorrect Answers:</p><ul><li>A: Per AIA C401-2017, Article 1.5, all communications between consultants,</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">between consultant and owner or contractor should be orchestrated by the\narchitect. The architect is the administrator of the professional services.</pre><ul><li>C: Per AIA C401-2017, Article 2.1 &amp; 3.1, the consultant should provide a</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">service consistent with the standard of care in the prime agreement. Since\nthe Prime Contract is adjusted, the consultant should follow that and cannot\noffer the same level of standard of care as colleagues in the same area.</pre><ul><li>E: Per AIA C401-2017, Article 6.3, this is part of the consultant's basic</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">services, not additional services.</pre>"},
{id:"q187",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 4.2",t:"cata",s:"<p>A large architecture firm compares two different techniques for quality<br>management purposes, such as \"inspection-based\" and \"process-based\" methods.<br>Which of the following statements are correct for these two methods?<br>(Check the two that apply.)</p>",opts:["","CAD technologies made it so much simpler to implement inspection-based quality management methods, therefore it is the best tool for larger firms.","","For projects with more complex building types and faster delivery processes, the inspection-based quality management method is preferred."],c:[0,2],e:"<p>Incorrect Answers:</p><ul><li>B: Per The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 12, Section</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">12.1, the inspection-based quality management method was much better while\nfirms were using hand-drafting rather than CAD technologies. With CAD, the\nsenior team red-lines drawings after they are completed, making it easier to\nmiss mistakes.</pre><ul><li>D: Per The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 12, Section</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">12.1, process-based systems work better with complex projects with fast\ndelivery processes.</pre><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 12, Section 12.1</p>"},
{id:"q188",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.1",t:"mc",s:"<p>Architectural firms usually fit into categories that highlight their core values,<br>services, and typical client types. Which of the following categories defines an<br>architectural office owned by a celebrity architect and recently hired by a newly<br>elected mayor to deliver an eccentric and world-renowned concert hall?</p>",opts:["Expertise","Experience","Execution","Business-centered"],c:[0],e:"<p>Key phrases: \"celebrity architect\" and \"world-renowned concert hall.\"<br>According to Professional Practice: A Guide to Turning Designs into Buildings,<br>Chapter 9, the categories are:</p><ul><li>Expertise: Firms owned by celebrity architects who can deliver unusual,</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">atypical designs.</pre><ul><li>Experience: Firms experienced in certain building types (schools, residential,</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">hospitals). Clients who don't want risk prefer these firms.</pre><ul><li>Execution: Firms that specialize in delivering projects that are very hard for</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">typical offices and require specific execution techniques or have very\nchallenging design elements, schedules, or budgets.</pre><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Professional Practice: A Guide to Turning Designs into Buildings, Paul Segal, FAIA, W. W. Norton, 2006</p>"},
{id:"q189",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.2",t:"match",s:"<p>Match the following insurance terms to the correct definitions given below<br>accordingly. (Drag &amp; drop — Not all the terms will be used. Drop the irrelevant<br>term across the letter X.)</p>",items:["The insurance company that shares or covers the risks","The amount of money that firms pay each year to stay insured","The maximum amount retrievable from the insurance company that collects and redistributes the money","The process by which the insurance company evaluates the risks involved in a business","The amount of money that the firm owners are responsible for paying toward an insured loss"],opts:["Carrier","Premium","Coverage","Policy","Deductible","Underwriting"],c:[0,1,2,5,4],e:"<p>A Policy is an agreement between the insurance company and the insured firm<br>(not a process of evaluating risks).</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Professional Practice: A Guide to Turning Designs into Buildings, Chapter 10, Paul Segal, FAIA, W. W. Norton, 2006</p>"},
{id:"q190",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.2",t:"mc",s:"<p>The owner and the architect of a school project agree to use the traditional<br>project delivery method. The owner collects five bids at the end of the bidding<br>process. However, the company that provides the lowest bid decides to walk away<br>from the agreement. If the bid bond of this project was set at 10% in the<br>bidding documents, would that be enough to cover the losses of the project owner<br>fully?</p><pre class=\"qtab\">Bids per Construction Companies:</pre><ul><li>Titanic Construction Group      – $340,000</li><li>RMS Contracting                 – $325,000</li><li>White Star Line Construction    – $365,000</li><li>Olympic Builders &amp; Contractors  – $296,000  ← Lowest bid (walked away)</li><li>Carpathia Construction          – $374,000</li></ul>",opts:["The bid bond is not enough to fully cover the owner's loss.","The bid bond is enough to fully cover the owner's loss.","The bid bond is $600 shy of fully covering the owner's loss.","If the contractor walks away from the agreement, the surety or the contractor is not responsible for fully or partially covering the owner's losses."],c:[1],e:"<p>Per Professional Practice: A Guide to Turning Designs into Buildings, Chapter 5<br>(Bidding &amp; Negotiations): if the contractor of the lowest bid does not get into<br>an agreement, the owner can claim the difference between the lowest and<br>second-lowest bids from the surety company.</p><pre class=\"qtab\">Lowest bid: $296,000 | Second-lowest: $325,000\nDifference = $325,000 – $296,000 = $29,000\nBid bond = 10% × $296,000 = $29,600\n$29,600 &gt; $29,000 → The bid bond IS enough to fully cover the loss. ✓</pre><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Professional Practice: A Guide to Turning Designs into Buildings, Paul Segal, FAIA, W. W. Norton, 2006</p>"},
{id:"q191",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.2",t:"cata",s:"<p>An architect who owns a small firm with five employees recently had some<br>experience as a construction manager in some residential projects. Following<br>this, the architect debates about steering the firm toward becoming an<br>architect-led design-build firm. This decision will involve some challenges and<br>opportunities. Which of the following are key factors that should be evaluated<br>by this firm's owner? (Check the three that apply.)</p>",opts:["","","The firm must employ its own construction crew to reduce the liability risk originating from an outside party or business.","","Even if the firm employs its own construction crew, it cannot claim fees for the contractor's overhead or markup on construction labor.","Architect-led design-build firms are more prone to conflict during the life of a project."],c:[0,1,3],e:"<p>Per The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 9, Section 9.5,<br>forming an ALDB firm for a firm with CM-as-architect experience can be a smart<br>business decision. The architect can charge not only design fees but also<br>construction management fees, contractor's overhead, and markup on labor.</p><ul><li>C is incorrect: Other measures (e.g., joint venture partnerships) can reduce</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">liability risk. Avoid options with the word \"must\" — they imply certainty.</pre><ul><li>E is incorrect: If the ALDB firm employs its own crew, they CAN claim markups</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">on construction labor.</pre><ul><li>F is incorrect: Conflict is actually LESS in ALDB structures because the same</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">entity designs and builds.</pre><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, AIA, John Wiley &amp; Sons, 15th edition (2014)</p>"},
{id:"q192",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 3.1",t:"cata",s:"<p>Which of the following parameters are crucial to be listed at the beginning of<br>any agreement between the architect and the owner for a design project?<br>(Check the three that apply.)</p>",opts:["The bank statements of the architect to show that they have enough resources to complete the project.","","","","The means and methods of the construction.","Construction schedule."],c:[1,2,3],e:"<p>The 5 critical parameters (per source) are:</p><pre class=\"qtab\">1. Clearly stating the project scope.\n2. The budget of the project.\n3. The list of consultants that will be hired by the architect.\n4. The full legal names of the parties signing the contract.\n5. Project schedule (different from the construction schedule, which the\n   contractor typically prepares).</pre><p>Incorrect Answers:</p><ul><li>A: The architect is not responsible for payment. The owner pays; the architect</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">may request proof of the client's financial capacity if suspicious.</pre><ul><li>E: The contractor is responsible for means and methods, not the architect.</li><li>F: Project schedule ≠ Construction schedule. Construction schedule is</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">prepared by the contractor.</pre><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Professional Practice: A Guide to Turning Designs into Buildings, Chapter 7, Paul Segal, FAIA, W. W. Norton, 2006</p>"},
{id:"q193",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.1",t:"mc",s:"<p>Based on the statement of professional services submitted by R.E.M. Architects,<br>what is the total due of this statement for the standard services of this firm?</p><pre class=\"qtab\">[Note: This question references a billing statement/table displayed on the\nquiz page showing fees due per phase based on completion percentages.]</pre>",opts:["$289,550","$273,000","$205,000","$173,000"],c:[1],e:"<p>The most important thing to solve this question is to analyze the statement<br>carefully. Once fees due per phase are calculated (based on the completed<br>portions given as percentages), subtract the paid-to-date line from the total<br>fees due to date.</p>"},
{id:"q194",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 3.3",t:"mc",s:"<p>Two years after finishing a house project, the owner informs the architect that<br>there is a mold issue in the basement wall facing north, below the first-floor<br>deck. The architect visits the site and provides the owner with a list of<br>recommendations to remedy the issue. One year after this, the architect finds<br>out that the owner is now suing him to correct the work. What could the<br>architect do differently when he first heard the mold issue to prevent the<br>lawsuit?</p>",opts:["When he first heard of the problem, the architect was supposed to direct the client to the contractor as he was not responsible for the means and methods of the construction.","As part of the standard of care, the architect is responsible to pay all the damages caused by the erroneous work.","The architect could have stayed in touch with the owner after forwarding the recommendations to ensure that the issue was solved and the client was happy.","The architect could hire his own construction crew to uncover the faulty work and fix it immediately to prevent the lawsuit."],c:[2],e:"<p>Per Professional Practice: A Guide to Turning Designs into Buildings, keeping<br>good relationships with clients even after the project is finalized is essential<br>for architects to mitigate the risk of lawsuits. The architect did the right<br>thing by visiting and sending recommendations — but failed to follow up.</p><p>Important note: Architects are not responsible for the means and methods of<br>projects; contractors are. However, the owner, architect, contractor, and<br>sub-contractor should all meet to determine fault before pointing fingers<br>(could be maintenance, installation, or design).</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Professional Practice: A Guide to Turning Designs into Buildings, Paul Segal, FAIA, W. W. Norton, 2006</p>"},
{id:"q195",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.1",t:"mc",s:"<p>The total indirect expenses of an office are $365,000 and the break-even rate<br>is 2.54. What are the total direct labor expenses of this firm?</p>",opts:["$143,701","$237,013","$385,420","$562,100"],c:[1],e:"<pre class=\"qtab\">Break-even Rate = Overhead Rate + 1\n2.54 = Overhead Rate + 1\nOverhead Rate = 1.54</pre><pre class=\"qtab\">Overhead Rate = Total Indirect Expenses / Total Direct Labor (TDL)\n1.54 = $365,000 / TDL\nTDL = $365,000 / 1.54 = $237,013 (rounded)</pre><p>Key concepts:</p><ul><li>Overhead Rate: money spent on items not directly related to the project</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">(indirect expenses).</pre><ul><li>Break-even Rate: cost of operations per every dollar spent on direct labor.</li></ul>"},
{id:"q196",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.1",t:"mc",s:"<p>Based on the given numbers below, what is the return on equity for the<br>stakeholders of an architecture firm?</p><ul><li>Total Equity                  = $1,164,300</li><li>Total Net Operating Revenue   = $758,050</li><li>Total Expenses                = $589,250</li></ul>",opts:["7%","7.5%","13.7%","14.5%"],c:[3],e:"<p>Per The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 7, Section 7.2:</p><pre class=\"qtab\">Return on equity = [(Total Net Operating Revenue − Total Expenses) /\n                    Total Equity] × 100</pre><pre class=\"qtab\">= [($758,050 − $589,250) / $1,164,300] × 100\n= [$168,800 / $1,164,300] × 100\n= 14.5%</pre><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, AIA, John Wiley &amp; Sons, 15th edition (2014)</p>"},
{id:"q197",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.2",t:"cata",s:"<p>Project design time is simply defined by the size and the complexity of the<br>architectural projects. However, some additional factors may be prominent when<br>determining the design time. Which of the following can be considered among<br>those? (Check the three that apply.)</p>",opts:["","","The availability of skilled contractors in the area.","Bidding environment.",""],c:[0,1,4],e:"<ul><li>C is incorrect: Availability of skilled contractors may affect construction</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">time, not design time.</pre><ul><li>D is incorrect: Bidding environment is irrelevant to the design process and</li></ul><pre class=\"qtab\">varies depending on the selected project delivery method.</pre><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, AIA, John Wiley &amp; Sons, 15th edition (2014), Chapter 7, Section 7.1</p>"},
{id:"q198",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.2",t:"cata",s:"<p>Which of the following statements are accurate regarding LLPs and LLCs?<br>(Check the three that apply.)</p>",opts:["Liability is limited with the investment of the owner(s).","It is not perceived as a separate entity by the government, so the business itself is not taxed separately.","The business and the owner(s) are taxed separately, therefore it is taxed double.","Typically, Secretary of State filing is not required.","They must file annual reports, or they risk being dissolved."],c:[0,1,4],e:"<p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice (AIA, Wiley, 15th ed.)</p>"},
{id:"q199",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.2",t:"mc",s:"<p>Which of the following among the three key factors that can be the basis of compensation for architects IS NOT one of them?</p>",opts:["The risk involved in the project","The required effort for the project","The type of agreement used between the client and the architect.","The value of the services"],c:[2],e:"<p>The compensation of the architect is based on the risk, effort, and value of the services. The type of agreement used is not one of the three key factors.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice (AIA, Wiley, 15th ed.)</p>"},
{id:"q200",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.3",t:"mc",s:"<p>Financial planning is a critical part of starting a new firm. Which of the following vital for two partners starting a new small firm IS NOT applicable?</p>",opts:["Selecting and establishing an accounting system.","Setting up a bank account dedicated to the firm.","Setting up tax identification and filing status.","Finding trustworthy advisers, including an accountant and attorney.","Acquiring the consent of stockholders."],c:[4],e:"<p>This is a small firm with two employees — it doesn't have stockholders. Corporate companies are owned by stockholders. All other options are vital for financial planning when starting a new firm (per The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 5, Section 5.2).</p><p>Side Note: NCARB does not ask questions with negative clauses like this. The bonus quizzes use them for deeper study.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice (AIA, Wiley, 15th ed.)</p>"},
{id:"q201",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 3.3",t:"mc",s:"<p>Which of the following is the reason behind a surprising number of legal claims in the construction industry?</p>",opts:["Lack of education of the parties in legal matters.","Failure in proper communication between parties.","Late payments or clients refusing to pay for additional work for unseen conditions.","Very competitive market conditions forcing contractors to bid lower than they can afford."],c:[1],e:"<p>According to The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 5, Section 5.1, effective communication may be the most crucial part of a proper construction project.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice (AIA, Wiley, 15th ed.)</p>"},
{id:"q202",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 4.2",t:"match",s:"<p>Match the payment methods to the following scenarios depending on the priorities of each project. (Drag &amp; drop)</p>",items:["The scope of a project was well defined, and the construction manager as constructor prepared a cost estimate for the owner.","Due to unknown existing site conditions, the architect of a project suggests using this payment type.","The owner of a project didn't know the exact details of the interior design work during the earlier phases.","At the end of a competitive bidding process of a publicly funded project, this is the cost that is agreed upon."],opts:["Guaranteed Maximum Price","Unit Price","Cost Plus Fee","Stipulated Sum"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice (AIA, Wiley, 15th ed.)</p>"},
{id:"q203",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.2",t:"cata",s:"<p>Which of the following legal business structures protects the personal assets of the practice owners?<br>(Check the four that apply.)</p>",opts:["Sole proprietorship","LLC","LLP","General partnership","S-Corporations","Business Corporations"],c:[1,2,4,5],e:"<p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice (AIA, Wiley, 15th ed.)</p>"},
{id:"q204",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 4.1",t:"mc",s:"<p>At the end of the design development phase of a project, the architect, owner, and constructor manager reviewed the cost estimate. Who is \"at risk\" in this scenario?</p>",opts:["The Owner","The Construction Manager","The Architect"],c:[1],e:"<p>According to The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, Chapter 9, Section 9.1, the CM is at risk because they take responsibility for the cost estimate at the earlier phases. If the cost comes higher than expected, the CMc can offer the owner two options to reduce their own risk.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice (AIA, Wiley, 15th ed.)</p>"},
{id:"q205",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.3",t:"cata",s:"<p>Select the main differences between Subchapter S-Corporation and Subchapter C-Corporation.<br>(Check the two that apply.)</p>",opts:["Subchapter C-Corporations file federal returns but don't pay taxes because income/loss is passed through to shareholders.","Unlike Subchapter S-Corporations, in Subchapter C-Corporation, stockholders are double taxed.","In Subchapter S-Corporations, stockholders cannot be more than 100 people, and they all must be US citizens or permanent residents.","In Subchapter C-Corporations, all stockholders must be licensed professionals."],c:[1,2],e:"<p>Explanation of incorrect options:</p><ul><li>A is wrong: It's S-Corporations (not C-Corporations) that file federal returns without paying taxes, as income/loss passes through to shareholders' individual returns.</li><li>D is wrong: In professional corporations, all stockholders must be licensed professionals — this is not a requirement of standard C-Corporations.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice (AIA, Wiley, 15th ed.)</p>"},
{id:"q206",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 4.1",t:"mc",s:"<p>AIA measures continuing education in Learning Units (LUs), and AIA member licensed architects must earn a certain number each year. Select the correct statement below.</p>",opts:["AIA members are required to complete 18 LUs from registered AIA/CES providers each year. 12 of the total LUs must be Health, Safety, and Welfare (HSW) LUs.","AIA members are required to complete 16 LUs from registered AIA/CES providers each year. 10 of the total LUs must be HSW LUs.","AIA members are required to complete 20 LUs from registered AIA/CES providers each year. 14 of the total LUs must be HSW LUs."],c:[0],e:"<p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA Website (aia.org/pages/2696-continuing-education-aia-and-state-requirement)</p>"},
{id:"q207",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.4",t:"mc",s:"<p>What is the main difference between a General Partnership (GP) and a Limited Liability Partnership (LLP)?</p>",opts:["LLPs are more expensive to start up.","Only LLPs file for a federal tax return and pay taxes; GPs do not.","Only GPs file for a federal tax return, but income/losses pass through partners' individual tax returns.","LLPs offer liability protections that are not available to GPs."],c:[3],e:"<p>General Partnership:</p><ul><li>Two or more architects may start one GP, with or without a written agreement.</li><li>Each partner is potentially responsible for the actions of other partners.</li><li>This liability can extend beyond the partnership to personal assets.</li><li>Files federal return but does not pay taxes (income/losses passed through to partners' individual returns).</li></ul><p>Limited Liability Partnership:</p><ul><li>Protects partners' personal assets, providing more liability protection.</li><li>LLP documents include a certificate filed with the Office of the Secretary of State, and they typically must file annual reports or risk dissolution.</li><li>Files federal return but does not pay taxes (income/losses passed through to partners' individual returns).</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice (AIA, Wiley, 15th ed.)</p>"},
{id:"q208",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 4.1",t:"mc",s:"<p>The AIA Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct binds the professional activities of all AIA members. Which of the following IS NOT a penalty applied by AIA?</p>",opts:["Admonition","Censure","Deterrence","Suspension of membership for a period of time.","Termination of membership."],c:[2],e:"<p>Side Note: NCARB does not ask questions with negative clauses. The bonus quizzes use them for deeper study.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: 2020 Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct, AIA Office of General Counsel</p>"},
{id:"q209",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 4.2",t:"mc",s:"<p>In contracts between the architect and the owner, all of the work done by the architect, including drawings, specifications, models, mock-ups, prototypes, and other similar items, is collectively referred to as:</p>",opts:["Project Documents","Instruments of Services","Construction Documents","Project Scope"],c:[1],e:"<p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Professional Practice: A Guide to Turning Designs into Buildings, Chapter 7, Paul Segal, FAIA (W. W. Norton)</p>"},
{id:"q210",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 4.2",t:"mc",s:"<p>\"_______ firms provide services that rely upon extensive knowledge or talent. These are the firms that are owned by the highest-paid professionals in a field.\" Fill in the blank: E……. – Based</p>",opts:["Efficiency-based","Expertise-based","Experience-based","Evaluation-based"],c:[1],e:"<p>Side Note: There is no such thing as an Evaluation-Based firm.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice (AIA, Wiley, 15th ed.)</p>"},
{id:"q211",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.1",t:"match",s:"<p>Match the following financial terms to the correct definitions. (Drag &amp; drop)</p>",items:["Resources of a business that are converted into cash within a year","Expenses incurred to keep a business operating","The ratio of direct labor time to total labor time","Money that others owe to the business for services","The total effort of employees that can be charged to projects","The value of shares of stock, invested capital by shareholders, and total earnings/losses of a firm"],opts:["Current Assets","Overhead","Utilization Ratio","Accounts Receivable","Direct Labor","Equity"],c:[0,1,2,3,4,5],e:"<p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice (AIA, Wiley, 15th ed.)</p>"},
{id:"q212",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.2",t:"cata",s:"<p>A sizeable architectural firm has been in business through many different economic cycles and successfully sustained itself. Which of the following are the most likely reasons for this firm's resilience?<br>(Check the three that apply.)</p>",opts:["Geographic diversification.","Building type variety.","Expanding service range into different areas.","Keeping good relations with existing clients.","Keeping employee morale and motivation high."],c:[0,1,2],e:"<p>Explanations:</p><ul><li>Geographic diversification: Expanding services in different regions and cities to acquire more work.</li><li>Building type variety (Building Diversification): Providing design services in various building types to attract more clients.</li><li>Expanding service range into different areas (Service Diversification): Offering additional services like interior design, cost estimating, or construction management.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice (AIA, Wiley, 15th ed.)</p>"},
{id:"q213",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 1.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Matching staff level to the task</p><p>A 12-person firm is staffing a 40,000 SF branch library. The principal-in-charge, billed at $210/hr, has been drafting wall sections herself because \"it is faster than explaining it.\" Halfway through Design Development the project has used 70% of its fee.</p><p>What is the project manager's best response?</p>",opts:["Ask the owner for additional compensation because the work took longer than expected","Reassign the production drafting to a technical staff member and keep the principal on review and client decisions","Keep the principal on the drafting, since her experience lowers the risk of errors","Cut the remaining Construction Administration hours to recover the overrun"],c:[1],e:"<p>The overrun comes from mismatching staff level to task: a high-rate principal doing production work burns fee faster and lowers the project's net multiplier. Resource management means putting each task at the lowest staff level that can do it well, with senior people reviewing.</p><ul><li>A → Nothing in the scope changed, so there is no basis for additional services; the overrun is the firm's own staffing decision.</li><li>C → Experience does not justify the cost; a review by the principal gives the same protection at a fraction of the hours.</li><li>D → Construction Administration is a contracted service. Cutting it to fix a staffing problem moves the risk into construction, where errors cost most.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 10 (project management); PjM notes, Resource Management</p>"},
{id:"q214",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 1.1",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Selecting consultants</p><p>An architect is assembling consultants for a hospital surgical-suite renovation that must stay in operation during construction.</p><p>Which THREE factors should carry the most weight in selecting the MEP engineer?</p>",opts:["Recent experience with occupied healthcare renovations and infection-control phasing","The lowest fee proposal received","Staff availability for the project schedule, including night and weekend site visits","Familiarity with the state health-department and FGI Guidelines review","The engineer's willingness to work without a written agreement to save time","A previous working relationship with the owner's facilities staff, regardless of project type"],c:[0,2,3],e:"<p>Consultants are selected on qualifications and fit for this project: relevant experience, capacity on the schedule, and knowledge of the regulators who will review the work.</p><ul><li>B → Fee matters, but choosing on price alone is the classic way to end up with an under-resourced consultant on a high-risk project.</li><li>E → Every consultant relationship needs a written agreement (for example AIA C401) so the scope and standard of care flow down from the prime agreement.</li><li>F → A relationship helps only if the experience matches; it is not a substitute for healthcare renovation expertise.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA C401–2017; The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 10</p>"},
{id:"q215",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 2.1",t:"num",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">From fee to labor budget</p><p>An architect's total fee for a project is $300,000. Consultants' fees of $75,000 are passed through without markup. The firm plans a 15% profit on the architect's own portion of the fee, and its net multiplier (direct labor to fee, covering overhead) is 3.0.</p><p>How many dollars of direct labor can the project manager budget? Enter a whole number.</p>",ans:63750,tol:0,unit:"dollars",e:"<p>Work from the architect's portion only:</p><ul><li>Architect's fee = $300,000 − $75,000 consultants = $225,000</li><li>Less 15% profit: $225,000 × 0.85 = $191,250 available for labor and overhead</li><li>Direct labor = $191,250 ÷ 3.0 = $63,750</li></ul><p>Common errors: leaving the consultants in (gives $85,000), or taking profit after dividing by the multiplier. The labor budget is then spread across phases and staff in the work plan.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 7 (financial management) and Ch. 10 (work plans)</p>"},
{id:"q216",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 2.1",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">What belongs in the project work plan</p><p>Match each item to the part of the project work plan where it belongs.</p>",items:["Staff names and hours by phase","Fee allocated to each phase and consultant","Milestones for owner approvals and agency submittals","The list of services in the owner–architect agreement, with what is excluded"],opts:["Staffing plan","Budget","Schedule","Scope"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p>A work plan ties together scope (what the agreement requires), staffing (who does it), budget (fee and hours by phase) and schedule (when). If any one changes, the others must be revisited; that is why the work plan is maintained, not written once.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 10</p>"},
{id:"q217",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 2.2",t:"num",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Earned value check</p><p>The Design Development phase has a labor budget of $80,000. At the monthly review the team reports DD is 45% complete, and $44,000 of labor has been charged to the phase.</p><p>By how many dollars is DD over budget for the work done so far? Enter a whole number.</p>",ans:8000,tol:0,unit:"dollars",e:"<p>Compare what the work done is worth with what it cost:</p><ul><li>Earned value = 45% × $80,000 = $36,000</li><li>Actual cost = $44,000</li><li>Overrun = $44,000 − $36,000 = $8,000</li></ul><p>Comparing spending with time elapsed (or with the whole budget) hides this: $44,000 is only 55% of the budget, which looks fine until you see that only 45% of the work is done. The response is to find the cause (staffing, scope creep, rework) and adjust the plan.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 10 (monitoring the work plan)</p>"},
{id:"q218",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 2.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Schedule float</p><p>In the design schedule for a school, the structural engineer's review of the foundation package has 10 working days of float. The engineer reports the review will take 6 days longer than planned.</p><p>What is the effect on the project completion date?</p>",opts:["It moves 6 working days later","None, as long as no other delay uses up the remaining float on that path","It moves 4 working days later","It moves 10 working days later"],c:[1],e:"<p>Float is the time a task can slip without delaying the project end date. A 6-day delay on a task with 10 days of float leaves 4 days of float and does not move completion, but that path is now closer to critical and needs watching.</p><ul><li>A → That would be true only for a task on the critical path, which has zero float.</li><li>C → 10 − 6 = 4 is the float left, not a delay.</li><li>D → Float is a cushion, not a delay.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 10 (scheduling)</p>"},
{id:"q219",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 2.3",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Who talks to whom during construction</p><p>During construction under AIA A201–2017, the owner's facilities director emails the contractor's superintendent directly, asking for the lobby lighting to be changed to a different fixture.</p><p>What should the architect do?</p>",opts:["Nothing; the owner can direct the contractor on its own building","Remind the parties that communications go through the architect, and process the change with the proper change document","Tell the contractor to install the new fixture and sort out the cost later","Issue a stop-work order for the lobby"],c:[1],e:"<p>A201 has the owner and contractor communicate through the architect about matters arising under the contract, so changes are documented, priced and coordinated. A change in fixtures affects cost and possibly time, so it needs a Change Order or a Construction Change Directive, not an email.</p><ul><li>A → The owner owns the building, but direct instructions bypass the change process and invite disputes.</li><li>C → Work without an agreed change document leaves cost and time open, the main source of claims.</li><li>D → The architect has no general authority to stop the work; that is the owner's right in limited circumstances.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA A201–2017, §4.2.4 (communications) and Article 7 (changes in the work)</p>"},
{id:"q220",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 2.3",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Meeting minutes</p><p>An architect leads a design coordination meeting with the owner and consultants.</p><p>Which THREE practices make the minutes useful as a project record?</p>",opts:["Distribute them promptly to everyone who attended and everyone with an assigned action","Record decisions made, who is responsible for each action, and the due dates","Ask recipients to report corrections by a stated date, after which the minutes stand as written","Write them only if a dispute seems likely","Leave out decisions the owner made reluctantly","Keep them only in the project manager's personal notebook"],c:[0,1,2],e:"<p>Minutes are the written record of decisions and responsibilities. They are useful only if they are complete, distributed promptly, and open to correction for a short period.</p><ul><li>D → Records are kept routinely; deciding case by case means the record is missing exactly when it is needed.</li><li>E → A reluctant decision is still a decision, and it is often the one disputed later.</li><li>F → A record nobody else can see confirms nothing.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 10 (project communication)</p>"},
{id:"q221",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 3.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Services that are not in Basic Services</p><p>Under AIA B101–2017, partway through Design Development the owner asks the architect to prepare three additional site options for a new parking structure the owner has just decided to add.</p><p>What should the architect do before doing the work?</p>",opts:["Start right away so the schedule does not slip, and bill the hours at the end","Notify the owner that this is an Additional Service, and obtain the owner's written authorization before proceeding","Refuse, because the scope in the agreement cannot change","Do the work at no charge to keep the client relationship"],c:[1],e:"<p>Work outside Basic Services is an Additional Service under Article 4. The architect notifies the owner and does not proceed without the owner's written authorization; the compensation then follows Article 11.</p><ul><li>A → Billing later for work the owner never authorized is the most common fee dispute.</li><li>C → Agreements can be amended; scope changes are normal and are handled as Additional Services.</li><li>D → Giving away services undermines the firm's fee and sets an expectation for the rest of the project.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA B101–2017, Article 4 (Supplemental and Additional Services) and Article 11 (Compensation)</p>"},
{id:"q222",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 3.1",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">B101–2017 articles</p><p>Match each situation to the article of AIA B101–2017 that governs it.</p>",items:["The owner wants to use the drawings to build a second, identical branch elsewhere","The owner stops paying invoices","The project budget is exceeded by the lowest bid","The owner and architect disagree about fees and want a neutral to help them settle"],opts:["Article 7 — Copyrights and Licenses","Article 9 — Termination or Suspension","Article 6 — Cost of the Work","Article 8 — Claims and Disputes"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p>Knowing the article order is a shortcut to the answer:</p><ul><li>Article 7: the architect owns the Instruments of Service; the owner's licence covers this project only, so reuse on another site needs the architect's permission.</li><li>Article 9: non-payment lets the architect suspend services after 7 days' written notice.</li><li>Article 6: if bids exceed the owner's budget, the owner chooses among approving an increase, rebidding, terminating, revising the scope, or another mutually acceptable option.</li><li>Article 8: mediation comes first, before binding dispute resolution.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA B101–2017, Articles 6–9</p>"},
{id:"q223",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 3.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Flow-down to consultants</p><p>An architect signs B101–2017 with an owner and then hires a structural engineer on AIA C401–2017. The owner later asks for a change in the structural system, and the architect agrees to it as an Additional Service.</p><p>How should the engineer's part of the change be handled?</p>",opts:["The engineer must do the extra work at no charge because the architect accepted it","Through the architect, as a change to the engineer's services under C401, with compensation agreed between architect and engineer","Directly between the owner and the engineer, outside the architect's agreement","By the contractor, who coordinates the engineer during construction"],c:[1],e:"<p>C401 is written to flow down from the prime agreement: the consultant has the same responsibilities to the architect that the architect has to the owner for that part of the work. The owner has no contract with the engineer, so changes in the engineer's services are made through the architect.</p><ul><li>A → An Additional Service to the architect is also additional to the consultant; the consultant is paid for it.</li><li>C → There is no owner–engineer contract; going around the architect breaks the chain of responsibility.</li><li>D → The contractor does not manage the design team.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA C401–2017 (Architect–Consultant agreement); AIA B101–2017, Article 4</p>"},
{id:"q224",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 3.2",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">What the architect–consultant agreement should match</p><p>Which THREE terms in an architect–consultant agreement should be made consistent with the owner–architect agreement?</p>",opts:["The standard of care","Insurance coverage the consultant must carry","How disputes are resolved, so the consultant can be joined in the same proceeding","The consultant's hourly billing rates","The consultant's office location","The consultant's choice of drawing software, regardless of the project's requirements"],c:[0,1,2],e:"<p>The consultant agreement should carry the prime agreement's obligations down: the same standard of care, insurance that supports the architect's own obligations, and a dispute process that lets the consultant be brought into the same proceeding.</p><ul><li>D → Rates are a business term between architect and consultant; they need not match the owner's rates.</li><li>E → Irrelevant to the contract chain.</li><li>F → If the prime agreement sets BIM or file requirements, those flow down; but this option ignores them, so it is the opposite of consistency.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA C401–2017; AIA B101–2017</p>"},
{id:"q225",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 3.3",t:"num",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">How long to certify a payment</p><p>Under AIA A201–2017, the contractor submits an Application for Payment on Monday, March 3.</p><p>Within how many days after receiving it must the architect issue a Certificate for Payment (or notify the owner and contractor of the reasons for withholding)? Enter the number of days.</p>",ans:7,tol:0,unit:"days",e:"<p>A201–2017 §9.4.1 gives the architect seven days after receipt of the Application for Payment to certify the amount due, or to notify the owner and contractor in writing of the reasons for withholding all or part of it.</p><p>The certificate is the architect's representation, based on site visits and the data in the application, that the work has progressed to the point indicated and is in accordance with the contract documents to the best of the architect's knowledge. It is not a guarantee of the contractor's work.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA A201–2017, §9.4.1 and §9.4.2</p>"},
{id:"q226",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 3.3",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Concealed conditions</p><p>During demolition in a 1920s school, the contractor exposes an abandoned underground fuel tank not shown on any survey.</p><p>Under AIA A201–2017, what must the contractor do?</p>",opts:["Remove the tank and submit the cost with the next payment application","Notify the owner and architect promptly, before the conditions are disturbed and within 14 days of first observing them","Notify the architect within 21 days after the work is complete","Stop all work on the site until the owner hires a new contractor"],c:[1],e:"<p>A201–2017 §3.7.4: for concealed or unknown physical conditions, the party who finds them notifies the other promptly, before the conditions are disturbed, and in no event later than 14 days after first observance. The architect then investigates and, if the conditions differ materially, recommends an adjustment to the contract sum or time.</p><ul><li>A → Disturbing the conditions first destroys the evidence and the right to an adjustment.</li><li>C → 21 days is the general time limit for initiating Claims, and the 2007 edition's period for concealed conditions; A201–2017 shortened it to 14.</li><li>D → The work continues where it can; the contract has a process for this.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA A201–2017, §3.7.4 (concealed or unknown conditions)</p>"},
{id:"q227",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 3.4",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Relying on the owner's surveys</p><p>The owner provides a geotechnical report from its own consultant. The architect and structural engineer design the foundations based on its bearing capacity. During excavation, the soil turns out to be much weaker than the report stated.</p><p>Under AIA B101–2017, who is responsible for the accuracy of the geotechnical report?</p>",opts:["The architect, who should have verified the report","The structural engineer, who used the data","The owner, who furnished it; the architect is entitled to rely on information the owner provides","The contractor, who excavated the site"],c:[2],e:"<p>B101–2017 makes the owner responsible for furnishing site surveys and geotechnical engineering, and entitles the architect to rely on the accuracy and completeness of services and information the owner furnishes. The architect still coordinates with the owner's consultants and should flag anything obviously wrong.</p><ul><li>A, B → Neither is required to re-verify the owner's consultant's findings.</li><li>D → The contractor did not prepare the report.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA B101–2017, Article 5 (Owner's Responsibilities)</p>"},
{id:"q228",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 3.4",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Integrating the owner's consultants</p><p>For a corporate headquarters, the owner hires its own AV consultant and kitchen designer directly.</p><p>Which THREE are the architect's responsibilities toward these owner's consultants?</p>",opts:["Coordinate the architect's services with theirs, such as space, power and structural needs","Ask the owner to have them provide their documents in the formats and at the milestones the project needs","Tell the owner promptly if their information conflicts with the architect's design","Check their technical calculations for accuracy","Pay their invoices and add a markup","Take responsibility for errors in their designs"],c:[0,1,2],e:"<p>Owner's consultants are under contract to the owner. The architect coordinates with them, so their work fits the building, and raises conflicts promptly, but does not take responsibility for their designs.</p><ul><li>D → The architect may rely on owner-furnished information; checking another professional's calculations is not part of Basic Services.</li><li>E → The owner pays its own consultants.</li><li>F → Responsibility stays with the consultant and the owner who hired them.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA B101–2017, Articles 3 and 5</p>"},
{id:"q229",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 4.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">When the estimate exceeds the budget</p><p>At the end of Design Development, the cost estimate for a community center is 9% over the owner's budget for the Cost of the Work.</p><p>What should the architect do first?</p>",opts:["Proceed to Construction Documents and hope bids come in lower","Explain the estimate to the owner and present options, such as scope reductions, alternates or revisions to quality, for the owner to decide","Cut the HVAC system to the minimum allowed by code without telling the owner","Increase the architect's fee because the budget changed"],c:[1],e:"<p>Monitoring the construction budget means acting at each estimate, while changes are cheapest. The architect recommends; the owner decides how to bring cost and scope back into line, and the decision is documented.</p><ul><li>A → Ignoring the estimate defers the problem to bidding, when fixes cost the most and delay the project.</li><li>C → Changes to the owner's program or quality are the owner's decisions.</li><li>D → A budget problem is not a fee event; the fee changes only if services change.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA B101–2017, Article 6 (Cost of the Work)</p>"},
{id:"q230",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 4.1",t:"num",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Escalating an estimate</p><p>A cost estimate of $12,400,000 was prepared in January for a project whose construction midpoint is 18 months later. The estimator recommends escalation of 4% per year, applied simply (not compounded).</p><p>What is the escalated estimate at the construction midpoint? Enter a whole number in dollars.</p>",ans:13144000,tol:0,unit:"dollars",e:"<p>Escalate to the midpoint of construction, when the average dollar is spent:</p><ul><li>18 months = 1.5 years</li><li>Escalation = 4% × 1.5 = 6%</li><li>$12,400,000 × 1.06 = $13,144,000</li></ul><p>Using the bid date instead of the construction midpoint understates the cost; using a full two years overstates it.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed. (construction cost management)</p>"},
{id:"q231",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 4.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Scope creep</p><p>In Construction Documents, the owner's representative keeps asking for \"small\" additions in meetings: an extra conference room, a revised entry canopy, a new storage area. None is large on its own, but together they have added three weeks of work.</p><p>What is the best way for the project manager to handle this?</p>",opts:["Absorb them, since each one is small","Log each request, tell the owner in writing how it affects fee, schedule and construction cost, and get a decision before proceeding","Refuse all further changes until construction","Make the changes and present the fee increase at project close-out"],c:[1],e:"<p>Scope creep is many small changes nobody tracks. The fix is a change log and prompt written notice of each request's impact, with the owner's authorization before the work proceeds. The owner then decides with full information.</p><ul><li>A → Absorbing changes one at a time is how projects overrun.</li><li>C → Owners are entitled to change their minds; the issue is documenting and paying for it.</li><li>D → A surprise invoice at the end, for work never authorized, is a fee dispute waiting to happen.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA B101–2017, Article 4; PjM notes, Project Execution</p>"},
{id:"q232",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 4.2",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Which change document</p><p>Match each construction-phase situation to the document that handles it under the AIA A201–2017 family.</p>",items:["Owner, contractor and architect agree on a price and time for an added skylight","The owner needs extra blocking installed now, but the price is not yet agreed","The architect moves a light switch 12 inches with no change in cost or time","The contractor asks how to interpret a conflicting detail"],opts:["Change Order","Construction Change Directive","Architect's Supplemental Instructions","Request for Information"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<ul><li><strong>Change Order</strong> (G701): signed by owner, contractor and architect; agreed change in the work, contract sum and time.</li><li><strong>Construction Change Directive</strong> (G714): directs a change before price and time are agreed; signed by owner and architect.</li><li><strong>Architect's Supplemental Instructions</strong> (G710): a minor change with no change in cost or time, consistent with the contract documents.</li><li><strong>RFI</strong>: a request for clarification; the architect's answer interprets the documents but is not itself a change.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA A201–2017, Article 7; AIA G701, G714, G710</p>"},
{id:"q233",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 4.3",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Documents for a fast-track project</p><p>An owner wants a medical office building open in 14 months and hires a construction manager as constructor (CMc) who will release early bid packages.</p><p>How should the architect plan the construction documents?</p>",opts:["As one complete set issued for bidding after the design is finished","As a sequence of bid packages, such as site work and foundations first, then the structural frame, then the enclosure, coordinated so each is complete for its own scope","As performance specifications only, with no drawings","As schematic drawings that the CM completes"],c:[1],e:"<p>Fast-track delivery overlaps design and construction, so the documents are issued in packages, in the order the work is built. Each package must be complete for its own scope and coordinated with decisions that are not yet final, which raises coordination risk; the work plan should budget for it.</p><ul><li>A → That is the traditional design-bid-build sequence; it cannot meet an overlapped schedule.</li><li>C, D → The architect still produces construction documents; the CM provides cost and constructability input, not the design.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 9 (project delivery)</p>"},
{id:"q234",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 4.3",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Bridging documents in design-build</p><p>A state agency will procure a maintenance facility by design-build and hires an architect to prepare bridging documents.</p><p>Which THREE belong in the bridging documents?</p>",opts:["The program and functional requirements","Performance criteria for systems, such as HVAC capacity and envelope performance","Schematic or preliminary design drawings showing the intended layout","Shop drawings for the structural steel","The design-builder's means and methods","Final construction documents ready for permit"],c:[0,1,2],e:"<p>Bridging documents define what the owner wants (program, performance criteria and a preliminary design) so design-build proposals can be compared. The design-builder's own architect then completes the design.</p><ul><li>D → Shop drawings come from fabricators during construction.</li><li>E → Means and methods belong to the builder.</li><li>F → Taking the design to final construction documents would defeat the purpose of design-build.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 9</p>"},
{id:"q235",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 4.4",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Plan review comments</p><p>The building department's plan review of a restaurant returns 14 comments, including one requiring an additional exit that the architect believes the code does not require.</p><p>What is the architect's best course of action?</p>",opts:["Ignore the comment, since the code is on the architect's side","Respond in writing to every comment, citing the code sections for the disputed one and asking for a meeting or a formal interpretation, while keeping the owner informed","Add the exit without telling the owner, to avoid delay","Tell the contractor to build it either way and sort it out at inspection"],c:[1],e:"<p>The authority having jurisdiction (AHJ) decides what is approved. The architect responds to every comment, supports a disagreement with code citations, and uses the AHJ's process (a meeting, a formal interpretation, or an appeal) while the owner decides on time and cost.</p><ul><li>A → An unanswered comment means no permit.</li><li>C → Adding a required exit changes cost and layout; it is the owner's decision.</li><li>D → Building without approval risks failed inspections and rework.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: PjM notes, Project Execution (authorities having jurisdiction)</p>"},
{id:"q236",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 4.4",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Which authority</p><p>Match each approval to the body that typically grants it.</p>",items:["A variance from a required side-yard setback","The building permit","A certificate of appropriateness for a building in a historic district","Approval of the fire alarm and sprinkler shop drawings"],opts:["Zoning board of appeals","Building department","Historic preservation commission","Fire marshal"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p>Each authority having jurisdiction has its own scope. Part of planning the project is identifying them all early and adding their reviews to the schedule:</p><ul><li>Zoning relief such as variances goes to the zoning board of appeals (or board of adjustment).</li><li>The building department issues the building permit after code review.</li><li>Work in a designated historic district needs the preservation commission's approval.</li><li>The fire marshal reviews fire protection and alarm systems.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed. (regulatory approvals)</p>"},
{id:"q237",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 5.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Sealing documents</p><p>A firm's principal is licensed in the state where a project is located. A staff member, licensed in another state only, prepared the drawings under the principal's direct supervision.</p><p>Who may seal the documents?</p>",opts:["The staff member, because they prepared the drawings","The principal, who is licensed in the project state and had responsible control of the work","Either one, as long as the firm is registered","Nobody; out-of-state work cannot be sealed"],c:[1],e:"<p>State licensing laws allow an architect licensed in that state to seal documents prepared under their responsible control (direct supervision). The seal is a professional representation and must be applied by an architect licensed in the jurisdiction.</p><ul><li>A → A license in another state does not authorize practice in this one.</li><li>C → Firm registration does not replace an individual license to seal.</li><li>D → The principal is licensed in the project state, so the work can be sealed.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: NCARB Model Law; state architectural licensing statutes</p>"},
{id:"q238",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 5.1",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Laws that reach the design</p><p>An architect is designing a 60-unit multifamily building with ground-floor retail and federal financing.</p><p>Which THREE apply to the design, beyond the local building code?</p>",opts:["The Fair Housing Act accessibility requirements for the dwelling units","The Americans with Disabilities Act for the retail spaces","The local zoning ordinance","The owner's preferred interior color palette","The contractor's internal safety manual","The architect's firm handbook"],c:[0,1,2],e:"<p>Several laws apply at once: the Fair Housing Act to covered multifamily dwellings, the ADA to places of public accommodation such as the retail, and zoning to use, bulk and parking. The architect's standard of care includes knowing which laws apply.</p><ul><li>D → A client preference, not a law.</li><li>E, F → Internal documents, not laws governing the design.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Fair Housing Act Design Manual; 2010 ADA Standards for Accessible Design</p>"},
{id:"q239",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 5.2",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Building QC into the process</p><p>A firm finds that its drawing checks happen only at the end of Construction Documents, and errors found then are expensive to fix.</p><p>Which change best reduces risk?</p>",opts:["Hire more checkers for the final review","Schedule quality reviews at each phase milestone, with checklists and reviewers who are not on the project team","Rely on the contractor's RFIs to find errors during construction","Shorten the Construction Documents phase to leave more time for the check"],c:[1],e:"<p>Quality management works best as a process throughout the project, not one inspection at the end. Reviews at each milestone catch problems while changes are cheap, and independent reviewers see what the team has stopped noticing.</p><ul><li>A → More checking at the end still finds errors at their most expensive.</li><li>C → RFIs during construction are the costliest place to find errors, and they bring claims.</li><li>D → Squeezing production to make room for checking creates more errors.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 12 (quality management)</p>"},
{id:"q240",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 5.2",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Reducing professional liability exposure</p><p>Which THREE practices reduce a firm's professional liability exposure on a project?</p>",opts:["Documenting the owner's decisions and directions in writing","Avoiding guarantees and warranties that go beyond the standard of care","Reviewing the agreement for clauses that are uninsurable","Promising the owner the project will be finished on a fixed date regardless of contractor delays","Taking on services outside the firm's expertise to keep the client","Agreeing to indemnify the owner for the contractor's errors"],c:[0,1,2],e:"<p>Liability exposure is reduced by contracts the firm can insure, by not promising more than the standard of care, and by a written record of decisions.</p><ul><li>D → A guarantee of results outside the architect's control is a warranty that professional liability insurance usually excludes.</li><li>E → Working outside your expertise invites claims.</li><li>F → Taking on the contractor's responsibilities shifts risk the firm cannot control or insure.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 5 (risk management)</p>"},
{id:"q241",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 5.3",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Drawings and specifications that disagree</p><p>The drawings show a 5/8-inch Type X gypsum board ceiling, but the specification section lists 1/2-inch regular gypsum board for the same room. The contractor asks which governs.</p><p>Under AIA A201–2017, what is the right approach?</p>",opts:["The specifications always govern over the drawings","The drawings always govern over the specifications","The contract documents are complementary; the architect interprets them, and the conflict is resolved and corrected","The contractor chooses the cheaper option"],c:[2],e:"<p>A201–2017 §1.2.1 treats the contract documents as complementary: what one requires is as binding as if all required it. A201 does not set an order of precedence between drawings and specifications. The architect interprets them, here probably in favor of the rated Type X, which a fire-resistance design usually needs, and the conflict is corrected through the proper document. The real QC lesson is coordinating drawings and specs before issue.</p><ul><li>A, B → An order of precedence can be added in the supplementary conditions, but it is not in A201 itself.</li><li>D → The contractor does not decide; and the cheaper option may break a required fire rating.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA A201–2017, §1.2.1 and §4.2.11</p>"},
{id:"q242",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 5.3",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Coordination before issue</p><p>Before issuing a set of construction documents for a laboratory, which THREE checks most improve coordination and constructability?</p>",opts:["Overlay the structural, mechanical and plumbing drawings, or run clash detection in the model, to find conflicts in the ceiling","Check that every specification section referenced on the drawings exists in the project manual","Confirm that ceiling heights work with the deepest duct and beam crossings","Spell-check the cover sheet only","Remove the general notes to make the sheets cleaner","Wait for the contractor's shop drawings to coordinate the trades"],c:[0,1,2],e:"<p>Coordination checks look across disciplines and documents: physical conflicts between systems, drawing references that point to real specification sections, and dimensional checks where systems are tightest.</p><ul><li>D → Cosmetic, not coordination.</li><li>E → General notes carry requirements; removing them creates gaps.</li><li>F → Shop drawings show how the contractor intends to build; they do not replace coordinating the design documents.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 12</p>"},
{id:"q243",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 5.4",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Reviewing a substitution</p><p>After award, the contractor proposes substituting a less expensive curtain wall system for the one specified, citing a long lead time.</p><p>What should the architect review?</p>",opts:["Only the price difference","Whether the proposed system meets the performance, appearance and warranty requirements of the specified system and the design intent, with the owner deciding on cost and time","Whether the curtain wall manufacturer is local","Nothing; substitutions are the contractor's responsibility"],c:[1],e:"<p>Protecting the integrity of the design means reviewing substitutions against the specified performance (structural, thermal, water and air), appearance, warranty, and the effect on other work. The architect recommends; accepted substitutions are documented by a change in the contract documents.</p><ul><li>A → Price alone ignores performance and design intent.</li><li>C → Location is irrelevant unless the specification requires it.</li><li>D → The contractor proposes, but the architect evaluates and the owner approves.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: Specification Division 01, Substitution Procedures (MasterFormat 01 25 00); AIA A201–2017, §3.4.2</p>"},
{id:"q244",d:"PjM",o:"PjM 5.4",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Shop drawing review</p><p>Match each item in a shop drawing submittal to how the architect treats it under AIA A201–2017.</p>",items:["Whether the storefront profile and finish match the design","The fabricator's welding procedure","Whether the dimensions are correct for field conditions","Conformance with the design concept expressed in the contract documents"],opts:["Reviewed by the architect","Contractor's responsibility, not reviewed for accuracy"],c:[0,1,1,0],e:"<p>The architect reviews submittals for the limited purpose of checking conformance with information given and the design concept expressed in the contract documents (A201–2017 §4.2.7). Dimensions, quantities, fabrication processes, means and methods, and coordination of the trades remain the contractor's responsibility.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA A201–2017, §3.12 and §4.2.7</p>"},
{id:"q245",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 1.3",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">A gift from a supplier</p><p>A flooring manufacturer whose product an architect is evaluating for a large school district project offers the architect a paid trip to its factory and a resort stay.</p><p>What is the ethically appropriate response?</p>",opts:["Accept, since every firm does it","Decline the resort stay; if a factory visit is genuinely needed for the evaluation, disclose it to the client and avoid anything that could influence the specification","Accept and specify the product to return the favor","Accept, but keep it quiet"],c:[1],e:"<p>The AIA Code of Ethics asks architects to avoid conflicts of interest and not to accept gifts or favors that could reasonably be seen as influencing their judgment on behalf of the client. A legitimate technical visit can be acceptable if it is disclosed; a resort stay is not.</p><ul><li>A → Common practice is not a defense.</li><li>C → Specifying to return a favor is a direct conflict of interest.</li><li>D → Concealment makes it worse.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct (obligations to clients); The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 1</p>"},
{id:"q246",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 2.3",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Growing into a new market</p><p>A 20-person firm that designs K–12 schools wants to enter higher-education work, where it has no completed projects.</p><p>Which strategy is most likely to succeed?</p>",opts:["Bid low on a large university project to buy the work","Team with an experienced higher-education firm on a first project, while building relevant staff and marketing material","Rename the firm to sound more academic","Claim the school projects as higher-education experience in proposals"],c:[1],e:"<p>Entering a new market works best by building real qualifications: teaming or a joint venture on a first project, hiring people with the experience, and marketing related strengths honestly.</p><ul><li>A → Buying work at a loss risks the firm's finances and does not build credibility.</li><li>C → Branding without experience does not win selection.</li><li>D → Misrepresenting experience is an ethics violation.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 6 (business development and strategy)</p>"},
{id:"q247",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 3.1",t:"mc",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">A request outside the firm's expertise</p><p>A long-time client asks a firm known for small commercial buildings to design a 200-bed hospital. The firm has no healthcare experience.</p><p>What is the most appropriate response?</p>",opts:["Accept, and learn on the job","Decline, or propose associating with a firm experienced in healthcare, and explain why to the client","Accept, but leave out the complex medical spaces","Refer the client to another firm and charge a finder's fee without telling the client"],c:[1],e:"<p>An architect should take on only work it can perform within the standard of care. Associating with an experienced firm, or declining, protects the client and the firm.</p><ul><li>A → Learning on a hospital puts the client at risk and invites claims.</li><li>C → Leaving out the hard parts of the scope does not serve the client.</li><li>D → An undisclosed referral fee is a conflict of interest.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: AIA Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct; The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed.</p>"},
{id:"q248",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 3.1",t:"cata",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Before accepting a new client</p><p>A developer the firm has not worked with asks for a proposal for a speculative office building.</p><p>Which THREE should the firm evaluate before accepting?</p>",opts:["The client's ability to pay, such as financing in place and payment history with other firms","Whether the project's scope and schedule fit the firm's capacity","Whether the client's expectations about budget and quality are realistic","The developer's preferred architectural style","The color of the client's logo","Whether the client will let the firm skip a written agreement"],c:[0,1,2],e:"<p>Evaluating a prospective client is part of risk management: ability to pay, whether the project fits the firm's capacity and expertise, and whether expectations are realistic.</p><ul><li>D → Style is a design conversation, not a go or no-go risk.</li><li>E → Irrelevant.</li><li>F → A client who wants to avoid a written agreement is a warning sign, not a reason to accept.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 5 and Ch. 6</p>"},
{id:"q249",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 3.3",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Risks and how to mitigate them</p><p>Match each project risk to the most fitting mitigation.</p>",items:["The owner's budget is far below the program's likely cost","The site has an unknown subsurface history","The owner wants an unproven building system","The schedule overlaps design and construction"],opts:["Reconcile program and budget early with an independent estimate","Ask the owner for a geotechnical investigation and environmental assessment before design","Ask for mock-ups and performance testing, and document the owner's informed decision","Plan coordinated bid packages and budget extra coordination time"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p>Project risks are identified early and assigned a response:</p><ul><li>A budget gap is a programming problem and is fixed before design.</li><li>An unknown site is an owner-furnished-information problem; the owner provides investigations.</li><li>An unproven system needs testing and the owner's informed decision on record.</li><li>A fast-track schedule needs a documented package strategy and coordination time in the work plan.</li></ul><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 5 (risk management)</p>"},
{id:"q250",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 4.1",t:"match",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Business structures</p><p>Match each feature to the business structure it describes.</p>",items:["Owners are personally liable for all business debts, and income passes through to their personal taxes","Income is taxed at the company level and again when distributed to shareholders as dividends","Owners have limited personal liability, and by default profits pass through to the owners' personal taxes","A corporation that elects to pass income through to its shareholders, with a limit on the number of shareholders"],opts:["Sole proprietorship","C corporation","Limited liability company (LLC)","S corporation"],c:[0,1,2,3],e:"<p>The structures differ on two axes, liability and taxation:</p><ul><li><strong>Sole proprietorship</strong>: no liability shield; pass-through income.</li><li><strong>C corporation</strong>: taxed separately (double taxation of distributed profits); liability limited to the investment.</li><li><strong>LLC</strong>: limited liability with pass-through taxation by default.</li><li><strong>S corporation</strong>: corporate liability protection with pass-through taxation; limited number of shareholders.</li></ul><p>Professional liability for one's own negligent work is not shielded by any structure.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 5, Table 5.1</p>"},
{id:"q251",d:"PcM",o:"PcM 4.1",t:"num",s:"<p class=\"qtitle\">Utilization rate</p><p>In a four-week period, a project architect worked 168 hours, of which 134 were charged to projects. The rest went to marketing, training and office administration.</p><p>What is the architect's utilization rate, as a percentage rounded to the nearest whole number?</p>",ans:80,tol:0.5,unit:"%",e:"<p>Utilization rate = direct (chargeable) hours ÷ total hours worked</p><p>= 134 ÷ 168 = 0.798 → <strong>80%</strong></p><p>Utilization measures how much of the staff's time produces fee. Firms set targets by role: production staff high, principals lower because of marketing and management.</p><p class=\"qsrc\">Source: The Architect's Handbook of Professional Practice, 15th ed., Ch. 7 (financial management)</p>"}
];
